start

00:57:55.066 --> 00:57:56.233
briefly by mentioning that

00:57:57.066 --> 00:58:00.433
as the Comprehensive Plan Committee has wrapped up its work, they voted to send you

00:58:00.467 --> 00:58:03.367
now a final draft of their updated comprehensive plan,

00:58:04.600 --> 00:58:08.100
and now under law, it's up to the elected officials of the community

00:58:08.967 --> 00:58:12.400
to pass the plan in order for it to become

00:58:12.467 --> 00:58:16.333
effective. Milan and Howard will

00:58:16.333 --> 00:58:16.900
talk

00:58:18.767 --> 00:58:20.767
extensively about what's in the plan.

00:58:21.100 --> 00:58:24.467
Even though I know a lot of folks have already read it and provided

00:58:25.600 --> 00:58:27.266
informed comments on the plan.

00:58:28.533 --> 00:58:32.300
Before I turn it over to

00:58:32.300 --> 00:58:36.000
Howard to do some introductory remarks of his own, I just want to thank

00:58:36.834 --> 00:58:39.900
several entities, including the committee itself,

00:58:41.000 --> 00:58:44.967
the staff that staffs the committee and has also provided input,

00:58:45.433 --> 00:58:49.200
and then the public for showing up at all of these meetings and

00:58:49.233 --> 00:58:53.066
providing extensive input in person and via email, and

00:58:53.967 --> 00:58:57.767
things of that nature. I've been working in various capacities in municipal

00:58:57.867 --> 00:59:01.800
government for over 20 years now. Comp plans don't come up that

00:59:01.900 --> 00:59:05.300
often, so I've only lived through really two different processes, this one and

00:59:05.367 --> 00:59:05.767
another.

00:59:06.667 --> 00:59:10.333
But in talking to other colleagues, I don't know any process that's gone on

00:59:10.467 --> 00:59:14.367
for over really a year, and this one has gone on, we're entering

00:59:14.400 --> 00:59:18.100
year four, and so that's just a lot of time and energy for the

00:59:18.200 --> 00:59:21.533
staff, for the committee members, for members of the public to stay engaged,

00:59:22.033 --> 00:59:25.300
and I think that deserves some

00:59:25.333 --> 00:59:26.066
kudos,

00:59:27.000 --> 00:59:30.800
especially on the committee's end for all the time that they've spent on getting

00:59:30.867 --> 00:59:34.700
this document to where it is. So thank you to the committee, thank you

00:59:34.800 --> 00:59:37.400
staff and members of the public for showing up.

00:59:37.500 --> 00:59:40.867
Now I'm going to turn it over to the comp plan chair, Howard Kunin, to provide

00:59:40.900 --> 00:59:44.266
some remarks.

00:59:48.700 --> 00:59:49.300
Thank you, Scott.

00:59:51.367 --> 00:59:53.900
So I'm Howard Kunin. I live on Walnut Street.

00:59:53.967 --> 00:59:57.800
I'm the District 2 representative and the current chair of the comp

00:59:57.867 --> 00:59:59.100
plan.

01:00:00.066 --> 01:00:03.767
I want to begin by acknowledging and thanking everyone who served on the committee,

01:00:04.667 --> 01:00:04.734
and

01:00:05.600 --> 01:00:07.033
of course, the current members.

01:00:08.066 --> 01:00:09.400
That includes Kathleen Egan,

01:00:10.300 --> 01:00:11.100
Bob Foster,

01:00:12.000 --> 01:00:12.667
Mike Hulsey,

01:00:13.533 --> 01:00:14.300
Anne Jordan,

01:00:15.166 --> 01:00:18.967
Jennifer Kinney, Jeff McDonald, Christopher Paine,

01:00:19.800 --> 01:00:20.400
Marty Reel,

01:00:21.233 --> 01:00:22.066
Peter Stanton,

01:00:22.867 --> 01:00:23.834
Pedro Vazquez,

01:00:24.700 --> 01:00:26.667
Mayor Tipton, and Councilor West.

01:00:28.467 --> 01:00:29.567
I want to thank them for all their-

01:00:29.900 --> 01:00:30.600
Get the mic.

01:00:30.600 --> 01:00:31.000
Excuse me?

01:00:31.567 --> 01:00:32.467
Get closer to the mic.

01:00:32.500 --> 01:00:32.900
Okay.

01:00:34.767 --> 01:00:35.166
How's that?

01:00:35.700 --> 01:00:36.100
Yeah.

01:00:36.567 --> 01:00:39.567
All right. So I want to thank them all for their time, their hard work, and their

01:00:39.600 --> 01:00:43.300
dedication to the plan. Several of these members have been on the committee since

01:00:43.300 --> 01:00:45.367
it was established more than three years ago.

01:00:46.467 --> 01:00:50.166
I also want to particularly acknowledge Peter and Kathleen for their

01:00:50.166 --> 01:00:52.834
dedicated service as previous chairs of the committee.

01:00:54.000 --> 01:00:57.600
And on behalf of the committee, we want to thank Milan and Eli Rubin for

01:00:57.667 --> 01:01:01.300
supporting this process with their expertise, their skill, and

01:01:01.333 --> 01:01:02.600
especially their patience.

01:01:03.834 --> 01:01:06.967
The committee would also like to acknowledge and thank the many members of the

01:01:07.000 --> 01:01:10.834
public who participated in outreach efforts, attended committee

01:01:10.900 --> 01:01:11.333
meetings,

01:01:12.166 --> 01:01:15.900
contributed oral and written comments, and provided online

01:01:15.934 --> 01:01:18.367
input to the completed plan draft.

01:01:19.767 --> 01:01:23.133
The majority of the policy guidance in the plan was reached either through

01:01:23.166 --> 01:01:25.567
consensus or by solid majority vote,

01:01:26.667 --> 01:01:30.500
and I think it's fair to say that the majority of this policy guidance

01:01:30.600 --> 01:01:32.767
also seems to have good community consensus.

01:01:33.867 --> 01:01:37.500
The area that we struggled with, as you all know, is the

01:01:37.533 --> 01:01:40.600
eastern waterfront. So I just want to speak briefly to

01:01:40.667 --> 01:01:44.433
that.The policies that we are recommending for CALUPA

01:01:44.600 --> 01:01:48.266
21, the Eastern Waterfront coastal area, and

01:01:48.333 --> 01:01:51.500
CALUPA 22, the shipyard and terminal area,

01:01:52.266 --> 01:01:55.500
are attempting to balance the city's goals of climate resilience,

01:01:56.433 --> 01:01:57.634
protecting public health,

01:01:58.500 --> 01:01:59.867
promoting balanced growth,

01:02:00.667 --> 01:02:01.700
and increasing housing.

01:02:03.667 --> 01:02:07.567
We were also mindful that the property covered by these policies is

01:02:07.600 --> 01:02:08.400
privately owned.

01:02:09.767 --> 01:02:13.000
We heard the public's valid concerns about sea level rise,

01:02:13.967 --> 01:02:15.000
oil tank emissions,

01:02:16.133 --> 01:02:18.133
level of development, and traffic.

01:02:19.867 --> 01:02:23.433
We share those concerns, and we try to address them through policies that

01:02:23.467 --> 01:02:27.400
include, for example, accounting for 8.8 feet

01:02:27.433 --> 01:02:30.567
of sea level rise for public and private critical infrastructure,

01:02:31.500 --> 01:02:35.200
which can be found in core theme 1A.2 on page 15 of the plan.

01:02:36.667 --> 01:02:40.600
Incorporating the coastal resilience overlay zone, which is referenced in

01:02:40.634 --> 01:02:41.500
multiple places.

01:02:42.700 --> 01:02:46.166
Conditioning new residential development on evidence-based studies

01:02:46.600 --> 01:02:50.533
by a credible scientific source that considers both current and

01:02:50.600 --> 01:02:53.333
potential VOC emissions impact on health,

01:02:54.200 --> 01:02:58.066
and considering the adoption of residential land use separation standards.

01:02:59.066 --> 01:03:01.600
Both of these are in core theme 5C.14

01:03:02.567 --> 01:03:06.467
on page 37, and in CALUPA 22 strategy B

01:03:06.533 --> 01:03:07.467
on page 108.

01:03:09.166 --> 01:03:13.033
And also then adopting development standards that will minimize increase

01:03:13.066 --> 01:03:15.266
in congestion along Broadway,

01:03:16.100 --> 01:03:19.800
which is strategy J9 on page 109 of CALUPA 22.

01:03:22.000 --> 01:03:25.867
There was intense and thoughtful debate among committee members while we worked

01:03:25.900 --> 01:03:26.867
on these policies,

01:03:27.767 --> 01:03:31.467
and we certainly recognize and respect that there are members of the public who

01:03:31.467 --> 01:03:35.166
don't agree with the approach that we are proposing for the Eastern Waterfront.

01:03:36.467 --> 01:03:39.567
But at the end of the process, the plan that we are presenting to you for your

01:03:39.600 --> 01:03:43.200
consideration reflects our best effort at contributing to the

01:03:43.233 --> 01:03:46.800
positive future of South Portland in the Eastern

01:03:46.867 --> 01:03:48.533
Waterfront and indeed across the city.

01:03:49.867 --> 01:03:52.700
I'll be available to answer any questions as you have your deliberations.

01:03:52.767 --> 01:03:53.567
So, thank you.

01:03:55.000 --> 01:03:58.867
Thank you very much, Howard, and thank you for serving as the

01:03:58.900 --> 01:04:00.567
committee chair.

01:04:08.500 --> 01:04:11.567
I'm not sure who has the control for the projection in the back.

01:04:14.734 --> 01:04:16.900
Once again, Milan Nevida, the planning director.

01:04:18.500 --> 01:04:20.000
Just as that's coming up, I

01:04:20.867 --> 01:04:23.066
had several words, but most of them have already been said.

01:04:23.667 --> 01:04:27.233
So I just want to echo my thanks to Howard and the prior chairs, Kathy and

01:04:27.500 --> 01:04:29.567
Peter, and all the members of the committee.

01:04:30.600 --> 01:04:34.567
I also want to specifically call out Eli Rubin, our community planner, who

01:04:34.600 --> 01:04:38.467
really led the planning effort for the last year and a half,

01:04:38.900 --> 01:04:42.867
making sure that we're getting through the large volume of both research

01:04:42.900 --> 01:04:46.700
that's required to present the packets, and then also the content that goes into

01:04:46.767 --> 01:04:47.967
each one of the CPC meetings.

01:04:48.967 --> 01:04:51.967
Specifically on that, and this is some of the stuff that we don't necessarily get

01:04:52.000 --> 01:04:52.634
to showcase,

01:04:53.433 --> 01:04:54.900
I do want to call out Eli for

01:04:56.934 --> 01:04:59.867
some of the hard work that he put in on the engagement side of things.

01:04:59.900 --> 01:05:03.900
As Scott was mentioning, this has been a long four-year process, and one of the

01:05:03.900 --> 01:05:07.867
key reasons why it's taken that amount of time is because we wanted to design a

01:05:07.900 --> 01:05:11.700
process that allowed people to communicate with us and weigh in,

01:05:12.500 --> 01:05:16.400
and weigh in in multiple ways, and also to do it within a span of time that

01:05:16.400 --> 01:05:20.400
seemed reasonable. There were four or five times through this process that

01:05:20.433 --> 01:05:23.967
we pushed out the comment period by two, three months or four months or five

01:05:24.000 --> 01:05:27.033
months, to just give that space because this is such

01:05:27.066 --> 01:05:31.000
an important milestone for the community and sets us up for a very long

01:05:31.033 --> 01:05:34.667
time. One of the things that Eli, I think, really

01:05:35.300 --> 01:05:39.166
emphasized and put energy towards, and I think helped in this plan, is

01:05:39.200 --> 01:05:42.800
reaching out to specific target groups and trying to understand what their

01:05:42.834 --> 01:05:45.667
perspectives were, folks that don't typically come out.

01:05:45.967 --> 01:05:47.467
So coming and having meetings with

01:05:48.433 --> 01:05:52.133
South Portland high school kids, organizations that work throughout the region to

01:05:52.166 --> 01:05:53.200
represent minority groups,

01:05:54.367 --> 01:05:58.300
working alongside folks that are able to translate to meet with minority

01:05:58.333 --> 01:06:02.000
populations that don't have the voice even to speak in the way that we would

01:06:02.066 --> 01:06:05.934
normally understand it, to just try and solicit as much public input as we

01:06:06.033 --> 01:06:09.767
possibly could from both the folks that can come out comfortably and do

01:06:09.867 --> 01:06:12.066
so routinely, but also for everybody else.

01:06:12.100 --> 01:06:13.300
I think that was an important

01:06:14.700 --> 01:06:18.066
amount of time spent, and I really do think it is reflected in the plan.

01:06:19.867 --> 01:06:22.767
And so I want to call out his efforts there.

01:06:23.567 --> 01:06:27.433
I also want to thank our city manager's office for supporting this

01:06:27.467 --> 01:06:31.367
through the budget process and the city council for providing us the funding to

01:06:31.400 --> 01:06:35.300
do this work, but also all my department head peers

01:06:35.400 --> 01:06:39.367
and colleagues and all of their staff because this plan was vetted at

01:06:39.400 --> 01:06:43.266
multiple stages through your technical staff members to advise on

01:06:43.300 --> 01:06:46.767
whether these things that we've included on the pages here are feasible,

01:06:47.600 --> 01:06:51.433
are responsive to what they're seeing on the ground, are real,

01:06:52.100 --> 01:06:56.066
and to inform how we can do them and where we can get the funding to do them

01:06:56.100 --> 01:06:59.533
and what creative ways we can approach some of these problems

01:07:00.400 --> 01:07:04.200
with. So this is not a plan represented just by even the

01:07:04.233 --> 01:07:08.100
CPC and planning, even though we certainly spent the more focused time

01:07:08.166 --> 01:07:12.033
on it, but everyone has weighed in, and I hope this is resonating

01:07:12.066 --> 01:07:13.100
in what's on the page.

01:07:13.834 --> 01:07:15.266
Milan, hang on for just one second.

01:07:16.066 --> 01:07:17.400
Can people hear Milan?

01:07:17.834 --> 01:07:18.734
Not really.

01:07:19.634 --> 01:07:23.400
Milan, we've noticed that this microphone requires you to practically

01:07:23.467 --> 01:07:26.767
eat it sometimes. So you really have to lean in.

01:07:27.066 --> 01:07:27.233
Okay.

01:07:28.000 --> 01:07:28.200
Thank you.

01:07:28.233 --> 01:07:28.667
Is this better?

01:07:29.266 --> 01:07:29.567
Yes.

01:07:30.567 --> 01:07:31.166
All right.

01:07:32.834 --> 01:07:33.967
It'll almost be better.

01:07:35.333 --> 01:07:36.500
Big thanks to everybody.

01:07:37.667 --> 01:07:38.300
So

01:07:39.800 --> 01:07:43.567
this council received the bulk of this plan

01:07:43.767 --> 01:07:45.567
back in June of 2025.

01:07:45.700 --> 01:07:46.066
Right.

01:07:46.133 --> 01:07:50.000
So I wanted to focus the presentation just on what's changed

01:07:50.100 --> 01:07:53.867
since then.And highlight some of the key things, and then

01:07:53.967 --> 01:07:57.834
as Chair Coonan had emphasized, obviously go into a

01:07:57.867 --> 01:08:01.500
little bit more detail on that Eastern Waterfront area, just because the policy

01:08:01.533 --> 01:08:05.500
approach there was just so nuanced and delicate to try and find

01:08:05.500 --> 01:08:07.600
a balance between everything that we had been hearing.

01:08:08.000 --> 01:08:11.934
So there'll be a little background, primarily overview of our plan changes,

01:08:11.967 --> 01:08:15.000
and then just a reminder again on what we're seeking from council tonight, which is

01:08:15.066 --> 01:08:17.066
a decision on where to go next.

01:08:18.900 --> 01:08:21.133
We obviously worked carefully with the Comp Plan committee.

01:08:21.300 --> 01:08:24.000
I just want to call out one more time that the Comp Plan committee is both the

01:08:24.033 --> 01:08:27.600
council's committee, but it's also the committee recognized by the state to perform

01:08:27.667 --> 01:08:31.133
the act of preparing a comprehensive plan and making a recommendation to you.

01:08:31.600 --> 01:08:33.567
So it does wield that responsibility.

01:08:34.567 --> 01:08:36.800
And I'm happy to report, as you have in your packet, that the

01:08:37.867 --> 01:08:38.066
state

01:08:39.066 --> 01:08:40.934
has found the committee's plan consistent.

01:08:42.233 --> 01:08:46.033
In the final slides, I will go over a little bit of process,

01:08:46.233 --> 01:08:49.800
and one of those procedural steps is that we do need to send this back to the state

01:08:49.834 --> 01:08:52.433
for recertification because there have been refinements.

01:08:52.800 --> 01:08:54.600
Any changes have to go back to the state.

01:08:55.500 --> 01:08:59.467
But in communication with them, all of the changes that the CPC and perhaps

01:08:59.500 --> 01:09:03.233
the council will introduce have proposed they don't

01:09:03.300 --> 01:09:06.200
really interact with anything that the state cares about.

01:09:07.266 --> 01:09:10.767
They're local policies, things like what are we doing with the Eastern Waterfront.

01:09:10.800 --> 01:09:14.433
The state sets larger state policies, and

01:09:14.467 --> 01:09:17.767
so the recertification is not a concern of ours,

01:09:18.934 --> 01:09:19.400
and we

01:09:20.333 --> 01:09:23.634
expect that we will be able to get recertified pending your guidance, obviously.

01:09:26.533 --> 01:09:30.166
This plan includes everything that the Comp Plan is supposed to include.

01:09:30.166 --> 01:09:32.600
But again, it also goes beyond that.

01:09:33.166 --> 01:09:36.767
What's been clear from the get-go when we engage with the community is that there

01:09:36.800 --> 01:09:39.934
are elements of the plan that

01:09:40.000 --> 01:09:43.667
don't get addressed by the state requirements, and we want to

01:09:43.700 --> 01:09:47.667
address them as a community. Those specifically are our policies related to health

01:09:47.667 --> 01:09:51.100
and well-being, social vitality, sustainability and climate action, and

01:09:51.166 --> 01:09:55.066
education. These don't appear on the state's checklist, but they're

01:09:55.066 --> 01:09:57.433
important to us and they have been included.

01:09:57.967 --> 01:10:01.333
I'm quite proud that we were able to dedicate the time to do that.

01:10:01.433 --> 01:10:05.300
We began this in 2021. We began with a sort of forensic analysis on

01:10:05.400 --> 01:10:08.900
how we've been doing with land use planning so far and the policies of 2012.

01:10:08.967 --> 01:10:11.634
This is a picture of an audit that we did of that plan.

01:10:12.233 --> 01:10:15.166
And then we engaged with the community, as I've mentioned a few times.

01:10:16.066 --> 01:10:19.800
Organizations, individuals, met them where they are, invited them

01:10:19.867 --> 01:10:23.533
into hosted curated meetings, workshops, walk shops,

01:10:24.066 --> 01:10:25.000
all manner of things.

01:10:26.166 --> 01:10:30.066
So far, the CPC has met on 64 occasions in formal meetings,

01:10:31.300 --> 01:10:35.000
with the staff attending an additional 23 meetings with other committees.

01:10:35.567 --> 01:10:39.467
And as of today, we've received about 450 written comments that

01:10:39.500 --> 01:10:43.000
have all been posted to CPC agendas and made available to the committee so that

01:10:43.000 --> 01:10:45.867
they can deliberate as they consider the next policy step.

01:10:46.734 --> 01:10:50.634
And in our final round of communications after engagement, after

01:10:50.734 --> 01:10:52.000
your June workshop,

01:10:52.834 --> 01:10:56.533
we continued that model. We added in a new interactive

01:10:56.700 --> 01:11:00.667
tool when we prepared that preliminary draft plan to have the community weigh in

01:11:00.767 --> 01:11:03.700
on it in real time. I think some of you engaged with it.

01:11:03.767 --> 01:11:06.800
You can digitally read the plan, submit your comments, see what your neighbors

01:11:06.800 --> 01:11:10.734
submitted, riff on that, add to that, like what they said, emphasize

01:11:10.767 --> 01:11:14.400
it, and so on. We had 688 submissions come through and a

01:11:14.867 --> 01:11:18.800
ton of content that the committee digested one by one and

01:11:18.867 --> 01:11:21.867
addressed each issue that was raised.

01:11:21.934 --> 01:11:25.800
When you last saw this plan, it was what we call a preliminary

01:11:25.900 --> 01:11:28.266
draft plan. We wanted to get your input on key

01:11:29.600 --> 01:11:31.867
questions, and you gave us the go ahead with some guidance.

01:11:32.100 --> 01:11:35.934
You're now seeing the final draft plan, and it is, if you accept it,

01:11:36.967 --> 01:11:40.634
ready for adoption as written, but can still be

01:11:40.667 --> 01:11:43.033
modified. So this is still very much an open process.

01:11:44.800 --> 01:11:46.834
I think you're familiar with the structure of the plan.

01:11:46.867 --> 01:11:49.567
It works through our goals and strategies.

01:11:49.600 --> 01:11:53.233
It's expressed spatially on a map called the Future Land Use

01:11:53.400 --> 01:11:53.734
plan.

01:11:54.900 --> 01:11:58.634
And it includes what's unique to South Portland, these key land use policy areas,

01:11:58.767 --> 01:11:59.300
KLUPAs.

01:12:01.100 --> 01:12:04.767
Our goals and strategies are expressed through six key themes.

01:12:05.166 --> 01:12:09.100
But since June, we've added a seventh one, and it's a little bit of a housekeeping

01:12:09.166 --> 01:12:12.967
administrative one. This is just a shell for all of the

01:12:13.200 --> 01:12:17.133
required state policies. Many of them are redundant and overlap

01:12:17.233 --> 01:12:20.667
substantially with policies we've adopted at the local level through themes one

01:12:20.667 --> 01:12:24.567
through six. But in order to make sure that we can easily pass

01:12:24.567 --> 01:12:28.567
the certification step, we also included the high-level required

01:12:28.600 --> 01:12:31.300
policies, and that's what theme seven is about.

01:12:31.433 --> 01:12:34.934
I just want to call that out because again, you did not see that last time.

01:12:34.967 --> 01:12:36.033
These are state required.

01:12:36.867 --> 01:12:39.800
This is what your future land use map looks like today.

01:12:40.333 --> 01:12:43.367
It's very similar to what you saw last time.

01:12:43.400 --> 01:12:46.166
We changed the colors and graphics a little bit for some ADA.

01:12:46.533 --> 01:12:50.033
We ran it through an ADA accessibility reader test and made some

01:12:50.066 --> 01:12:52.100
refinements to make sure we were accounting for that.

01:12:52.133 --> 01:12:55.600
That's why color-wise, it's quite different.

01:12:55.667 --> 01:12:59.367
But there are a handful of adjustments I do want to call out in what

01:13:00.233 --> 01:13:04.033
you saw last. First, we did not formally designate at the

01:13:04.066 --> 01:13:08.033
time in June what was happening outside of the key land use policy areas.

01:13:08.066 --> 01:13:10.700
We knew generally what the policy intent was.

01:13:10.700 --> 01:13:13.900
The future land use plan with the CPC's guidance has now cemented that.

01:13:14.567 --> 01:13:18.400
All of those areas you see in this plan with an X are outside

01:13:18.500 --> 01:13:22.333
of key land use policy areas. We expected that those areas would

01:13:22.400 --> 01:13:26.266
continue to contribute to the community in the way that they already

01:13:26.300 --> 01:13:28.066
are. So just do more of the same.

01:13:29.066 --> 01:13:33.000
And since we last saw you, we've now formalized that guidance and given it a

01:13:33.033 --> 01:13:36.133
limited growth designation, and this is the kind of growth where we're looking for

01:13:36.367 --> 01:13:38.700
infill development similar to what's there.

01:13:39.300 --> 01:13:42.967
So that's a small tweak, but an important one just to clarify what's happening in

01:13:43.000 --> 01:13:44.767
the voids between KLUPAs.

01:13:45.600 --> 01:13:46.867
Obviously, we've done

01:13:47.734 --> 01:13:49.700
considerable work within the Eastern Waterfront.

01:13:49.767 --> 01:13:52.166
I have dedicated slides to that, so we'll get back to that

01:13:52.233 --> 01:13:55.467
one.The Pleasantdale coastal area.

01:13:56.967 --> 01:14:00.567
Staff missed a bit on this one. We had a clear, I think,

01:14:00.667 --> 01:14:04.567
policy intent. I think the CPC read what our policy intent was, but the way we

01:14:04.600 --> 01:14:08.533
expressed the policies just got muddled when the community

01:14:08.567 --> 01:14:12.300
read it. And so we had to make some really simple but important

01:14:12.333 --> 01:14:15.500
distinctions to be clear about what we were doing in Pleasantdale, and it was to

01:14:15.533 --> 01:14:19.433
protect the single-family neighbors that are there, but also

01:14:19.467 --> 01:14:23.333
create some incentives so Broadway properties can do more for coastal resilience.

01:14:23.333 --> 01:14:24.700
So that was an important refinement.

01:14:26.200 --> 01:14:28.800
And then we did do some boundary adjustments here.

01:14:28.834 --> 01:14:32.734
There's a large commercial property, the former Aetna building, that we formerly

01:14:32.767 --> 01:14:36.634
put into CALUPA 1, which is a residential-focused CALUPA, and we

01:14:36.667 --> 01:14:40.033
moved it to CALUPA 2, which is a commercial-focused CALUPA.

01:14:40.133 --> 01:14:43.533
So, a few adjustments there. This is what our

01:14:43.734 --> 01:14:47.033
CALUPAs look like when you open them up on the page.

01:14:47.100 --> 01:14:50.367
It's a little different than what you saw last time, but generally very

01:14:50.533 --> 01:14:54.467
similar. It has all those elements that tell you what you're looking at.

01:14:54.867 --> 01:14:58.767
One thing that's been introduced since we last saw you is that we now set a

01:14:58.767 --> 01:15:02.767
preferred scale for the height and density

01:15:02.767 --> 01:15:05.333
of development within that area. That's residential density.

01:15:05.433 --> 01:15:07.600
So if the committee felt it was important

01:15:08.467 --> 01:15:12.367
for this area to have some form of a benchmark, to some form of a number

01:15:13.000 --> 01:15:16.867
to help identify what a certain policy statement might be, like when we

01:15:16.934 --> 01:15:19.333
say moderate growth, what does that mean?

01:15:19.367 --> 01:15:21.500
Well, in some CALUPAs, it's really sensitive.

01:15:22.066 --> 01:15:26.000
And so we took the time to insert those guidance posts there.

01:15:26.000 --> 01:15:28.634
And then, of course, there's been refinements through the language all throughout.

01:15:28.667 --> 01:15:32.567
But that scale preference piece was an important addition, and we've

01:15:32.634 --> 01:15:33.867
gone through each CALUPA and

01:15:34.700 --> 01:15:35.266
addressed that.

01:15:37.166 --> 01:15:39.467
All right. Major revisions since June,

01:15:40.467 --> 01:15:42.133
starting with the Eastern Waterfront.

01:15:42.233 --> 01:15:43.867
This is the map that you saw

01:15:44.700 --> 01:15:48.166
back at your workshop. We divided the Eastern Waterfront into two areas.

01:15:48.233 --> 01:15:50.834
They're labeled one and two there, light and dark green.

01:15:51.934 --> 01:15:55.700
The kind of organizing principle at the time was that we look at

01:15:55.734 --> 01:15:59.700
the tank properties or tank-held properties as one

01:15:59.734 --> 01:16:03.433
thing because they needed focus guidance, and then the other

01:16:03.634 --> 01:16:04.900
more development-ready

01:16:06.166 --> 01:16:07.767
areas as something else.

01:16:09.200 --> 01:16:11.634
And then created a whole policy framework on that.

01:16:11.734 --> 01:16:15.367
As a quick reminder, area one, I think that number was

01:16:15.400 --> 01:16:17.433
14 at the time that we presented it to you.

01:16:18.000 --> 01:16:19.433
It was meant to be those tank areas.

01:16:19.767 --> 01:16:23.333
It was supposed to follow what happens in the other

01:16:23.400 --> 01:16:27.333
area once those tanks were gone. So it just sort of deferred to

01:16:27.667 --> 01:16:29.300
the second one.

01:16:30.233 --> 01:16:33.567
And some of the key pieces in there was that it would have to be scaled down.

01:16:33.967 --> 01:16:37.000
So it had single-family neighbors. It had to ratchet down.

01:16:37.400 --> 01:16:40.433
It had to be substantially set back from the water, any development.

01:16:40.834 --> 01:16:42.734
Residential was not allowed near the water.

01:16:43.066 --> 01:16:45.834
Greenbelt needed to be expanded, and we were supposed to track and reduce

01:16:45.867 --> 01:16:49.667
emissions. Those are some of the high-level key focal points for area

01:16:49.800 --> 01:16:51.166
one. And area two

01:16:52.333 --> 01:16:55.634
allowed a mix of uses, was able to reach up to six stories,

01:16:56.367 --> 01:16:59.700
focused on pedestrian scale, required contamination cleanup,

01:17:00.200 --> 01:17:03.433
connected open spaces need to be provided that build on,

01:17:03.533 --> 01:17:06.867
expand Bug Light, and also become available for the neighbors.

01:17:07.634 --> 01:17:09.100
Freight traffic had to be avoided.

01:17:09.233 --> 01:17:12.834
Traffic needed to avoid Ferry Village and not connect to Ferry Village.

01:17:13.500 --> 01:17:17.000
That's a 2012 policy that was still there back in June.

01:17:18.166 --> 01:17:20.333
It needed to provide access to the waterfront.

01:17:20.734 --> 01:17:23.300
Peak hour traffic on Broadway needed to be limited.

01:17:23.800 --> 01:17:27.567
The street network had to be connected. Transit had to be supported.

01:17:27.934 --> 01:17:31.700
We need to provide a mix of housing types and the forested area near Bug

01:17:31.767 --> 01:17:33.634
Light that we all read as Bug Light

01:17:34.500 --> 01:17:34.700
would

01:17:35.533 --> 01:17:39.166
be acquired hopefully, or we would put energy towards acquiring it.

01:17:40.367 --> 01:17:42.100
This is the map that's before you today.

01:17:43.000 --> 01:17:46.400
We have two CALUPAs, 21 and 22,

01:17:47.166 --> 01:17:50.600
and I'm just showing there Bug Light, which is in a separate CALUPA, always has

01:17:50.634 --> 01:17:54.634
been, and that's 23. All the formal city property is

01:17:54.667 --> 01:17:55.333
separated out.

01:17:56.567 --> 01:17:57.867
I'm going to just highlight the key

01:17:59.100 --> 01:18:02.500
approaches now that we have with CALUPA 21 and 22.

01:18:03.533 --> 01:18:04.467
In 21,

01:18:05.634 --> 01:18:08.567
this is now not a tank-oriented CALUPA.

01:18:08.734 --> 01:18:11.767
It's focused on what we're doing in the coastal area.

01:18:11.900 --> 01:18:15.300
One of the sticking points was that we had this policy that says

01:18:15.667 --> 01:18:18.867
residential needs to be set back from the water, trying to respond to some of the

01:18:18.900 --> 01:18:21.100
concerns around sea level rise, flooding,

01:18:22.233 --> 01:18:22.800
and so on.

01:18:23.734 --> 01:18:27.367
But it was always difficult with the committee to address, well, what do we mean by

01:18:27.400 --> 01:18:29.800
set back? Is it 10 feet? Is it 5,000 feet?

01:18:29.867 --> 01:18:32.767
Is it something in between? What's the appropriate number?

01:18:33.233 --> 01:18:34.734
Comp plans are supposed to be guidance.

01:18:34.900 --> 01:18:35.834
So it's not

01:18:37.934 --> 01:18:41.834
common to have something so specific prescribed in here, but we wanted to give

01:18:41.867 --> 01:18:45.800
better definition to it. So by changing the boundaries and saying, "Let's be more

01:18:45.867 --> 01:18:49.367
specific about what we want near the water because that's clearly a sensitivity,

01:18:49.934 --> 01:18:53.100
and then let's look at inland upland property a little bit differently," that

01:18:53.133 --> 01:18:55.266
helped drive how we drew the boundaries.

01:18:56.367 --> 01:19:00.100
In those areas closest to the water, we ratcheted the

01:19:00.066 --> 01:19:02.934
heights down from six stories to three stories.

01:19:03.467 --> 01:19:06.667
We added policies around preserving public views.

01:19:07.200 --> 01:19:10.867
We stated that open spaces had to be maximized, not just

01:19:10.867 --> 01:19:14.667
provided. Stormwater had to be retained, and space had to be

01:19:14.734 --> 01:19:18.200
relegated for the stormwater retention as well as coastal

01:19:19.200 --> 01:19:20.967
buffering from the storms and flooding.

01:19:21.934 --> 01:19:25.734
And we had to connect to rather than avoid Ferry Village and

01:19:25.900 --> 01:19:26.500
fight this

01:19:27.533 --> 01:19:29.667
prior policy of creating an enclave.

01:19:29.700 --> 01:19:32.867
We wanted it to be, if it was going to be developed, be developed

01:19:33.500 --> 01:19:36.667
contiguous with and connected with and

01:19:36.767 --> 01:19:40.100
benefiting the surrounding neighborhoods, not separate and apart from.

01:19:41.367 --> 01:19:42.166
21B

01:19:43.166 --> 01:19:46.166
is a sub-area. There's two in there, A and B.

01:19:46.300 --> 01:19:50.000
21B is over by the Portland Terminal and that forested area of Bug

01:19:50.066 --> 01:19:53.734
Light. It allows a much more restricted

01:19:54.266 --> 01:19:58.166
number of land uses because it says that only land uses that

01:19:58.233 --> 01:20:01.867
are water-dependent can exist there, so water-dependent forms of

01:20:01.934 --> 01:20:05.500
retail, water-dependent forms of marine uses, and so

01:20:05.567 --> 01:20:09.567
on.And then we've always had this policy of expanding the green

01:20:09.634 --> 01:20:13.367
belt, but we've added specific guidance around dedicating shoreline

01:20:13.433 --> 01:20:15.867
trail access, wraparound access, and through access.

01:20:16.000 --> 01:20:18.367
That's all contained in the new Area 21.

01:20:19.767 --> 01:20:21.767
The points here I won't emphasize.

01:20:21.834 --> 01:20:24.300
They're just things that we continue to maintain.

01:20:24.400 --> 01:20:26.300
It's not that they've been forgotten.

01:20:26.400 --> 01:20:30.000
So things about tracking VOCs, cleaning up contaminants,

01:20:30.266 --> 01:20:33.100
expanding Bug Light Park, all of that has been retained.

01:20:33.200 --> 01:20:37.100
I just want to highlight the things that are sort of unique and try to address

01:20:37.133 --> 01:20:38.100
the concerns we were hearing.

01:20:39.233 --> 01:20:42.834
In Area 22, the inland upland portions of the Eastern Waterfront,

01:20:43.600 --> 01:20:47.333
there are four sub-designations, A, B, C, and D, and

01:20:47.367 --> 01:20:50.900
generally, that gave us a policy framework so we could

01:20:51.634 --> 01:20:55.600
change what's happening and what we allow to happen closest to

01:20:55.667 --> 01:20:59.634
the water, and as we move further upland where it starts to get more closely

01:20:59.700 --> 01:21:00.166
resilient,

01:21:01.233 --> 01:21:02.734
we can allow more.

01:21:03.700 --> 01:21:06.433
So 22A and 22D

01:21:07.467 --> 01:21:11.433
also interact with the tanks. And so they have a specific provision

01:21:11.467 --> 01:21:14.800
in there about assessing VOC risks.

01:21:14.867 --> 01:21:18.433
This applies citywide, but we emphasize it again here.

01:21:18.467 --> 01:21:22.100
And I think the important language that we use is that residential can be

01:21:22.166 --> 01:21:25.500
considered. It is not an automatic allowance.

01:21:26.233 --> 01:21:29.533
Zoning has to happen with any of the recommendations in this plan,

01:21:30.033 --> 01:21:33.700
but there is no requirement to allow residential.

01:21:33.734 --> 01:21:37.634
The city council has to be sufficiently convinced that it's appropriate

01:21:38.166 --> 01:21:42.100
when it designates zoning in order to make that possible.

01:21:42.166 --> 01:21:45.867
So this is just saying what conversations can be had and not

01:21:45.967 --> 01:21:49.000
opening up residential upon plan adoption.

01:21:49.000 --> 01:21:51.734
And I have some procedure steps later on that we'll touch on.

01:21:52.767 --> 01:21:55.834
As the plan moves inland upland,

01:21:56.967 --> 01:22:00.100
the sub-areas A, B, C, and D ratchet up in scale.

01:22:00.200 --> 01:22:03.467
Closest to the water, it maintains that three-story height limit,

01:22:04.266 --> 01:22:08.200
and then as you get to 22C, it can allow up to five stories, and

01:22:08.333 --> 01:22:09.600
22D, which

01:22:10.467 --> 01:22:14.100
currently has those two tanks, but also the vacant property that abuts Broadway,

01:22:14.467 --> 01:22:18.166
can reach up to six stories, and that was to parallel what we see at Betsy Ross.

01:22:19.567 --> 01:22:22.100
Additions to 22 that are important

01:22:23.033 --> 01:22:26.533
is that bike ped improvements have to be provided, and they have to be provided by

01:22:26.634 --> 01:22:28.133
a developer at the time of development.

01:22:28.867 --> 01:22:32.433
Public views must be maintained, and importantly in

01:22:32.634 --> 01:22:36.333
22, because much of this area does not provide a view

01:22:36.600 --> 01:22:40.333
for the public from a public vantage point, they have to be newly

01:22:40.400 --> 01:22:44.266
created for the first time. The open spaces need to be

01:22:44.300 --> 01:22:48.266
maximized. We are targeting clean energy sector additions out here

01:22:48.333 --> 01:22:52.100
because there was a desire to see some industrial heritage maintained,

01:22:52.934 --> 01:22:56.867
but we want to do it in a more benign and more residential compatible way for the

01:22:56.900 --> 01:22:57.767
neighbors nearby.

01:22:58.667 --> 01:23:02.533
Coastal resilience is emphasized at the terminals, and we'd work in

01:23:03.100 --> 01:23:06.533
concert with Maine DEP, the Department of Environmental Protection,

01:23:07.033 --> 01:23:10.433
or even at the local level to ensure that those properties

01:23:10.533 --> 01:23:14.266
are resilient to coastal storms. We, again, would

01:23:14.333 --> 01:23:18.233
rather connect to than avoid Ferry Village and expand

01:23:18.266 --> 01:23:20.400
the green belt as well as that shoreline trail.

01:23:20.467 --> 01:23:22.266
The text in blue are all those additions.

01:23:22.600 --> 01:23:26.367
And again, the other familiar language from June is still in

01:23:26.433 --> 01:23:30.333
here, controlling for Broadway traffic, contamination cleanup, VOC

01:23:30.433 --> 01:23:31.600
tracking, et cetera.

01:23:33.433 --> 01:23:37.233
I want to now speak in a little bit more plain terms rather than policy terms on

01:23:38.000 --> 01:23:41.800
what success looks like based on the policies that are written on

01:23:41.834 --> 01:23:44.400
this page for the Eastern Waterfront on some

01:23:45.800 --> 01:23:48.133
major topics, traffic and three others.

01:23:49.000 --> 01:23:52.600
On traffic, this plan asks for limited

01:23:52.734 --> 01:23:56.567
new freight traffic on Broadway. It will be a transit-supportive

01:23:56.600 --> 01:24:00.100
form of development. It will be connected streets and avoid an

01:24:00.166 --> 01:24:03.867
enclave. Broadway will be familiar to what we know today.

01:24:04.834 --> 01:24:08.667
Bug Light access will be improved, enhanced, and made better

01:24:08.734 --> 01:24:12.467
in all possible ways. Bike ped access will be

01:24:12.500 --> 01:24:15.900
substantially better. Housing will be

01:24:15.934 --> 01:24:19.667
diversified, not purely luxury, set back,

01:24:19.900 --> 01:24:22.533
and flood resilient, human scaled.

01:24:23.166 --> 01:24:26.667
It will be inland and upland. It will be connected to and

01:24:26.767 --> 01:24:29.000
compatible with surrounding neighborhoods.

01:24:30.133 --> 01:24:33.033
On coastal resilience, the plan will only accept

01:24:34.634 --> 01:24:38.467
development that is responsive to the best available data, that

01:24:38.567 --> 01:24:42.533
leans on the cross, but then tracks new science and gets updated as

01:24:42.600 --> 01:24:46.500
we learn more. Flood mitigation is promoted and required

01:24:46.533 --> 01:24:50.166
in the way that land is used, and private developers have to

01:24:50.200 --> 01:24:51.634
contribute to that process.

01:24:53.033 --> 01:24:54.934
On open space recreation,

01:24:55.834 --> 01:24:59.533
a minimum substantial area has to be reserved

01:24:59.900 --> 01:25:01.634
even through private development.

01:25:02.233 --> 01:25:06.000
At least 20 acres is easily required by the plan,

01:25:06.166 --> 01:25:09.967
both in the conservation that's required over at the Bug Light adjacent area, the

01:25:10.033 --> 01:25:13.533
coastal areas, the green belt expansions, the public

01:25:13.567 --> 01:25:15.767
pathways that are required through here.

01:25:15.834 --> 01:25:16.600
That is a minimum,

01:25:17.834 --> 01:25:21.800
and certainly much, much more can be preserved and will depend on

01:25:21.834 --> 01:25:25.700
the partners that we strike, including nonprofits that may

01:25:25.734 --> 01:25:27.934
develop and acquire that land.

01:25:28.734 --> 01:25:32.533
The green belt will be widened, extended, provide more

01:25:32.567 --> 01:25:35.867
features, provide more views, provide access to more

01:25:35.934 --> 01:25:39.367
places. Bug Light will be expanded and

01:25:39.433 --> 01:25:43.333
sheltered from development or any adjacent development that may

01:25:43.400 --> 01:25:45.400
detract from what Bug Light is today.

01:25:46.567 --> 01:25:50.500
The open spaces will be woven into any development that has to

01:25:50.600 --> 01:25:54.500
come forward, not relegated as side thoughts at the time of

01:25:54.533 --> 01:25:58.233
development. And the Portland Street Pier property that the city manages

01:25:58.567 --> 01:26:02.433
will be supported and potentially finally have access to parking so

01:26:02.467 --> 01:26:06.133
that people can enjoy that benefit, whether the city's operating it or

01:26:06.433 --> 01:26:07.634
other operators are operating

01:26:07.667 --> 01:26:11.600
it.Shifting to other

01:26:11.667 --> 01:26:13.967
areas of the plan that were changed.

01:26:14.500 --> 01:26:17.300
We spent some time refining, as I mentioned, those scale

01:26:17.400 --> 01:26:21.300
preferences. When we published those, we heard feedback and we made

01:26:21.400 --> 01:26:25.266
further refinements, specifically on height and density preferences.

01:26:25.333 --> 01:26:29.266
We spent a little more time being delicate in areas like Mill Creek,

01:26:29.667 --> 01:26:31.900
where we have the letter streets and the more

01:26:34.066 --> 01:26:38.066
like the Knightville areas, which are smaller scale, but then also the concept

01:26:38.100 --> 01:26:40.166
of our downtown wanting to provide opportunities.

01:26:40.166 --> 01:26:42.967
So right now, Mill Creek advocates for six stories.

01:26:43.400 --> 01:26:46.900
It's potentially able to reach up to eight stories, but it requires a

01:26:46.967 --> 01:26:50.900
high-quality design proposal, and we would articulate what that is

01:26:50.967 --> 01:26:54.600
through the zoning framework. Our Nutter Hill inland tank farm,

01:26:55.600 --> 01:26:58.834
which has had significant community conversation.

01:26:59.200 --> 01:27:02.667
We learned from the community meetings and the thoughts shared through

01:27:03.300 --> 01:27:06.900
that effort. The Toss the Tanks folks were coming to many of our

01:27:06.967 --> 01:27:07.367
meetings,

01:27:08.600 --> 01:27:11.467
and we tried to reflect scale that we saw in the plan.

01:27:11.567 --> 01:27:15.433
Notably, five stories within the core seems reasonable,

01:27:15.800 --> 01:27:19.700
but it must taper and blend into the single-family neighborhoods around

01:27:20.066 --> 01:27:23.400
in order for it to be a successful and acceptable kind of development.

01:27:24.200 --> 01:27:27.634
And then over at the Sable Oaks area, we received, I think, some really

01:27:27.700 --> 01:27:31.400
interesting technical feedback from folks that

01:27:31.600 --> 01:27:35.600
have explored development out there that shed more detail on

01:27:35.667 --> 01:27:39.266
the development constraints because of ledge, because of wetlands, because of

01:27:39.300 --> 01:27:43.233
natural resources, and we responded by saying we still want to

01:27:43.300 --> 01:27:46.467
strive for that 20 units per acre. That is still our preference.

01:27:47.000 --> 01:27:50.867
But when we learn more details, technical details, geotechnical

01:27:50.967 --> 01:27:54.867
details, we will develop ordinances that allow us to have an

01:27:54.900 --> 01:27:58.834
intelligent conversation understanding those constraints and when they might

01:27:58.867 --> 01:27:58.967
get

01:28:00.533 --> 01:28:03.066
in the way of specifically that number in the plan.

01:28:04.600 --> 01:28:08.500
Corridors have been something that we talked about very

01:28:08.567 --> 01:28:11.967
early in the plan process and always understood as something we wanted to

01:28:12.000 --> 01:28:13.667
incorporate in this plan.

01:28:14.900 --> 01:28:17.634
But they got somewhat muted as we kept developing the

01:28:17.667 --> 01:28:21.667
policies. One of the refinements we made was to adopt

01:28:21.667 --> 01:28:25.333
a specific provision that just tied a

01:28:25.400 --> 01:28:28.900
bow around policies that we have been saying but not quite

01:28:28.934 --> 01:28:32.767
articulating, and that is that all areas of the community are going to need to

01:28:32.834 --> 01:28:36.767
contribute to housing in a way that's comfortable for what that area is.

01:28:37.367 --> 01:28:40.934
Some areas are comfortable for more levels of growth than others.

01:28:41.500 --> 01:28:45.500
I talked earlier about our non-CA LUPA areas, which should just continue

01:28:45.533 --> 01:28:49.500
to be like what they already are, but provide some infill opportunities.

01:28:50.667 --> 01:28:54.066
Wherever we land with the density designations throughout town, the

01:28:54.166 --> 01:28:56.800
corridors are expected to get a little oomph.

01:28:57.266 --> 01:29:01.200
They should be able to do a little more and provide a little bit more diversity and

01:29:01.233 --> 01:29:04.533
a little bit more flexibility. We're not talking going from two stories and then

01:29:04.567 --> 01:29:07.967
suddenly on a corridor you're at 10 stories, but the concept of the

01:29:08.033 --> 01:29:10.433
corridors can do a little bit more.

01:29:10.433 --> 01:29:13.567
And this is to try and reinforce the

01:29:13.667 --> 01:29:17.133
transit-oriented development that we need to see in our community to try and

01:29:17.166 --> 01:29:21.033
provide for our transit goals in the long term, but also where

01:29:21.100 --> 01:29:24.567
we see infrastructure and the potential to develop

01:29:24.634 --> 01:29:26.900
without disrupting neighborhoods.

01:29:28.500 --> 01:29:31.467
I talked a little bit about Pleasantdale coastal area.

01:29:31.500 --> 01:29:35.100
This is a technical correction, but it's important because we had some very

01:29:35.200 --> 01:29:38.600
specific comments come back from neighbors concerned, and I want to clarify it in

01:29:38.634 --> 01:29:39.500
case they're here tonight.

01:29:40.567 --> 01:29:44.300
The policy for Pleasantdale was always a level B

01:29:44.333 --> 01:29:48.133
designation of growth, which is just consistent with what it already is.

01:29:48.166 --> 01:29:51.700
What we were trying to achieve and what I think the policy now clearly states is

01:29:51.734 --> 01:29:55.000
that Broadway has the potential for the back lots

01:29:55.000 --> 01:29:58.700
behind these fronting properties to do more for

01:29:58.734 --> 01:30:01.400
coastal resilience by allowing inundation area.

01:30:01.934 --> 01:30:05.567
And we would try to encourage those Broadway property owners to provide that

01:30:05.667 --> 01:30:09.600
benefit by allowing more development flexibility closer to the street,

01:30:10.066 --> 01:30:13.734
to try and bring structures and pavement closer to the street and

01:30:13.867 --> 01:30:17.600
have open space in the back that can serve as that buffer.

01:30:18.100 --> 01:30:21.533
And so I think we just tried to clear up the language on that, but it was no

01:30:21.600 --> 01:30:24.767
different in the intent from what you saw in June, just better language.

01:30:26.100 --> 01:30:29.800
This slide has a lot on it. I will come back to it if there's council questions.

01:30:29.867 --> 01:30:32.100
I'm sure questions will come up from the public.

01:30:32.667 --> 01:30:35.967
Air quality and handling the VOC emissions

01:30:36.066 --> 01:30:39.567
tracking is an important consideration throughout this plan.

01:30:39.600 --> 01:30:43.367
We have citywide policies to mind this issue

01:30:43.433 --> 01:30:47.300
across the entire city, but we also have CA LUPA policies specifically

01:30:47.367 --> 01:30:48.767
where we know it's a sensitivity.

01:30:50.233 --> 01:30:52.867
There are three key things that we're doing.

01:30:52.900 --> 01:30:55.533
Howard, our chair, emphasized them here.

01:30:55.667 --> 01:30:59.033
I just want to highlight the headlines here, but we have specific references to, I

01:30:59.100 --> 01:31:00.867
think, relevant policy on this issue.

01:31:01.500 --> 01:31:05.100
Number one, we are supporting in any possible way the

01:31:05.166 --> 01:31:08.967
transition of the tank terminals into something that's an

01:31:09.000 --> 01:31:12.000
alternative, cleaner, better, more compatible form of development.

01:31:12.700 --> 01:31:15.500
That's a general policy, and there are many

01:31:16.634 --> 01:31:18.900
kind of nuanced policies that try to get at that.

01:31:18.967 --> 01:31:22.767
Some of them are listed here. The second major thread here is

01:31:22.800 --> 01:31:25.934
that we are committing to tracking,

01:31:26.133 --> 01:31:30.066
evaluating, overall reducing VOCs, and that

01:31:30.100 --> 01:31:33.567
we are not allowing new residential until the city council is

01:31:33.600 --> 01:31:37.166
sufficiently satisfied that the VOC emission risks

01:31:37.266 --> 01:31:40.400
are insignificant and it is

01:31:40.467 --> 01:31:44.367
appropriate to allow development next to them, but only then can

01:31:44.367 --> 01:31:45.467
residential be considered.

01:31:47.066 --> 01:31:51.000
And then we do have kind of related contamination

01:31:51.066 --> 01:31:54.934
cleanup policies throughout the plan to make sure that any

01:31:54.967 --> 01:31:58.700
existing contaminants are not evaporating and causing air

01:31:58.734 --> 01:32:01.567
quality or other contamination concerns to our waterways.

01:32:03.400 --> 01:32:07.266
Three other major amendments that we integrated since

01:32:07.367 --> 01:32:07.734
June.

01:32:08.634 --> 01:32:12.634
Civic facilities. TheCouncil has been engaged in a pretty

01:32:12.734 --> 01:32:16.667
heavy conversation about what the future of our city facilities are.

01:32:16.734 --> 01:32:20.700
And what we realize is that the plan, in some cases, largely pulling from

01:32:20.700 --> 01:32:24.634
the 2012 plan, was maybe a little too prescriptive in where

01:32:24.667 --> 01:32:27.066
we expected those solutions to come about.

01:32:27.133 --> 01:32:31.066
So one thing we did is we built in maximum flexibility

01:32:31.100 --> 01:32:34.400
for the city council with the community to

01:32:34.433 --> 01:32:38.233
designate where it wants to provide civic services and how it

01:32:38.333 --> 01:32:42.166
wants to provide those. And if CALUPAs were involved, they also echoed that

01:32:42.233 --> 01:32:46.200
language. We are trying to give, again, maximum flexibility so that we can

01:32:46.266 --> 01:32:50.066
do the best thing for the community for the long term with locating civic

01:32:50.233 --> 01:32:54.066
facilities. On open space and resilience, we

01:32:54.133 --> 01:32:57.967
introduced policy 1C19. The intent of

01:32:58.000 --> 01:33:01.767
this policy is to give council the best possible negotiating

01:33:01.834 --> 01:33:05.533
position to secure open space and coastal

01:33:05.567 --> 01:33:06.834
resilience commitments

01:33:07.634 --> 01:33:08.367
from development,

01:33:09.433 --> 01:33:12.667
and to make sure that they contribute to that process.

01:33:13.567 --> 01:33:17.367
And then finally, this is a bit of a wonky piece in our new

01:33:17.467 --> 01:33:18.100
policies.

01:33:19.266 --> 01:33:22.634
This planning office, together with the economic development office, has been

01:33:22.667 --> 01:33:26.233
working on a number of streamlining measures to help development move along.

01:33:26.667 --> 01:33:30.433
One of those is to look at absorbing state-level review for

01:33:30.467 --> 01:33:32.834
environmental impacts in the planning department.

01:33:33.634 --> 01:33:35.100
This is called delegated authority.

01:33:35.333 --> 01:33:37.934
MDEP would allow us to do the review for them.

01:33:38.867 --> 01:33:42.767
This requires some research and some work, and eventually a presentation

01:33:42.800 --> 01:33:46.667
to the city council, and discussions with the city manager and others on things

01:33:46.667 --> 01:33:50.500
like staffing capacity and so on. But if it is the right decision

01:33:50.567 --> 01:33:54.467
for the city of South Portland, it will require a statement

01:33:54.533 --> 01:33:57.634
in the plan that we are okay going that route.

01:33:58.166 --> 01:34:02.066
And so we've included the state-required verbatim language that

01:34:02.200 --> 01:34:05.734
just says, "If we're ready to make the decision, here's the policy that would

01:34:05.767 --> 01:34:09.233
support that decision. If we never make the decision, we're not obligated, and we

01:34:09.266 --> 01:34:12.734
keep doing what we're doing." But we just needed this in there, otherwise you'd

01:34:12.767 --> 01:34:16.066
have to amend the plan later if it was the smart thing for us to be doing.

01:34:17.634 --> 01:34:21.467
Okay. On comp plan implementation, I just want to walk

01:34:21.533 --> 01:34:24.533
us through, briefly, process. This is important.

01:34:25.033 --> 01:34:28.900
As we've been going through this long-range planning effort, we often hear

01:34:28.934 --> 01:34:30.934
comments, I'm sure you may hear some tonight,

01:34:31.767 --> 01:34:35.734
reacting to a policy statement on the page and a reality

01:34:35.767 --> 01:34:37.800
that that's going to create tomorrow.

01:34:38.367 --> 01:34:40.834
And that's unfortunately just not how it works.

01:34:41.567 --> 01:34:45.266
It takes a long time for the policy to become

01:34:45.433 --> 01:34:49.367
anything that can be realized. We are at this stage, the comp plan

01:34:49.433 --> 01:34:53.066
adoption stage. If you accept it, it would be adopted by the council

01:34:53.166 --> 01:34:57.066
tonight. We'd send it for certification with the state, and I'm confident

01:34:57.100 --> 01:34:58.100
we would receive that.

01:34:59.033 --> 01:35:02.467
Once the comp plan actually gives us direction through the adopted plan,

01:35:02.934 --> 01:35:06.634
we then go through mostly a zoning process, but there are many

01:35:06.767 --> 01:35:10.734
other processes that will have to take place with the non-land use provisions

01:35:10.800 --> 01:35:13.367
that are in this policy. On just land use,

01:35:14.800 --> 01:35:18.500
allowing residential in certain areas, for instance, changing the density, changing

01:35:18.533 --> 01:35:19.367
the heights allowed.

01:35:20.467 --> 01:35:22.567
We need time to develop that zoning.

01:35:23.333 --> 01:35:27.200
The comprehensive plan committee will presumably be involved

01:35:27.300 --> 01:35:30.667
in more of the significant ones, but the council will direct us to work with

01:35:30.700 --> 01:35:31.867
committees if it wants to.

01:35:33.066 --> 01:35:36.734
At a minimum, though, the planning board will have to review any zoning proposals.

01:35:36.767 --> 01:35:39.166
They do that at a planning board hearing.

01:35:39.200 --> 01:35:42.667
And that will be presented to council eventually through two ordinance adoptions,

01:35:42.700 --> 01:35:46.166
and a super majority is needed for zoning amendments, not just

01:35:46.200 --> 01:35:50.100
four. At the time that any zoning is presented, and I'll

01:35:50.166 --> 01:35:54.000
touch on the sensitive topic I'm sure you've already heard and will hear

01:35:54.033 --> 01:35:57.867
more about tonight is the allowance of residential near industrial

01:35:57.934 --> 01:36:01.667
areas. The council needs to evaluate whether that zoning

01:36:01.734 --> 01:36:03.133
proposal includes

01:36:04.200 --> 01:36:06.133
policies that are consistent with the plan,

01:36:07.166 --> 01:36:10.533
and that those policies are responding to

01:36:11.133 --> 01:36:14.400
what information we have available, and is the information that we have available

01:36:14.433 --> 01:36:14.934
complete.

01:36:15.767 --> 01:36:19.600
Are the VOC studies complete, and does the plan include the controls that

01:36:19.634 --> 01:36:21.400
respond to the data that we've received?

01:36:22.433 --> 01:36:25.934
What is the state of the tank farms? Are they operating? Are they operating at max?

01:36:25.967 --> 01:36:28.600
Are they operating with the potential to increase?

01:36:28.634 --> 01:36:30.767
What is the coastal flooding modeling telling us?

01:36:32.033 --> 01:36:34.767
What is the CROZ doing, and is it performing?

01:36:34.834 --> 01:36:38.133
Are the developments that we've seen with the CROZ, Coastal Resilience Overlay

01:36:38.266 --> 01:36:42.066
Zone, working? And is the CROZ still

01:36:42.100 --> 01:36:44.867
effective five years out as new models come about?

01:36:45.567 --> 01:36:48.600
What are the market conditions, and of course, what does the rest of the

01:36:48.667 --> 01:36:52.634
comprehensive plan say, and is this zoning proposal

01:36:52.934 --> 01:36:53.500
consistent?

01:36:54.667 --> 01:36:57.834
We expect over the next three years to be working on zoning.

01:36:58.600 --> 01:36:59.934
Even when zoning is adopted,

01:37:00.867 --> 01:37:04.734
it takes time for a development proposal to be drafted that is

01:37:05.634 --> 01:37:07.166
reflective of that zoning.

01:37:08.033 --> 01:37:11.734
We would expect there to be a lag after the zoning is adopted

01:37:11.767 --> 01:37:15.667
before we ever entertain any formal development proposal, and then that goes

01:37:15.734 --> 01:37:16.934
through development review.

01:37:17.967 --> 01:37:21.700
At the time of development review, based on the specific development in the

01:37:21.734 --> 01:37:25.600
specific location at the specific scale and the specific mix of

01:37:25.734 --> 01:37:29.567
uses that's proposed, we will be looking at what are the traffic impacts,

01:37:29.634 --> 01:37:33.500
if any. What is the lot plan and layout, and how does that marry

01:37:33.567 --> 01:37:36.800
up with the standards of the plan and the standards of the zoning ordinance that

01:37:36.900 --> 01:37:40.166
implement the plan? Are the elevations that are proposed

01:37:40.433 --> 01:37:44.233
consistent with where we expect them to be for that type of building and for

01:37:44.266 --> 01:37:45.166
that type of use?

01:37:46.100 --> 01:37:49.967
Is the view that's supposed to be preserved actually preserved?

01:37:50.300 --> 01:37:54.100
And so on and so forth. Development typically takes

01:37:54.200 --> 01:37:57.867
two to three years beyond the point when they've actually received their

01:37:57.900 --> 01:38:01.600
approval. So we need to understand all of these

01:38:01.600 --> 01:38:05.600
steps and layers to doing the checks and balances to make the

01:38:05.634 --> 01:38:08.667
policy something that's real and something that takes

01:38:08.734 --> 01:38:12.567
shape on the land.I want to speak a little

01:38:12.600 --> 01:38:16.300
bit to comp plan implementation, because this is a multiple

01:38:16.367 --> 01:38:19.867
hundred-page document. It includes appendices, it includes

01:38:20.000 --> 01:38:23.433
background information, and as we learned through the comprehensive plan audit of

01:38:23.467 --> 01:38:26.367
the 2012 plan, not everything got implemented.

01:38:28.266 --> 01:38:32.100
Our approach, and we're still developing this, so you will see this over the

01:38:32.133 --> 01:38:34.100
next year if the plan is adopted,

01:38:35.400 --> 01:38:39.166
is to continue working with the comprehensive plan committee, which is going to

01:38:39.266 --> 01:38:40.900
shift into implementation mode.

01:38:41.767 --> 01:38:45.266
The planning division is primarily administering the plan,

01:38:45.800 --> 01:38:49.533
but every one of these policies in here includes a responsible

01:38:49.567 --> 01:38:53.333
party who's responsible for reporting on progress made to that

01:38:53.467 --> 01:38:53.834
plan.

01:38:54.700 --> 01:38:56.166
So the CPC is a key

01:38:57.233 --> 01:39:00.767
actor in making sure that we're making progress, the planning division, and of

01:39:00.800 --> 01:39:02.600
course, the city manager and city council.

01:39:03.800 --> 01:39:07.700
The CPC is going to receive staff reports regularly as

01:39:07.767 --> 01:39:09.767
it meets on how we're doing with progress.

01:39:10.500 --> 01:39:14.467
They will, as a first step in the fall, be working on a work plan

01:39:14.634 --> 01:39:18.300
that takes that timeframe column of when we expect each policy to be

01:39:18.367 --> 01:39:21.467
implemented and move it into an action plan.

01:39:22.500 --> 01:39:26.333
So these policies don't necessarily express exactly what's going to be

01:39:26.367 --> 01:39:28.333
done in order to make the policy happen.

01:39:28.634 --> 01:39:32.433
The work plan with the CPC will do that, and it will be informed

01:39:32.467 --> 01:39:34.200
by feedback from the department heads.

01:39:35.200 --> 01:39:38.867
The CPC does annual reporting to the council, and it holds public

01:39:38.934 --> 01:39:42.867
meetings. The planning division does annual reporting, and we intend to

01:39:42.934 --> 01:39:46.834
stand up a website that will track the status of our policies and

01:39:46.900 --> 01:39:50.300
whether we've achieved them, are they're in progress, how far along are they, and

01:39:50.367 --> 01:39:50.734
so on.

01:39:51.567 --> 01:39:54.867
And then, of course, the city manager and the council process includes annual goal

01:39:54.967 --> 01:39:58.800
setting, where I expect we'll be having some conversations about where's the right

01:39:58.800 --> 01:40:01.600
place to put emphasis in that given year.

01:40:01.600 --> 01:40:05.400
And then, of course, our budget and CIP process will reflect the initiatives that

01:40:05.433 --> 01:40:08.266
we want to kick off, especially when they require funding.

01:40:09.667 --> 01:40:13.567
Every other department also requires an annual report and

01:40:13.634 --> 01:40:16.266
will share their thoughts on initiatives they have on the go.

01:40:18.367 --> 01:40:22.367
So tonight, I think what we've presented to the council for consideration

01:40:22.400 --> 01:40:26.100
is a balanced plan that responds to your

01:40:26.166 --> 01:40:28.100
June 2025 feedback,

01:40:29.333 --> 01:40:33.266
that responds to the community feedback that we've heard, that puts checks

01:40:33.300 --> 01:40:37.166
and balances in the appropriate places where we're sensitive, but

01:40:37.367 --> 01:40:41.200
also allows us to reach and strive for the things that we want to go after in the

01:40:41.266 --> 01:40:45.200
next 15 years. The council's options tonight, I think as the

01:40:45.266 --> 01:40:48.967
city manager's covered in his position paper, are to

01:40:49.000 --> 01:40:52.667
adopt, adopt with revisions, continue the meeting

01:40:52.967 --> 01:40:56.800
with amendments or guidance for amendments, and certainly the CPC is

01:40:56.834 --> 01:41:00.700
available if there are more complex topics to tackle to come back

01:41:00.734 --> 01:41:01.100
to you

01:41:02.200 --> 01:41:03.500
with more refined language.

01:41:04.300 --> 01:41:08.000
I'm happy to take any questions and dive deeper into any of these

01:41:08.033 --> 01:41:10.367
topics, but I think I'll stop it there.

01:41:12.166 --> 01:41:12.734
Thank you.

01:41:13.600 --> 01:41:14.233
Did you want to say something?

01:41:14.266 --> 01:41:15.367
I'm just going to wrap things up. Yeah.

01:41:16.367 --> 01:41:18.667
So thanks, Milan. So those are the council options.

01:41:18.700 --> 01:41:19.767
Thanks for covering those, Milan.

01:41:20.667 --> 01:41:22.667
Staff does recommend adoption of the plan.

01:41:22.667 --> 01:41:26.433
We do have, and we've provided input throughout the process about

01:41:26.734 --> 01:41:27.500
some things that

01:41:28.867 --> 01:41:32.667
we'd rather maybe see tweaked or not in there or

01:41:32.734 --> 01:41:36.433
that didn't make it in there. But we also, at this point, respect the

01:41:36.467 --> 01:41:40.133
process that things have gone through, and so we do recommend council approval.

01:41:40.533 --> 01:41:43.200
But you have those four options here tonight

01:41:44.266 --> 01:41:44.934
in front of you,

01:41:46.467 --> 01:41:49.900
ranging from if you feel a plan can be adopted as is all the way

01:41:49.967 --> 01:41:53.900
to it's nowhere near close to ready, so send it back to the committee to do more

01:41:53.934 --> 01:41:54.700
sausage making.

01:41:56.467 --> 01:41:59.900
Okay. Thank you very much, Scott. Thank you,

01:41:59.967 --> 01:42:03.800
Milan. And I was going to say we're going to move to

01:42:03.834 --> 01:42:07.767
council questions. So Councilor Pride, looks like you're starting

01:42:07.800 --> 01:42:08.500
us off.

01:42:08.533 --> 01:42:11.700
Thank you. And Milan, thank you for that great presentation.

01:42:11.734 --> 01:42:15.500
And Chair Kunin, thank you for those thanks to all the

01:42:15.500 --> 01:42:18.667
people, because it sounds like a lot of work went into this plan.

01:42:19.166 --> 01:42:21.100
In fact, I know a lot of work went into this plan.

01:42:22.166 --> 01:42:25.900
But I will start with questions, starting with maybe the one nearest

01:42:25.967 --> 01:42:28.166
dearest to my heart, probably the most important question.

01:42:28.900 --> 01:42:32.600
For K Lupa 16, if the Hearts of Pine were to purchase the Hill Street

01:42:32.767 --> 01:42:36.700
tank farm, would they be allowed under this plan to build their stadium

01:42:36.734 --> 01:42:38.233
there so that I could walk to games?

01:42:42.100 --> 01:42:45.867
We can look up the K Lupas policy specifically.

01:42:45.900 --> 01:42:46.400
I have them up.

01:42:47.634 --> 01:42:51.166
That area does allow and really focuses on

01:42:51.400 --> 01:42:54.800
residential, but it does allow compatible types of activities.

01:42:55.166 --> 01:42:59.133
So recreational indoor soccer field would be allowed.

01:42:59.800 --> 01:43:03.700
But a large scale commercial soccer facility end to

01:43:03.767 --> 01:43:06.500
end would not be supported. It would have to be a neighborhood.

01:43:07.000 --> 01:43:08.734
Okay. Might have to make a change there.

01:43:09.400 --> 01:43:12.133
So bringing us back to reality,

01:43:13.300 --> 01:43:15.600
K Lupa 22 and 23, I know a

01:43:16.500 --> 01:43:17.867
lot of people have that on their mind.

01:43:18.900 --> 01:43:22.567
Would the comprehensive plan as written, keep the

01:43:22.667 --> 01:43:26.500
city from designating a buffer zone between the

01:43:28.066 --> 01:43:31.634
Bug Light Park and further into those K Lupas

01:43:32.066 --> 01:43:32.700
as park

01:43:33.500 --> 01:43:33.867
zone?

01:43:35.367 --> 01:43:39.066
No. The policies would be supportive of that approach if you wanted to see it

01:43:39.100 --> 01:43:39.834
through zoning.

01:43:39.900 --> 01:43:43.567
Does it need to be in the comprehensive plan for the city to do that?

01:43:43.634 --> 01:43:43.700
No.

01:43:44.066 --> 01:43:44.233
Would you-

01:43:44.367 --> 01:43:45.667
Well, it is in the plan.

01:43:45.700 --> 01:43:46.166
Right. Okay.

01:43:46.300 --> 01:43:46.400
Yeah.

01:43:47.033 --> 01:43:47.266
Fair.

01:43:48.500 --> 01:43:52.133
Would you expect to see something like that in a comprehensive plan if there was

01:43:52.200 --> 01:43:54.800
a serious effort to do that?

01:43:57.767 --> 01:43:58.433
So we

01:43:59.533 --> 01:44:03.300
presented a matrix to the comprehensive plan committee, I think two

01:44:03.367 --> 01:44:04.066
meetings ago,

01:44:04.967 --> 01:44:08.967
two of their meetings ago.And what the matrix tried to do,

01:44:09.000 --> 01:44:10.800
maybe it was a little earlier than that, actually.

01:44:10.800 --> 01:44:14.767
What the matrix tried to do was lay out the policy options for some of the

01:44:14.800 --> 01:44:16.300
community conversations that were happening.

01:44:16.834 --> 01:44:20.700
And at the time, we understood groups like SOS were exploring

01:44:20.967 --> 01:44:23.600
an open space acquisition out there, a substantial one.

01:44:24.200 --> 01:44:27.700
And that's admirable goal. Virtually all of their

01:44:27.767 --> 01:44:30.400
goals are supported by the policies in this plan.

01:44:30.467 --> 01:44:34.000
The one area where the plan would have a

01:44:34.066 --> 01:44:37.934
limitation is if the expectation was that

01:44:37.967 --> 01:44:41.800
the city was purchasing all of the property in K

01:44:41.834 --> 01:44:44.266
LUPA 21 and 22. At that point,

01:44:45.333 --> 01:44:49.133
that's an action of the city council that would undermine some of the other goals

01:44:49.166 --> 01:44:52.767
that were put into K LUPA 21 and 22. And so our

01:44:52.800 --> 01:44:56.800
process when the city is contemplating acquisition is that you consult

01:44:56.834 --> 01:44:59.567
with the Open Space Acquisition Committee and the Planning Board.

01:45:00.100 --> 01:45:03.967
The Planning Board's role is to tell you whether there's policy in the plan

01:45:04.033 --> 01:45:07.300
that does not support us acquiring open space

01:45:07.333 --> 01:45:11.000
there. And I would suspect the Planning Board would say

01:45:11.000 --> 01:45:14.634
100% acquisition from everywhere by the city for a city

01:45:14.700 --> 01:45:17.700
park is not striking the balance that this plan calls for.

01:45:17.767 --> 01:45:21.667
So save that one scenario of total

01:45:21.734 --> 01:45:23.266
park acquisition everywhere,

01:45:24.900 --> 01:45:27.166
all other versions of it are possible.

01:45:27.767 --> 01:45:31.467
And if it was a private party buying all of the

01:45:31.567 --> 01:45:35.100
land and keeping it as a park, whether it's a nonprofit or some other

01:45:35.333 --> 01:45:36.467
partnership, they could do that

01:45:37.667 --> 01:45:40.634
all day long because it's private property, and they can just keep it as park and

01:45:40.700 --> 01:45:43.834
allow public access. But it would have to be just that city

01:45:44.500 --> 01:45:45.634
purchase scenario.

01:45:45.767 --> 01:45:49.367
I guess my question is, generally speaking, do you see

01:45:49.400 --> 01:45:52.867
that kind of granularity in a

01:45:53.200 --> 01:45:57.066
comprehensive plan in general? If a city is looking

01:45:57.133 --> 01:46:00.500
at creating a buffer like that or a park that's a part of a K LUPA

01:46:00.533 --> 01:46:03.767
or whatever they call them in other comp plans,

01:46:04.834 --> 01:46:08.700
do they generally have that plan sketched right out in detail in the comp

01:46:08.767 --> 01:46:09.033
plan?

01:46:10.367 --> 01:46:14.266
Yes and no. The no is relevant to our context.

01:46:14.567 --> 01:46:18.100
South Portland, complex community, multiple things going on,

01:46:18.367 --> 01:46:22.233
multiple issues to tackle. It's not common for

01:46:22.300 --> 01:46:26.166
us to be writing a site plan for an open space acquisition

01:46:26.200 --> 01:46:30.166
within a comp plan. But where in Maine, most comp plans

01:46:30.266 --> 01:46:34.200
are handling small communities where literally all they're doing for the

01:46:34.233 --> 01:46:37.900
next 10 years is trying to purchase that little piece of city forest, and

01:46:38.066 --> 01:46:41.300
that's what they spend all their time talking about, and it's important to them.

01:46:41.967 --> 01:46:42.800
So if you

01:46:43.700 --> 01:46:47.166
took all the comp plans in the main, you probably would find several that talk

01:46:47.166 --> 01:46:50.800
specifically about one land acquisition from one family, and that's important.

01:46:52.133 --> 01:46:55.467
But for us, for our context, for what we're trying to balance and manage,

01:46:56.066 --> 01:46:56.667
no.

01:46:56.700 --> 01:47:00.500
Okay. And maybe for communities of similar size doing similar kind of

01:47:00.567 --> 01:47:00.834
planning?

01:47:01.600 --> 01:47:01.700
Yeah.

01:47:02.066 --> 01:47:02.767
Yeah.

01:47:04.667 --> 01:47:08.567
The other piece that I really want to-- and there's a few more questions,

01:47:08.600 --> 01:47:11.634
so I apologize, councilors. But with the

01:47:14.700 --> 01:47:18.533
methodology by which any given developer were to

01:47:19.333 --> 01:47:23.300
suggest residential and then have to figure out the health

01:47:23.367 --> 01:47:25.700
impacts of VOCs and

01:47:26.567 --> 01:47:29.567
if they're close to any kind of tank farm facility

01:47:31.100 --> 01:47:31.900
or any tanks,

01:47:34.200 --> 01:47:35.767
does the council get to prescribe that?

01:47:36.367 --> 01:47:38.834
Could we make a zoning

01:47:39.734 --> 01:47:41.033
amendment that states that

01:47:42.834 --> 01:47:45.734
any developer would have to submit their

01:47:46.200 --> 01:47:49.767
data or allow the Sierra Club to come in,

01:47:50.000 --> 01:47:53.634
collect their sample, send it to a lab that the Sierra Club trusted,

01:47:53.967 --> 01:47:56.166
and then the Sierra Club would report to us whether

01:47:57.200 --> 01:47:59.367
they would be allowed to develop residential in the area.

01:47:59.467 --> 01:48:01.634
Could we create a zoning amendment like that?

01:48:05.200 --> 01:48:09.033
Yes, you can create a zoning standard that

01:48:09.066 --> 01:48:13.000
says in order to say yes to any development, the planning board has to

01:48:13.166 --> 01:48:13.467
see

01:48:14.467 --> 01:48:18.200
very prescriptive kinds of data. You can assign that yourself.

01:48:18.300 --> 01:48:22.100
If you wanted to defer the issue of getting that data to the

01:48:22.133 --> 01:48:24.834
private side of things, that's totally doable,

01:48:26.000 --> 01:48:29.066
and we can certainly do that. Because the community has been doing so much with

01:48:29.066 --> 01:48:31.367
data collection and analyzing it on our own,

01:48:32.367 --> 01:48:36.266
the assumption here is that we would be learning from that and then presenting

01:48:36.266 --> 01:48:38.400
the city council with data, yay or nay.

01:48:38.500 --> 01:48:42.500
This feels like we have enough to say it's safe, and therefore you're seeing

01:48:42.533 --> 01:48:45.834
an ordinance that allows residential, or we don't have enough

01:48:46.867 --> 01:48:50.467
staff at this point is not in a position of saying, "You clearly have an option

01:48:50.500 --> 01:48:54.467
to approve this," or we see a scenario where council can make the

01:48:54.500 --> 01:48:57.934
finding. So you can approach it any way you want.

01:48:58.800 --> 01:49:02.700
The one thing you are certainly protected from is a developer coming forward

01:49:02.734 --> 01:49:06.567
and saying, "There's policy. It says residential is okay

01:49:06.667 --> 01:49:10.634
here, and here's a study from whatever saying

01:49:11.100 --> 01:49:12.500
the VOCs are not a concern."

01:49:13.433 --> 01:49:17.000
Council is not obligated, has no requirement

01:49:17.066 --> 01:49:20.600
whatsoever to accept a residential proposal, whether it's from staff

01:49:21.133 --> 01:49:24.367
or whether it's from a state agency or whether it's from a private developer.

01:49:24.934 --> 01:49:28.900
And you set the terms on what is acceptable for you to make the finding, and

01:49:29.000 --> 01:49:32.266
five of you have to do that, not your normal majority.

01:49:34.000 --> 01:49:35.467
And I guess one sort of really

01:49:36.800 --> 01:49:40.166
60,000 foot view question, sort of in relation to that,

01:49:40.767 --> 01:49:44.467
if say that we put in the comp plan or the comp plan was accepted by council

01:49:44.533 --> 01:49:48.066
saying, "No residential development here," would that

01:49:48.100 --> 01:49:51.300
restrict a council from coming in and rezoning and saying, "You know what?

01:49:51.834 --> 01:49:53.467
We actually do want residential here"?

01:49:55.533 --> 01:49:57.934
One council cannot bind another council

01:49:58.767 --> 01:50:00.500
just strictly through policy.

01:50:01.367 --> 01:50:01.600
And that

01:50:02.467 --> 01:50:06.433
rolls way back up. A future council can delete this comp plan and write a new

01:50:06.467 --> 01:50:08.367
one and accept a whole new one with whole new policies.

01:50:08.367 --> 01:50:12.200
That's technically possible.So it would be

01:50:12.266 --> 01:50:15.634
doing measures like what you did, I think someone mentioned it in public comment,

01:50:15.834 --> 01:50:19.834
amending the charter and putting in more checks and balances on certain things

01:50:19.834 --> 01:50:23.200
that you want to protect. In those ways, you can establish a legacy.

01:50:24.367 --> 01:50:25.800
But the

01:50:27.033 --> 01:50:30.567
future council is the elected government of the people, and if they have a

01:50:30.600 --> 01:50:31.834
different direction they want to take,

01:50:32.667 --> 01:50:34.600
they have some opportunity to do that.

01:50:34.600 --> 01:50:38.433
So in that vein where zoning requires a super majority to

01:50:38.467 --> 01:50:42.433
change, changing the zoning after we've passed a comp plan would be the

01:50:43.667 --> 01:50:46.800
better area to make change that was more difficult for a future council to

01:50:46.834 --> 01:50:48.133
reverse?

01:50:48.166 --> 01:50:50.467
More difficult and more specific. Yeah.

01:50:55.800 --> 01:50:59.634
So for the last comp plan, we had several areas that

01:50:59.700 --> 01:51:03.600
got master plans. Like Knightville got a master plan, Cash Corner got a

01:51:03.634 --> 01:51:07.500
master plan. And so I noticed we kind of skipped the

01:51:07.500 --> 01:51:11.300
master plan step on your timeline, because I know not everybody gets

01:51:11.367 --> 01:51:12.033
one.

01:51:13.000 --> 01:51:15.834
But master planning is part of the plan after the

01:51:17.734 --> 01:51:19.133
comp plan is passed, correct?

01:51:20.734 --> 01:51:24.567
For areas where, yeah, a master plan has a specific

01:51:24.634 --> 01:51:27.934
role to play. For example, we call out master plans

01:51:28.433 --> 01:51:32.233
in tank farm properties, where we don't see that the

01:51:32.767 --> 01:51:35.600
applicant or property owner is wanting to do something different.

01:51:36.166 --> 01:51:39.900
Maybe what we need to do is create a master plan and think

01:51:39.967 --> 01:51:43.634
about all the barriers that they have and all the possible levers that the city

01:51:43.667 --> 01:51:47.567
council and the city can move in order to encourage them in a different direction.

01:51:47.967 --> 01:51:49.700
We need to spend some focus time dealing with that.

01:51:50.000 --> 01:51:53.300
Just as an example, Main Mall is one we're looking at right now.

01:51:53.734 --> 01:51:56.934
And what we're learning, one of the reasons why we haven't seen infill development

01:51:57.000 --> 01:52:00.900
in the parking lot areas is because there's major infrastructure there and

01:52:01.066 --> 01:52:03.467
huge geotechnical problems with the quality of the soil.

01:52:03.934 --> 01:52:05.667
You just can't support buildings out there.

01:52:06.500 --> 01:52:09.000
It's exorbitantly expensive to build at the mall.

01:52:09.467 --> 01:52:13.000
And so one of the things we've advocated for, and you'll hear more about at the

01:52:13.033 --> 01:52:16.200
time of the Texas Instruments TIF, for instance, is that maybe we can use some of

01:52:16.233 --> 01:52:19.533
that money to focus master planning and try to understand those areas

01:52:20.033 --> 01:52:23.834
better and maybe make some strategic investments to help the private market do

01:52:23.867 --> 01:52:27.800
something that we want it to do. So master plans have that purpose, which allows us

01:52:27.834 --> 01:52:31.767
to dive at a deeper level and understand and partner with people to better see

01:52:31.834 --> 01:52:32.634
the conditions there.

01:52:34.000 --> 01:52:37.700
Personally, I prefer master plans when they start to get at some of that

01:52:37.767 --> 01:52:41.634
infrastructure problem, and really figure out what the solutions are, and

01:52:41.734 --> 01:52:45.634
often those require investments or figuring out how we

01:52:45.700 --> 01:52:48.233
combine investments and find sources of funding.

01:52:48.667 --> 01:52:52.100
Not all areas need that. So it will be up to the

01:52:52.133 --> 01:52:53.834
CPC and obviously the council.

01:52:54.734 --> 01:52:57.734
We come to you through workshops and we'll say, "Look, I think we're ready for just

01:52:57.834 --> 01:53:01.233
rezoning this area. We don't need to study X, Y, and Z.

01:53:02.033 --> 01:53:04.734
But in this area, I think we need to get a master plan approach.

01:53:04.800 --> 01:53:08.000
We can do it in-house. We need funding to do it if we need support," that kind of

01:53:08.033 --> 01:53:10.233
thing. All those conversations would happen with you.

01:53:10.367 --> 01:53:13.533
Yeah. So ultimately, the council could designate

01:53:13.867 --> 01:53:17.767
a group of neighbors in the shipyard area to do a master plan for that

01:53:17.834 --> 01:53:21.667
area that would also have an effect on zoning at a more granular level

01:53:21.767 --> 01:53:23.333
than the comprehensive plan does.

01:53:24.800 --> 01:53:28.433
So something like what we did with O'Neil Street, for instance, we created a

01:53:28.500 --> 01:53:32.066
committee. It included community members in that vicinity to help

01:53:32.100 --> 01:53:35.934
inform the vision, what kinds of things we were looking for that then

01:53:36.000 --> 01:53:39.800
led to an RFP process and so on. So yeah, there's various ways that

01:53:39.867 --> 01:53:43.834
the council can choose to form a body to be a sounding board or work with staff

01:53:43.867 --> 01:53:44.967
or guide the process.

01:53:45.500 --> 01:53:46.600
And all of that would be--

01:53:47.834 --> 01:53:51.667
This comp plan obviously allows for all of that, the way that it's currently

01:53:51.767 --> 01:53:52.066
stated.

01:53:52.867 --> 01:53:53.700
Yeah. Yes.

01:53:54.033 --> 01:53:54.233
All right.

01:53:55.133 --> 01:53:56.634
That's all I have for questions. Thank you.

01:53:56.700 --> 01:53:59.533
Thank you very much, Councilor Pride. Do we have more questions?

01:53:59.567 --> 01:54:02.767
And also, I just want to remind folks who might accidentally be looking at that

01:54:02.800 --> 01:54:05.900
clock, good news, it's 8:31.

01:54:09.000 --> 01:54:10.433
Councilor Walker.

01:54:10.734 --> 01:54:14.066
So I have a number of questions, but I'll just do a couple at a time, and so that

01:54:14.133 --> 01:54:16.266
other people get a chance to ask their questions, too.

01:54:17.266 --> 01:54:18.000
I'll start with two.

01:54:18.834 --> 01:54:21.967
One is, ask them both, and you can decide which one you want to answer first.

01:54:22.767 --> 01:54:25.834
One, I wanted to ask what happened with K Lupa 27.

01:54:26.667 --> 01:54:30.533
Because it seems like it was something that solved for

01:54:30.734 --> 01:54:34.533
many things, and I understand that it was the will of the Comp Plan committee for

01:54:34.533 --> 01:54:36.834
it to go away, but if you could maybe talk a little bit about that.

01:54:37.200 --> 01:54:39.433
And then my second one that I wanted to talk about

01:54:40.533 --> 01:54:43.634
is K Lupa 1, or is it K Lupa 1?

01:54:46.166 --> 01:54:49.900
It's the height requirements around at Main Mall, how there is no height

01:54:49.934 --> 01:54:52.900
requirement around the Main Mall, and I just have some concerns about that, and

01:54:52.934 --> 01:54:54.166
just wanted to talk about that, too.

01:54:54.834 --> 01:54:55.066
Sure.

01:54:57.000 --> 01:54:59.333
So K Lupa 27 was a

01:55:01.567 --> 01:55:05.500
proposal that staff put together after we took a

01:55:05.533 --> 01:55:09.400
little break from CPC meetings so that we could meet with individual CPC members

01:55:09.433 --> 01:55:12.400
and try to understand how they were seeing the issues in that area.

01:55:13.600 --> 01:55:16.467
And then we came back with what seemed to be

01:55:17.667 --> 01:55:21.634
reflecting many of the things we heard, and then the CPC digested that

01:55:21.634 --> 01:55:22.367
and considered it.

01:55:23.767 --> 01:55:27.133
It led to what you see on the screen here,

01:55:28.133 --> 01:55:30.533
and what's proposed tonight, the 21 and 22.

01:55:30.934 --> 01:55:34.634
The big difference with K Lupa 27 is that we connected these two

01:55:35.967 --> 01:55:39.500
kind of through this area, and in part, that was really looking at

01:55:39.834 --> 01:55:42.400
distance from the water and adjacencies to Bug Light Park.

01:55:43.533 --> 01:55:47.367
But apart from shifting the boundary to allow

01:55:47.433 --> 01:55:51.333
more of this area, which is in common ownership, and also just

01:55:51.400 --> 01:55:52.934
organized better in that sense,

01:55:54.533 --> 01:55:58.333
I think K Lupa 27, a lot of it is reflected in the framework of

01:55:58.500 --> 01:56:00.066
21 and 22.

01:56:01.767 --> 01:56:05.567
But really, the K Lupa 22 Area B,

01:56:05.667 --> 01:56:09.467
which is this one here, is the one where there was discussion about

01:56:09.667 --> 01:56:13.133
what could that be, in part because it has that frontage on Madison.

01:56:13.166 --> 01:56:16.433
It's the connection intoBug Light Park.

01:56:17.133 --> 01:56:19.467
It already has buildings and development on it.

01:56:20.533 --> 01:56:24.467
They've felt and moved it into more of the Kaloopa 22

01:56:24.533 --> 01:56:24.934
framework.

01:56:25.467 --> 01:56:26.533
Okay. Thank you.

01:56:28.166 --> 01:56:30.567
And then the height. So,

01:56:32.367 --> 01:56:34.700
this is one designation I think that it

01:56:36.600 --> 01:56:37.166
was pretty

01:56:38.433 --> 01:56:41.467
clean consensus all the way through when we were introducing that.

01:56:41.600 --> 01:56:42.934
I think the

01:56:44.033 --> 01:56:47.133
only way I would answer your question is to say that the committee, including

01:56:47.166 --> 01:56:48.634
through public comment,

01:56:49.567 --> 01:56:53.467
felt really bold about the mall and what it could do and what it

01:56:53.467 --> 01:56:57.133
should be allowed to do, and the city did not want to establish height as

01:56:57.266 --> 01:57:00.767
any barrier. One of the things we did at the mall and Sable Oaks

01:57:01.200 --> 01:57:02.033
was say, not only

01:57:02.967 --> 01:57:06.500
for the mall, the height can go unlimited, but we also established a minimum, not a

01:57:06.533 --> 01:57:10.300
maximum density. And so one of the things that the committee, I think,

01:57:13.166 --> 01:57:17.066
accepted or found comfortable was the idea that, well, look,

01:57:17.133 --> 01:57:19.133
if we want them to strive for as much as possible,

01:57:20.266 --> 01:57:23.767
why would we set up a scenario where maybe we say it's six stories, maybe we say

01:57:23.834 --> 01:57:27.800
it's eight stories, and then someone needs eight stories plus a rooftop area that

01:57:27.867 --> 01:57:29.667
the fire department would deem a story.

01:57:30.300 --> 01:57:34.133
Or eight stories, but they need nine if they want to make the affordable

01:57:34.166 --> 01:57:38.033
housing component viable. Or those geotechnical costs

01:57:38.133 --> 01:57:42.100
are way higher than we anticipated, and again, they want to go just that

01:57:42.166 --> 01:57:46.100
one story. So why establish height as an

01:57:46.166 --> 01:57:50.100
overall limit? We did factor whether the airport would be a concern from

01:57:50.133 --> 01:57:52.767
a safety perspective. It's not. It's too far away.

01:57:53.834 --> 01:57:54.934
So really, I think the

01:57:55.767 --> 01:57:57.166
committee felt that

01:57:58.266 --> 01:58:02.166
development proposals are not going to be bringing on something that

01:58:02.300 --> 01:58:05.834
is wild for the main context

01:58:06.133 --> 01:58:09.867
out at the mall area. There are just some limitations just in the market

01:58:10.133 --> 01:58:11.433
to getting something like that.

01:58:13.333 --> 01:58:16.266
And we didn't have a lot of debate on that issue.

01:58:16.600 --> 01:58:17.934
People felt pretty comfortable with it.

01:58:18.000 --> 01:58:21.467
So I don't know that there's a scientific answer to why we picked that,

01:58:22.166 --> 01:58:24.533
but those are some of the things that fed into it.

01:58:25.367 --> 01:58:29.133
And so just to follow up with that, is there zoning that exists now

01:58:29.166 --> 01:58:33.100
that needs to be changed for that Kaloopa, not just for the

01:58:33.133 --> 01:58:35.100
height requirement, but for that Kaloopa in general?

01:58:35.900 --> 01:58:36.867
Oh yeah, big time.

01:58:37.734 --> 01:58:41.233
The 2012 plan wanted to protect the

01:58:41.300 --> 01:58:44.066
mall and its vibrancy by

01:58:44.867 --> 01:58:46.667
sheltering it from anything else.

01:58:46.767 --> 01:58:50.667
And so the mall does not allow all of the things that today we

01:58:50.667 --> 01:58:54.433
understand an evolving mall would need and want, and what frankly, the

01:58:54.467 --> 01:58:57.300
mall owners have been talking to us about being very interested in.

01:58:58.900 --> 01:59:00.767
It's just a little bit dated thinking.

01:59:01.767 --> 01:59:05.634
And so we need to add residential. We need to modify parking

01:59:05.734 --> 01:59:09.000
standards. We need to allow increased development

01:59:09.667 --> 01:59:13.467
in bulk and form. So setbacks are really wide out there because the way they

01:59:13.533 --> 01:59:17.300
viewed it is those major roads, we want volume

01:59:17.433 --> 01:59:19.834
going through there, and we want nothing obstructing it.

01:59:20.433 --> 01:59:21.033
And,

01:59:21.867 --> 01:59:25.567
what that takes away is things like a high-quality

01:59:25.600 --> 01:59:29.100
pedestrian environment. It's kind of hostile to pedestrians out there.

01:59:29.133 --> 01:59:31.634
If you want to live out there, you're kind of living in an airport.

01:59:32.934 --> 01:59:36.734
And so there's a variety of things that we need to revamp for the mall to start

01:59:36.767 --> 01:59:39.333
feeling more like a mixed-use node.

01:59:40.533 --> 01:59:43.634
And this isn't planners talking in a vacuum.

01:59:43.767 --> 01:59:47.133
This is what we've been hearing from the development community as to what they

01:59:47.166 --> 01:59:49.667
would feel is supportive of the type of direction they want to take.

01:59:50.000 --> 01:59:53.400
Okay. And so much like your answer to Councilor Pride, if we wanted to put

01:59:53.433 --> 01:59:57.300
some restraints on height, we could do that through zoning as we're moving through

01:59:57.300 --> 01:59:57.767
this process.

01:59:57.934 --> 01:59:58.533
Absolutely, yeah.

01:59:58.967 --> 02:00:01.233
Thank you. And just Mayor would ask if we can

02:00:02.033 --> 02:00:03.367
come back for more questions.

02:00:03.734 --> 02:00:07.200
Yes. I think we should have a thorough amount of councilor

02:00:07.300 --> 02:00:10.767
questions. We also would like to move to public

02:00:10.900 --> 02:00:13.800
comment because we have a lot of members of the public here.

02:00:15.133 --> 02:00:18.834
I don't think we can say that when we get to

02:00:19.033 --> 02:00:22.900
council discussion that no councilor can ever ask a

02:00:22.967 --> 02:00:26.900
question again. I think questions will also be allowed, but don't feel shy

02:00:26.967 --> 02:00:29.867
right now. Other questions from councilors?

02:00:32.567 --> 02:00:36.266
I don't see any on the left, and now I'll turn to the right.

02:00:36.467 --> 02:00:38.333
Questions from councilors? I don't see.

02:00:38.700 --> 02:00:42.567
Councilor Walker, I think you can continue then, and I have a

02:00:42.634 --> 02:00:43.367
few myself.

02:00:43.967 --> 02:00:45.667
Does Councilor Scott have any questions before I continue?

02:00:45.700 --> 02:00:47.867
Oh, Councilor Scott. Hello.

02:00:49.433 --> 02:00:51.533
Councilor Scott, do you have questions?

02:00:53.300 --> 02:00:53.600
Not yet.

02:00:53.667 --> 02:00:53.967
Thank you.

02:00:54.934 --> 02:00:55.934
Thank you so much.

02:00:56.133 --> 02:00:56.300
Okay.

02:00:56.367 --> 02:00:57.100
All right, Councilor Walker.

02:00:57.133 --> 02:00:57.900
I'm going to keep going then.

02:00:58.734 --> 02:01:02.567
So in terms of the housing initiatives that are baked into the

02:01:02.634 --> 02:01:06.300
comp plan in general, I know many of us are here to talk about the eastern

02:01:06.333 --> 02:01:09.467
waterfront, but as a resident of the west side of the city, it is something that I

02:01:09.533 --> 02:01:11.433
have been paying close attention to as well.

02:01:12.533 --> 02:01:15.867
How do we ensure that growth isn't just made up of rental units?

02:01:16.066 --> 02:01:16.367
So

02:01:17.166 --> 02:01:18.734
what I mean by that is

02:01:19.634 --> 02:01:21.634
I have grown concern over the last

02:01:22.767 --> 02:01:26.066
little while when I think about the growth on the west side of the city, which is

02:01:26.100 --> 02:01:29.834
where the majority of the community feels encouraged to put

02:01:29.900 --> 02:01:33.700
growth. The growth over the last 10 years has been primarily rental units,

02:01:34.133 --> 02:01:37.767
and so I worry about the dichotomy that we're setting up between the east side of

02:01:37.767 --> 02:01:41.467
the city where home ownership is encouraged, and the west side of the city, if we

02:01:41.467 --> 02:01:44.166
continue on this path, where it's primarily rental units.

02:01:44.367 --> 02:01:48.266
So where in the housing initiatives and the comp

02:01:48.300 --> 02:01:52.100
plan do we address things like that, or can we through the comp plan?

02:01:53.166 --> 02:01:54.233
We certainly can.

02:01:55.734 --> 02:01:59.300
So just because I have the map up here, the eastern waterfront has this policy, and

02:01:59.367 --> 02:02:02.200
we move the same policy over into Sable Oaks,

02:02:03.133 --> 02:02:06.667
and in the mall area, that housing has to be diverse.

02:02:06.767 --> 02:02:10.133
It can't just be one type, one bedroom,

02:02:11.100 --> 02:02:14.967
high quality luxury apartments for empty nesters or something like that,

02:02:15.000 --> 02:02:17.166
which the market has ample desire

02:02:17.200 --> 02:02:21.000
for.So diversity, and we don't

02:02:21.100 --> 02:02:24.734
prescribe, because our housing report found that we have such

02:02:24.934 --> 02:02:28.834
chronic and significant underproduction across the board, that it

02:02:29.266 --> 02:02:33.066
makes no sense for us to be saying things like, "We need to really

02:02:33.133 --> 02:02:34.767
amp up family-sized units."

02:02:35.600 --> 02:02:38.567
Frankly, it's something I was looking for when we commissioned that study is, is

02:02:38.567 --> 02:02:42.000
there a particular housing type that's just underserved, and do we need to help

02:02:42.066 --> 02:02:45.367
it? And it's just grossly underserved in all categories.

02:02:45.400 --> 02:02:48.667
So whatever we get is going to be really beneficial.

02:02:49.467 --> 02:02:51.600
That's not to say that we can't set policy goals.

02:02:52.333 --> 02:02:54.767
And we have done that in key land use policy areas.

02:02:54.834 --> 02:02:57.900
We also have, in the housing theme, which applies

02:02:57.967 --> 02:03:00.767
citywide, a statement to diversify housing.

02:03:01.166 --> 02:03:04.634
We also have a statement to protect existing housing and protect existing

02:03:04.667 --> 02:03:07.967
affordable housing. Something that we haven't really approached, for instance, is

02:03:08.000 --> 02:03:11.967
the gentrification. As you invest in areas, whether it's for housing

02:03:12.000 --> 02:03:15.734
or for non-residential development, wanting to protect the rents

02:03:15.767 --> 02:03:16.100
and

02:03:17.500 --> 02:03:21.433
the ability of existing homes to keep that affordable housing option available for

02:03:21.467 --> 02:03:24.867
folks. So we have that baked into the plan.

02:03:25.667 --> 02:03:28.900
When we get into the zoning for specific areas, if there's a

02:03:28.900 --> 02:03:32.800
sensitivity in one area in particular, and I think your point is

02:03:32.867 --> 02:03:33.567
valid about

02:03:35.867 --> 02:03:39.800
new housing development really leaning onto the rental side because

02:03:39.834 --> 02:03:43.800
it's done at scale, especially when we ask for 20 units per acre minimum,

02:03:44.667 --> 02:03:46.367
are we only going to be getting apartments?

02:03:47.900 --> 02:03:51.867
And so when we see the zoning for the main mall or for the

02:03:51.867 --> 02:03:55.834
Sable Oaks area, that would be the time where we look to see those

02:03:55.867 --> 02:03:59.767
things defined. Sable Oaks does have that diversification goal,

02:03:59.867 --> 02:04:00.934
and the concept plans

02:04:01.867 --> 02:04:05.567
that have already been put forward by the Sable Oaks developers, just as a proof of

02:04:05.634 --> 02:04:09.500
what could go there and how it could get laid out, are a range of

02:04:09.567 --> 02:04:13.433
apartments to cul-de-sac, single-family home type divisions.

02:04:13.500 --> 02:04:13.634
So,

02:04:14.900 --> 02:04:18.867
it's already looking at a range, and we can certainly reinforce that in policy.

02:04:19.166 --> 02:04:19.967
Okay. Thank you.

02:04:20.767 --> 02:04:21.300
Can I keep going?

02:04:22.100 --> 02:04:25.367
Sure, please. You have good questions, and I think people are learning from them.

02:04:25.433 --> 02:04:25.700
Okay.

02:04:26.700 --> 02:04:30.567
So, in terms of the reduced parking requirements, how does that

02:04:30.600 --> 02:04:32.200
play out with our winter parking ban?

02:04:32.400 --> 02:04:35.867
And I'm thinking about specifically over the next two to four years.

02:04:35.900 --> 02:04:39.266
So, we're talking about having one and a half

02:04:39.800 --> 02:04:43.667
spaces or 0.75 spaces, and we want to kind of force the

02:04:43.700 --> 02:04:45.333
reduction of cars.

02:04:45.367 --> 02:04:45.433
Yeah.

02:04:45.467 --> 02:04:48.467
But our transit environment does not support that yet.

02:04:48.533 --> 02:04:50.934
So, how does the winter parking ban line up with that?

02:04:52.166 --> 02:04:54.900
They have to come together. So we,

02:04:55.967 --> 02:04:59.767
especially in more congested areas or more built-out areas like our

02:04:59.800 --> 02:05:00.367
downtown,

02:05:02.266 --> 02:05:05.700
we can't really eliminate the off-street parking

02:05:05.767 --> 02:05:08.767
without having an actual solution to parking.

02:05:09.066 --> 02:05:12.767
One way to approach it is to just remove the parking requirement, allow the

02:05:12.767 --> 02:05:16.000
development to happen. It puts pressure on and makes things

02:05:16.066 --> 02:05:19.066
uncomfortable, and then we get to a point where we want to fix that problem.

02:05:19.934 --> 02:05:23.133
I think that's what you'll hear from the market, from the developers is, "We don't

02:05:23.166 --> 02:05:26.800
really know that it's that bad. Let's just try it out, and we'll see it, and then

02:05:26.867 --> 02:05:28.367
once you get the headaches, then we'll fix it."

02:05:30.467 --> 02:05:32.367
We have no intention of approaching it that way.

02:05:32.467 --> 02:05:35.700
I think we really need to understand all the mechanisms that are there.

02:05:36.600 --> 02:05:40.166
I think our zoning ordinance absolutely has

02:05:42.200 --> 02:05:45.600
a requirement of too much parking in certain scenarios.

02:05:45.700 --> 02:05:49.600
And probably pretty easily, we already have the information, even

02:05:49.667 --> 02:05:52.233
in our community, to say we just don't need that much parking.

02:05:52.600 --> 02:05:56.433
If you look at things like the mall, where the ratio of parking required was

02:05:56.467 --> 02:05:59.100
compliant 30 years, 40 years ago when it got built,

02:06:00.867 --> 02:06:03.967
we definitely don't need that much, and we wouldn't put that number where it was

02:06:04.000 --> 02:06:07.767
then today. So there's certain things we can do without a full-blown study and

02:06:07.800 --> 02:06:09.834
without impacting the winter parking scenario.

02:06:11.567 --> 02:06:15.200
But downtown Mill Creek, for instance, we can't touch parking until we have a

02:06:15.266 --> 02:06:19.100
real solution on that. There is, I think, some

02:06:19.166 --> 02:06:23.000
important analysis that has to be done about how we set up a

02:06:23.066 --> 02:06:26.800
no winter parking ban scenario. I think that's

02:06:26.867 --> 02:06:30.700
probably the lowest investment form of providing parking

02:06:30.767 --> 02:06:34.100
to allow the whole area to become more of what the vision is.

02:06:35.066 --> 02:06:36.834
But that's not to say it's an easy endeavor.

02:06:36.867 --> 02:06:39.300
There's a lot of operational stuff we'll have to do.

02:06:39.333 --> 02:06:42.300
We've had some conversations with our public works director.

02:06:42.333 --> 02:06:46.233
We're talking different equipment, different scheduling, different police

02:06:46.333 --> 02:06:50.066
pricing on illegal parking, parking enforcement in

02:06:50.100 --> 02:06:53.634
general, possibly paid parking metering, and all the administration that goes

02:06:53.667 --> 02:06:57.600
beside that. It's a fraction of the cost of building a garage and

02:06:57.667 --> 02:06:58.967
just opening it up to folks.

02:07:00.834 --> 02:07:03.834
But just because it costs less doesn't mean that it doesn't require

02:07:04.367 --> 02:07:06.767
significant energy to make happen properly.

02:07:07.967 --> 02:07:09.800
So we will have to take those two together.

02:07:10.433 --> 02:07:10.567
Okay.

02:07:11.800 --> 02:07:15.767
Just some technical... Sorry. Just some technical questions, and it sounds

02:07:15.800 --> 02:07:17.266
like you may have answered this earlier.

02:07:17.667 --> 02:07:21.500
With the timelines, the short, the medium, and the long-term timelines, is

02:07:21.567 --> 02:07:23.834
that measured in years, months,

02:07:24.734 --> 02:07:25.867
decade, next comp plan?

02:07:26.700 --> 02:07:30.667
It's over the life of the plan, which is by state certification at 12 years.

02:07:30.700 --> 02:07:32.800
So think of it in four-year chunks.

02:07:33.233 --> 02:07:33.533
The work-

02:07:34.000 --> 02:07:34.800
So we're at least one to four.

02:07:34.867 --> 02:07:35.200
Yep.

02:07:35.266 --> 02:07:35.800
Okay.

02:07:36.066 --> 02:07:39.200
The work plan that the Comprehensive Plan Committee will define will actually put

02:07:39.200 --> 02:07:43.133
some more refinement to it. It's worth mentioning again that

02:07:43.166 --> 02:07:46.734
the plan is guidance. So, if something

02:07:46.734 --> 02:07:50.600
appears in the medium range, but then it hops up as like a...

02:07:50.834 --> 02:07:54.266
We've got a great opportunity to partner with something where the state's just made

02:07:54.300 --> 02:07:57.400
a bunch of money available for a very particular kind of thing, but we happen to

02:07:57.433 --> 02:07:59.634
have it in a long-term ordinance revision.

02:08:00.367 --> 02:08:03.367
There's no reason why we can't move it up at your discretion.

02:08:04.567 --> 02:08:06.600
Okay. I think I'll pause for now. Thank you.

02:08:06.667 --> 02:08:08.400
Thank you very much, Councilor Welker.

02:08:12.033 --> 02:08:12.033
Oh, Councilor Pride didn't apologize for asking questions. What the heck?Did too.

02:08:12.100 --> 02:08:14.166
Oh, did you? Okay.

02:08:14.300 --> 02:08:18.233
I will ask just a

02:08:18.500 --> 02:08:22.166
couple of things because I think maybe it'd be helpful in advance of public comment

02:08:22.200 --> 02:08:24.467
to get a little clarification. So,

02:08:25.467 --> 02:08:29.300
on the matter of whether unique growth is high growth, we

02:08:29.433 --> 02:08:33.333
do describe on page eight of this plan unique

02:08:33.400 --> 02:08:37.033
high growth zones. And what I'm wondering is,

02:08:37.667 --> 02:08:41.667
is that some sort of edit that needs to be made that they were originally

02:08:41.667 --> 02:08:45.667
thought of as high growth zones before they got renamed

02:08:45.800 --> 02:08:46.300
unique?

02:08:49.667 --> 02:08:53.600
No. I think the section on page eight

02:08:53.634 --> 02:08:56.100
is an executive summary. It's trying to distill-

02:08:57.700 --> 02:08:58.200
Yes, I know that

02:08:58.200 --> 02:08:59.634
... 100 pages into one.

02:09:01.166 --> 02:09:05.133
And in this sense, we use the term unique high

02:09:05.200 --> 02:09:05.600
growth

02:09:08.834 --> 02:09:11.467
in talking the range of scales that's allowed.

02:09:11.533 --> 02:09:15.333
So the specific policy is that CALUPAs can range from

02:09:15.634 --> 02:09:18.700
a full conservation type CALUPA to something that

02:09:19.333 --> 02:09:23.300
allows unique growth to what's there before, and also high growth.

02:09:24.000 --> 02:09:24.033
But-

02:09:24.066 --> 02:09:27.233
So I guess, Milan, I'm sorry to interrupt, but it's going to get late early.

02:09:27.634 --> 02:09:27.667
Yep.

02:09:27.734 --> 02:09:31.367
So, what I'm saying is, if I am a member of the public and I

02:09:31.634 --> 02:09:35.166
say to my neighbor, "Hey, this unique growth zone really means high growth

02:09:36.100 --> 02:09:37.700
because I've read it that way,"

02:09:38.734 --> 02:09:40.133
is that true?

02:09:42.133 --> 02:09:46.000
This statement is true. The Bug Light area

02:09:46.467 --> 02:09:49.233
or Forest City Cemetery, those are

02:09:49.300 --> 02:09:53.166
pure conservation zones. And there are

02:09:53.233 --> 02:09:56.767
zones like Sable Oaks, like Maine Mall, which are

02:09:57.033 --> 02:10:00.033
asking for unique and high growth.

02:10:00.667 --> 02:10:04.567
But not all unique growth areas are also

02:10:04.634 --> 02:10:07.400
seeking high maximum amounts of growth.

02:10:07.767 --> 02:10:11.433
So that would fit somewhere in the range of what these two bookends are trying to

02:10:11.533 --> 02:10:15.467
describe in this summary, that we have something at the extreme conservation end

02:10:15.934 --> 02:10:19.400
and something at the unique high growth end, but we also have everything in

02:10:19.467 --> 02:10:23.367
between, and you need to look at the CALUPA policies for what's actually

02:10:23.400 --> 02:10:25.867
being asked for in each area.

02:10:27.934 --> 02:10:31.867
Okay. I get you now, but I needed you to do all of

02:10:31.967 --> 02:10:34.667
those words to explain to me what it means.

02:10:34.700 --> 02:10:35.066
So I-

02:10:35.100 --> 02:10:39.000
It actually confused me more. If we go to 64 of the actual plan,

02:10:39.033 --> 02:10:42.000
there's nothing listed as unique high growth.

02:10:42.033 --> 02:10:44.333
It's just unique growth. And so I was just-

02:10:45.133 --> 02:10:45.133
Correct.

02:10:45.133 --> 02:10:45.266
Is

02:10:46.233 --> 02:10:47.867
unique growth high growth-

02:10:48.600 --> 02:10:48.767
No

02:10:48.800 --> 02:10:49.834
... on that page? Okay.

02:10:49.934 --> 02:10:53.800
Unique growth is reserved for areas that are going

02:10:53.834 --> 02:10:56.700
to change from what we know them to be today.

02:10:57.767 --> 02:11:01.600
And if what it is today is an undeveloped area, and it

02:11:01.667 --> 02:11:05.133
will be asked to become even low

02:11:05.266 --> 02:11:08.266
density and pure residential, that's a significant change.

02:11:08.266 --> 02:11:11.066
From an open space to a residential, that's big change.

02:11:12.133 --> 02:11:13.634
It will still be called unique growth,

02:11:14.667 --> 02:11:18.500
but it's rural. Our subdivisions out on Outer

02:11:18.600 --> 02:11:22.266
Hyland in 2012 were called growth areas, maximum

02:11:22.367 --> 02:11:24.834
growth areas, and they're single-family detached

02:11:26.000 --> 02:11:29.934
homes on half acre lots because it took Greenfield

02:11:30.200 --> 02:11:34.166
into development. Added roads, sewer, all the public infrastructure

02:11:34.233 --> 02:11:38.033
that we built out there, right? The point of

02:11:38.100 --> 02:11:42.033
unique growth is to say we are not trying to conserve what's

02:11:42.133 --> 02:11:44.767
already there. We're trying to evolve it.

02:11:44.834 --> 02:11:48.734
And so for the Eastern Waterfront, it is unique because it's going from a

02:11:49.166 --> 02:11:52.667
industrial brownfield site that is

02:11:52.734 --> 02:11:56.567
currently mostly gravel and dilapidated industrial

02:11:56.567 --> 02:12:00.066
buildings, and the plan asks for a mix of

02:12:00.066 --> 02:12:03.700
uses, residential in character, which has not ever been there before,

02:12:03.734 --> 02:12:07.700
apart from the worker housing, and significant open space dedication was never

02:12:07.767 --> 02:12:10.066
used for that. That's why it's unique.

02:12:10.967 --> 02:12:14.934
It's different from what it was. The mall is unique

02:12:15.000 --> 02:12:18.767
because it's adding residential and going mixed use, where for 60 years, we

02:12:18.834 --> 02:12:21.867
said, "This will be a transaction business area, and that's it.

02:12:22.266 --> 02:12:25.734
Get everything else out of there. We need to protect it for that." And we are now

02:12:25.767 --> 02:12:29.533
taking a whole new paradigm, and on top of that, we want it to

02:12:29.567 --> 02:12:33.333
be at scale. We want it to be big. So that's unique high

02:12:33.367 --> 02:12:34.934
growth at the mall.

02:12:35.867 --> 02:12:39.834
But what's been a challenge in the 2012 plan is we

02:12:40.000 --> 02:12:43.867
asked for certain areas to grow, and when we

02:12:43.934 --> 02:12:46.634
had community conversations about allowing that growth,

02:12:47.767 --> 02:12:51.634
it never happened, and the implementation didn't happen because what we failed

02:12:51.667 --> 02:12:55.166
to do in the planning timeframe of writing the plan is to say,

02:12:56.133 --> 02:12:59.800
"What kind of change are we actually comfortable with?" People said

02:12:59.867 --> 02:13:03.300
growth, but then they later had conversations about it and said, "Well, no, no, no.

02:13:03.433 --> 02:13:07.367
Not that kind of growth. We want more of this other thing." And so we

02:13:07.433 --> 02:13:11.300
had policy without real guidance and without asking the real questions of do

02:13:11.367 --> 02:13:14.467
we expect change, something different

02:13:14.533 --> 02:13:17.834
today versus 10 years from now if this plan is implemented.

02:13:18.400 --> 02:13:22.133
And so we needed that fourth designation, which is different from the growth

02:13:22.533 --> 02:13:25.000
designation because it's also a transformation.

02:13:25.934 --> 02:13:29.367
Doesn't always mean the scale is being exploded.

02:13:29.800 --> 02:13:33.600
Okay. I guess very different from

02:13:33.634 --> 02:13:37.567
what's been there. Growth would've involved a lot of hyphens and been a

02:13:37.634 --> 02:13:38.734
bit unwieldy.

02:13:39.867 --> 02:13:43.667
But that basically describes it, and sometimes people will think

02:13:43.734 --> 02:13:47.133
of what happens there as high, because it will be, and

02:13:47.200 --> 02:13:48.867
sometimes not so much.

02:13:50.233 --> 02:13:50.667
Okay.

02:13:51.667 --> 02:13:55.000
One other thing that I just wanted some clarification on before we get to public

02:13:55.100 --> 02:13:58.266
comments. Again, page eight, executive summary.

02:13:58.834 --> 02:14:02.266
The plan also encourages the reuse of underutilized

02:14:02.300 --> 02:14:05.333
industrial lands, particularly former

02:14:05.433 --> 02:14:08.867
petroleum-related sites for new opportunities.

02:14:09.233 --> 02:14:13.133
So my question, do we now in South Portland have

02:14:13.233 --> 02:14:17.033
anything that we can legitimately call a former

02:14:17.100 --> 02:14:21.000
petroleum-related sites, or are all of

02:14:21.033 --> 02:14:24.233
the sites fully licensed,

02:14:24.867 --> 02:14:25.333
and

02:14:26.233 --> 02:14:30.000
are all of the tanks licensed to be filled?

02:14:32.634 --> 02:14:36.033
We definitely have former petroleum sites,

02:14:37.767 --> 02:14:41.433
but not at scale. For not whole farms that

02:14:41.467 --> 02:14:43.567
are vacant, obviously.

02:14:44.567 --> 02:14:48.500
I know of one former petroleum tank that is now

02:14:48.533 --> 02:14:51.333
a boat storage, indoor boat storage inside the tank.

02:14:51.934 --> 02:14:53.467
So yes, some sites have been converted.

02:14:53.533 --> 02:14:56.867
We're working with a business that wants to be doing

02:14:57.233 --> 02:15:01.000
offshoreconstruction and engineering

02:15:01.033 --> 02:15:04.300
supplies. So they take out the cranes to build things like wind turbines and

02:15:04.333 --> 02:15:06.967
whatnot. And so it'd be an offshore

02:15:08.367 --> 02:15:12.333
service company that's operating in the footprint of a former tank.

02:15:12.900 --> 02:15:13.567
Mm.

02:15:13.667 --> 02:15:14.967
So yes, we have some sites.

02:15:15.767 --> 02:15:18.567
It's not like the entire inland tank farm, for instance.

02:15:18.900 --> 02:15:20.367
And then there are a whole whack of sites,

02:15:22.200 --> 02:15:25.967
probably approaching 100 to 200 acres, that are owned by

02:15:26.066 --> 02:15:29.400
petroleum companies that may have underground pipe there,

02:15:30.667 --> 02:15:34.467
that aren't being utilized by tanks per se, but they're

02:15:34.500 --> 02:15:36.033
not developed, they're being held,

02:15:37.667 --> 02:15:38.166
and

02:15:39.100 --> 02:15:42.900
could transition to something different than what that current operator might

02:15:43.000 --> 02:15:43.867
have as their business.

02:15:45.300 --> 02:15:45.333
Mm-hmm.

02:15:45.400 --> 02:15:49.100
But they don't have a former above-ground tank that was sitting there.

02:15:49.767 --> 02:15:51.500
All right. I would like to get to public comment.

02:15:51.567 --> 02:15:55.200
So just one quick clarification left over from that is

02:15:55.800 --> 02:15:59.467
what people think of the tanks, what they can see right

02:15:59.533 --> 02:16:02.834
now, are those all licensed so that, for

02:16:02.867 --> 02:16:06.567
example, I don't know what's going to happen and I don't think anybody else does

02:16:06.634 --> 02:16:10.200
with oil and gas internationally.

02:16:10.667 --> 02:16:14.634
Are they licensed so that if someone said it's a matter of national security

02:16:14.734 --> 02:16:17.033
to fill all of them, that they could be?

02:16:18.634 --> 02:16:22.333
I'd maybe defer to Scott, but to my knowledge, all the licensed

02:16:22.433 --> 02:16:26.166
operators continue to maintain their licenses, whether they're

02:16:26.467 --> 02:16:26.867
filling-

02:16:26.900 --> 02:16:27.367
So that's yes?

02:16:28.133 --> 02:16:32.066
Well, maintaining your license is different than keeping the tanks full at all

02:16:32.100 --> 02:16:33.467
times. And so

02:16:34.333 --> 02:16:36.900
my understanding is the tanks are all licensed.

02:16:36.934 --> 02:16:39.066
They can operate at full capacity.

02:16:39.066 --> 02:16:42.266
They are not necessarily all operating at full capacity all the time.

02:16:42.500 --> 02:16:46.400
Right. So you've answered my question. You said they can operate at full capacity.

02:16:46.500 --> 02:16:49.100
All right. I just wanted to get some clarification on that.

02:16:49.200 --> 02:16:51.900
You hear very different things. All right.

02:16:51.934 --> 02:16:55.867
Well, if no councilors have any other questions, I think the moment we've all

02:16:55.900 --> 02:16:58.000
been waiting for is public comment.

02:16:58.567 --> 02:17:02.467
Folks, if you could do a nice little line, that would get it done,

02:17:03.100 --> 02:17:04.900
and we'd really appreciate it.

02:17:13.967 --> 02:17:21.200
There's

02:17:23.533 --> 02:17:25.166
a presentation going on.

02:17:33.900 --> 02:17:35.300
Jessica, you got to switch this.

02:17:35.400 --> 02:17:35.934
Okay.

02:17:37.367 --> 02:17:38.033
Switch this.

02:17:38.166 --> 02:17:38.667
Hang on.

02:17:39.300 --> 02:17:43.266
If I could ask the gentleman in the back to switch our projection to show the time

02:17:43.300 --> 02:17:43.700
for us.

02:17:44.533 --> 02:17:45.800
Thank you.

02:17:47.667 --> 02:17:48.200
That's wise.

02:17:49.500 --> 02:17:49.900
Go.

02:17:55.166 --> 02:17:58.100
Remember, name and the street you live on would be appreciated.

02:17:58.166 --> 02:18:00.500
You don't necessarily have to give your whole address.

02:18:02.867 --> 02:18:03.667
Here we go.

02:18:06.133 --> 02:18:06.533
Leslie Ham.

02:18:07.400 --> 02:18:07.533
Thank you.

02:18:07.567 --> 02:18:11.400
All right. My name is Linda Zemsch, and I live on 225

02:18:11.467 --> 02:18:12.166
High Street.

02:18:15.500 --> 02:18:19.100
What is in the best interest of South

02:18:19.100 --> 02:18:20.400
Portland residents?

02:18:21.734 --> 02:18:25.600
SOS, Save Our Shipyard, believes that our

02:18:25.667 --> 02:18:27.900
history provides the answer.

02:18:29.066 --> 02:18:32.767
In 1941 and 1942, as

02:18:32.867 --> 02:18:36.800
World War II was raging in Europe, two shipyards

02:18:36.867 --> 02:18:40.567
were being constructed, the East Yard near Spring

02:18:40.667 --> 02:18:41.967
Point Lighthouse,

02:18:43.000 --> 02:18:46.767
and the West Yard, where the Sunoco tanks are now located.

02:18:47.567 --> 02:18:50.934
The East and West Yards of South Portland together built

02:18:51.133 --> 02:18:54.867
244 Liberty ships and

02:18:54.967 --> 02:18:58.500
30 ocean ships. They were cargo ships

02:18:58.900 --> 02:19:01.000
that were used to break blockades.

02:19:01.667 --> 02:19:05.200
They came together quickly and then went overseas to

02:19:05.300 --> 02:19:08.500
Europe transporting ammunition, artillery,

02:19:09.200 --> 02:19:11.800
tanks, and sometimes even troops.

02:19:12.400 --> 02:19:16.066
In order to do this, many people had to make sacrifices.

02:19:18.066 --> 02:19:21.867
120 homes of working families were taken through eminent domain.

02:19:22.500 --> 02:19:26.200
Many men went off fighting in the war, so women became part of the

02:19:26.300 --> 02:19:28.600
workforce building these Liberty ships.

02:19:29.467 --> 02:19:31.567
You may have heard of Wendy the Welder.

02:19:32.567 --> 02:19:36.433
Workers came from all over Maine and New Hampshire to work here.

02:19:37.166 --> 02:19:41.066
Between 25,000 and 30,000 people worked at the shipyards.

02:19:41.700 --> 02:19:45.667
South Portland grew so quickly that it received emergency

02:19:45.700 --> 02:19:49.100
funds from the federal government to provide for a dramatic

02:19:49.200 --> 02:19:50.800
increase in population.

02:19:52.066 --> 02:19:56.033
Meanwhile, down the road from the West Yard, where the old ferry

02:19:56.100 --> 02:19:59.567
terminal was located, construction of the

02:19:59.667 --> 02:20:03.166
Portland Pipeline took place. The original

02:20:03.200 --> 02:20:06.867
12-inch Portland Montreal Pipeline headed down Sawyer

02:20:07.000 --> 02:20:10.667
Street and then north to Montreal, where refineries were

02:20:10.667 --> 02:20:14.066
producing gasoline and aviation fuel for the Allied

02:20:14.133 --> 02:20:14.700
Forces.

02:20:15.600 --> 02:20:17.200
The new overland pipe

02:20:18.567 --> 02:20:22.367
solved the problem of the St. Lawrence River icing over,

02:20:22.500 --> 02:20:26.467
which closed off winter deliveries, and it saved lives

02:20:26.500 --> 02:20:30.200
and ships that had been victims of U-boat attacks.

02:20:30.967 --> 02:20:34.400
Today, a park named after the lighthouse,

02:20:34.800 --> 02:20:38.567
Bug Light Park, allows residents and visitors to view

02:20:38.634 --> 02:20:38.834
the-

02:20:39.834 --> 02:20:40.667
30 seconds

02:20:40.734 --> 02:20:44.433
... to view the lighthouse up close while memorializing the

02:20:44.567 --> 02:20:46.500
shipbuilding efforts of World War II.

02:20:47.467 --> 02:20:50.834
And so the

02:20:50.900 --> 02:20:53.467
history does indeed give us the answer.

02:20:54.867 --> 02:20:58.734
SOS believes the development adjacent to Bug Light would take

02:20:58.767 --> 02:21:00.333
away from the public that

02:21:02.800 --> 02:21:06.800
which belongs to them and make the park a front yard for the

02:21:06.800 --> 02:21:10.333
few who can afford to live there.We'd like to see a

02:21:10.400 --> 02:21:13.634
larger park with a living shoreline, an example of

02:21:13.667 --> 02:21:16.233
nature-based climate solutions

02:21:17.734 --> 02:21:18.066
and

02:21:19.166 --> 02:21:20.433
parks and-

02:21:20.634 --> 02:21:21.667
Linda, you have to finish

02:21:22.066 --> 02:21:25.934
... creating a park and a memorial is a one-time only opportunity.

02:21:26.433 --> 02:21:27.166
Thank you.

02:21:32.700 --> 02:21:35.667
Hello, my name is Rachel Berger. I live at 17 Churchill.

02:21:36.667 --> 02:21:40.300
Can't hear you. We can't hear you. Rachel, you're going to have to try to-

02:21:40.333 --> 02:21:42.467
Okay. My name is Rachel Berger, and I-

02:21:42.500 --> 02:21:44.033
Keep that microphone. Pull it close to you if it helps.

02:21:44.367 --> 02:21:45.600
Get it closer to my face?

02:21:45.767 --> 02:21:46.500
If it... Yes.

02:21:47.066 --> 02:21:47.500
Like that?

02:21:47.834 --> 02:21:48.133
Mm-hmm. Very close.

02:21:48.133 --> 02:21:51.066
There's a pen too, so you can actually bend it towards you.

02:21:52.533 --> 02:21:53.233
How about that?

02:21:53.467 --> 02:21:53.667
Ah.

02:21:53.734 --> 02:21:54.333
There you go.

02:21:54.367 --> 02:21:54.934
That's better?

02:21:55.433 --> 02:21:55.433
Thank you.

02:21:55.467 --> 02:21:56.266
Okay, terrific.

02:21:57.767 --> 02:22:01.567
I have very personal reasons for supporting a shipyard

02:22:01.667 --> 02:22:05.600
memorial. The Liberty Ships have a special place in my

02:22:05.634 --> 02:22:09.400
memory as my family and I were at sea

02:22:09.467 --> 02:22:12.133
along with those ships during World War II.

02:22:14.100 --> 02:22:17.667
There is no doubt in my mind that America's Liberty Ships were

02:22:17.767 --> 02:22:21.300
critical to winning the war by delivering essential

02:22:21.867 --> 02:22:23.467
supplies overseas.

02:22:24.700 --> 02:22:28.567
I was two when the war started. My parents had joined an

02:22:28.667 --> 02:22:32.567
international Christian pacifist commune that included

02:22:32.600 --> 02:22:35.000
Germans, so we had to leave England.

02:22:35.867 --> 02:22:38.433
The only country that would take us was Paraguay.

02:22:39.133 --> 02:22:42.867
My family, along with 80 other people, crossed the Atlantic

02:22:43.834 --> 02:22:47.233
to settle in South America. But two years later,

02:22:47.734 --> 02:22:51.166
at the height of the war, we and other Brits in our

02:22:51.233 --> 02:22:54.100
group were called back to England.

02:22:55.634 --> 02:22:59.600
My dad worked very hard to secure a safe passage for

02:22:59.667 --> 02:22:59.934
us.

02:23:00.767 --> 02:23:04.100
All he could find was a small merchant ship that supplied

02:23:04.266 --> 02:23:08.066
Argentine meat to England. Hitler's wartime goal

02:23:08.133 --> 02:23:11.367
was to surround England and starve the Brits.

02:23:13.066 --> 02:23:16.834
We boarded our ships and waited for a convoy to travel with us.

02:23:17.400 --> 02:23:19.567
Once we got underway, it was

02:23:20.667 --> 02:23:24.634
slow going because our ship's bow had been

02:23:24.734 --> 02:23:25.734
blown off

02:23:26.634 --> 02:23:28.867
and replaced with concrete.

02:23:32.166 --> 02:23:35.767
The crew was very worried about having children aboard

02:23:36.200 --> 02:23:39.834
and made us sleep in life jackets with flashlights.

02:23:40.500 --> 02:23:43.433
They told our parents to throw us out as far as they could

02:23:46.834 --> 02:23:49.266
if the ships went down,

02:23:50.066 --> 02:23:53.800
so the captain's motorized lifeboat could collect

02:23:53.867 --> 02:23:54.800
us children.

02:23:55.967 --> 02:23:59.867
As we neared England, the war intensified, and we joined a

02:24:00.066 --> 02:24:03.800
21-ship convoy that included Liberty Ships

02:24:04.667 --> 02:24:07.967
from the US. While adults

02:24:08.433 --> 02:24:12.367
gathered in our ship's lounge to hear Churchill, my brother

02:24:12.400 --> 02:24:15.900
and I slipped out on the deck to see why the guns were

02:24:15.934 --> 02:24:19.367
firing. We saw a German plane drop a bomb a

02:24:19.433 --> 02:24:22.066
few hundred yards from where we were,

02:24:23.133 --> 02:24:23.867
from our ship.

02:24:26.200 --> 02:24:29.600
Since England was surrounded by U-boats, our

02:24:29.667 --> 02:24:31.567
convoy had to change direction-

02:24:31.667 --> 02:24:31.667
Back end

02:24:31.667 --> 02:24:34.900
... to avoid the English Channel. We were guided

02:24:37.200 --> 02:24:40.266
around to another way.

02:24:43.533 --> 02:24:47.467
In this way, the Liberty Ships helped the British Isles and my family

02:24:47.567 --> 02:24:48.533
survive the war.

02:24:49.433 --> 02:24:53.066
I think a memorial park on our waterfront would be a wonderful

02:24:53.166 --> 02:24:53.600
tribute.

02:24:54.467 --> 02:24:54.834
Thank you.

02:24:55.767 --> 02:24:56.967
Thank you very much.

02:25:01.800 --> 02:25:05.266
Susan Henderson on 9 East Street.

02:25:07.266 --> 02:25:08.467
What Rachel said

02:25:09.567 --> 02:25:11.867
about the Liberty Ships and

02:25:13.033 --> 02:25:15.567
what Linda said about the Portland pipeline,

02:25:16.934 --> 02:25:18.800
to me, these efforts

02:25:19.867 --> 02:25:23.367
exemplify the best of our

02:25:23.433 --> 02:25:25.967
nation, the skill,

02:25:26.900 --> 02:25:30.033
the intelligence, the organization,

02:25:30.800 --> 02:25:34.667
the use of resources to achieve a goal,

02:25:35.800 --> 02:25:39.066
and it was a difficult goal. And that goal they

02:25:39.133 --> 02:25:41.033
achieved. We won

02:25:42.233 --> 02:25:45.767
and defeated Hitler's forces, and we

02:25:45.934 --> 02:25:49.734
saved England and children like Rachel.

02:25:51.233 --> 02:25:55.066
That was the task of the 1940s.

02:25:55.834 --> 02:25:58.133
Now we are in 2026,

02:25:59.400 --> 02:26:00.567
and a park

02:26:02.233 --> 02:26:03.667
at the Eastern Waterfront

02:26:05.066 --> 02:26:06.834
could exemplify

02:26:07.634 --> 02:26:10.934
the best of what our country can do now.

02:26:12.033 --> 02:26:15.834
Now our task is to keep our planet

02:26:16.433 --> 02:26:16.934
habitable

02:26:17.800 --> 02:26:21.367
and to keep it beautiful with open space that

02:26:21.567 --> 02:26:24.834
people can use, and children can see natural

02:26:24.934 --> 02:26:27.500
space and run freely.

02:26:28.667 --> 02:26:30.600
This Eastern Waterfront

02:26:32.033 --> 02:26:35.567
could be considered perhaps one of the most beautiful natural

02:26:35.667 --> 02:26:38.233
shorelines on the whole Eastern Seaboard.

02:26:39.066 --> 02:26:42.634
It's an amazing amount of open space if you walk

02:26:42.667 --> 02:26:44.333
there a lot.

02:26:45.266 --> 02:26:46.433
It is

02:26:48.834 --> 02:26:52.133
a place that once developed will never come back.

02:26:52.967 --> 02:26:56.634
When you're down there, you have this feeling of spaciousness

02:26:57.266 --> 02:27:01.233
and wind and vistas and light on water

02:27:01.266 --> 02:27:03.667
and clouds and peace

02:27:04.467 --> 02:27:05.867
and little animals.

02:27:07.333 --> 02:27:11.133
Somebody said once that the Packard property is nothing property.

02:27:11.634 --> 02:27:15.600
It should be made nice. Well, the Packard property has been open

02:27:15.667 --> 02:27:19.166
space almost for 30 years. And birds and little

02:27:19.300 --> 02:27:21.834
animals and plants have grown there.

02:27:22.934 --> 02:27:23.233
So

02:27:24.533 --> 02:27:26.033
I think if we

02:27:26.233 --> 02:27:29.500
couldDevelop that space

02:27:30.033 --> 02:27:33.400
as an open space. It would be an

02:27:33.467 --> 02:27:36.767
amazing achievement of this-

02:27:36.800 --> 02:27:37.166
30 seconds

02:27:37.333 --> 02:27:41.066
... city. And it would be almost a national achievement,

02:27:41.700 --> 02:27:45.667
be akin to Central Park in New York or Prospect

02:27:45.767 --> 02:27:46.700
Park in Brooklyn,

02:27:47.567 --> 02:27:47.867
or

02:27:50.433 --> 02:27:52.066
the Fort Williams.

02:27:53.500 --> 02:27:55.467
So ask you to please

02:27:56.767 --> 02:27:59.400
think of that space and its beauty. Thank you.

02:28:08.800 --> 02:28:10.500
Barbara Dee, Scammon Street.

02:28:11.900 --> 02:28:13.867
Good evening, counselors and community members.

02:28:14.000 --> 02:28:17.767
I want to acknowledge the thousands of hours of this

02:28:17.800 --> 02:28:20.200
little city has spent on the comprehensive plan.

02:28:21.166 --> 02:28:24.300
The comprehensive plan committee, the planning department, and I will give a

02:28:24.300 --> 02:28:25.233
shout-out to Eli,

02:28:26.100 --> 02:28:28.667
the city council staff, community members,

02:28:29.600 --> 02:28:33.066
Protect South Portland, No Yard South, Save Our Shipyard, petitions,

02:28:33.200 --> 02:28:36.166
emails, letters to the editor, comments, surveys, meetings.

02:28:36.767 --> 02:28:40.667
This has been an amazing show of devotion to South Portland, and we all

02:28:40.767 --> 02:28:44.233
want the best for our community. The SOS committee, Save Our

02:28:44.266 --> 02:28:47.967
Shipyard, asks the council to adopt option

02:28:48.133 --> 02:28:51.600
three, which states, "Continue for substantial amendments,"

02:28:52.333 --> 02:28:55.433
because we are requesting the creation of CALUPA 27.

02:28:56.500 --> 02:29:00.467
CALUPA 27 removes 21 and modifies 22 to

02:29:00.467 --> 02:29:03.967
allow the creation of the Eastern Waterfront Coastal Protection and

02:29:04.066 --> 02:29:07.767
Heritage Area. K27 solves the community's

02:29:07.867 --> 02:29:11.834
concerns with extensive residential and lodging development next

02:29:11.834 --> 02:29:15.600
to Bug Light by providing a publicly accessible living

02:29:15.634 --> 02:29:19.233
shoreline, a working waterfront, and a Liberty Ship

02:29:19.300 --> 02:29:23.033
Heritage Common instead of housing or lodging on what we all

02:29:23.066 --> 02:29:26.533
know is unsafe, contaminated land at the end of a

02:29:26.567 --> 02:29:30.233
peninsula. On June 12th, 2025, the city

02:29:30.266 --> 02:29:32.867
council held a workshop on the comprehensive plan.

02:29:33.433 --> 02:29:36.967
At that workshop, I handed in a petition with 2,500 signatures

02:29:37.467 --> 02:29:40.433
protesting any residential development in the shipyard zone.

02:29:41.800 --> 02:29:45.033
I clearly remember the council in June of 2025

02:29:45.433 --> 02:29:49.300
asking the planning department to scale back the density in the shipyard

02:29:49.367 --> 02:29:53.166
zone and to change the growth designation from high growth to

02:29:53.266 --> 02:29:56.800
limited growth. These changes were never implemented.

02:29:57.967 --> 02:30:01.934
The agenda for the CPC on July 10th states, "Staff to

02:30:02.033 --> 02:30:05.967
ask CPC to consider a new CALUPA number 27 as shown in

02:30:06.000 --> 02:30:08.233
attachment." There was a map attached.

02:30:08.333 --> 02:30:11.967
The CPC discussed and revised CALUPAs 11 to 27

02:30:12.467 --> 02:30:16.367
to adopt the policies of CALUPA 27 as presented,

02:30:16.433 --> 02:30:20.400
but modified the boundaries to roughly follow a line 300 feet from

02:30:20.467 --> 02:30:23.934
the coast and separate the CALUPA into two areas, 21 and

02:30:24.100 --> 02:30:24.467
22.

02:30:25.367 --> 02:30:28.900
The minutes from July 30th reference the CALUPA formerly known as

02:30:29.000 --> 02:30:32.700
CALUPA 27. So we are bringing it back.

02:30:32.867 --> 02:30:36.066
We would like to bring it back. We want to revise it and revive it.

02:30:36.667 --> 02:30:40.634
Because designating CALUPA 27 was a brilliant move

02:30:40.667 --> 02:30:41.734
by the planning department-

02:30:41.900 --> 02:30:42.400
30 seconds

02:30:42.400 --> 02:30:45.934
... to address community unrest about development in the shipyard zone.

02:30:46.667 --> 02:30:50.066
K27 has everything in it that South Portland needs.

02:30:50.133 --> 02:30:53.400
Open space, working waterfront, nature-based coastal resilience,

02:30:53.867 --> 02:30:57.800
brownfield remediation, nature-based infrastructure, continuous

02:30:57.900 --> 02:31:01.867
public shoreline, a Liberty Ship memorial, an expanded museum, and a

02:31:01.900 --> 02:31:05.567
plan for after the tanks are gone. We have everything to gain from

02:31:05.667 --> 02:31:07.367
K27. Thank you.

02:31:08.300 --> 02:31:09.400
Thank you very much.

02:31:17.634 --> 02:31:19.367
I'm Josh Cutler from Bay Road.

02:31:20.700 --> 02:31:24.500
I once read that once upon a time in Maine, it used to be said that a

02:31:24.567 --> 02:31:26.834
man who owns a boat owns an island.

02:31:27.934 --> 02:31:31.600
Today, that's no longer true. Many islands are closed to visitors,

02:31:32.133 --> 02:31:33.900
even those with a boat to get them there.

02:31:34.567 --> 02:31:38.367
Most land on the coast of Maine is privately owned and inaccessible to

02:31:38.400 --> 02:31:39.100
Maine people.

02:31:39.967 --> 02:31:43.200
Businesses and people who rely on water access see it

02:31:43.233 --> 02:31:47.066
diminishing. Some of our recreational and working waterfront, Bug

02:31:47.133 --> 02:31:50.634
Light Park and the Eastern Waterfront area of South Portland, are threatened

02:31:51.467 --> 02:31:54.266
by the language of the draft of the comprehensive plan.

02:31:55.834 --> 02:31:59.700
As part of the process of exploring funding for the future Liberty

02:31:59.800 --> 02:32:03.367
Shipyard Memorial Park so that burden doesn't fall to the

02:32:03.533 --> 02:32:03.867
city,

02:32:04.767 --> 02:32:08.433
I've described it to funding organizations, including the Maine Coast Heritage

02:32:08.634 --> 02:32:12.000
Trust, the Trust for Public Land, and the Conservation Fund.

02:32:12.834 --> 02:32:16.333
They understand that more people can enjoy nearby parks with ocean

02:32:16.400 --> 02:32:20.333
access and views than can visit remote preserve sites,

02:32:20.600 --> 02:32:24.467
often offshore, that still require the boat

02:32:24.934 --> 02:32:27.934
and a lot of vacation time as well to get to.

02:32:28.100 --> 02:32:32.100
I suspect that many more people visit Bug Light Park on a weekday afternoon

02:32:32.133 --> 02:32:35.667
than visit some of the more remote preserved lands in an entire

02:32:35.734 --> 02:32:36.166
season.

02:32:37.934 --> 02:32:41.500
Does the proposed comprehensive plan advance this vision?

02:32:42.467 --> 02:32:46.433
It is possibly true, as we've heard, that land use policy for the

02:32:46.467 --> 02:32:49.634
Eastern Waterfront area articulated in the plan in

02:32:49.667 --> 02:32:53.567
K21 and K22 does not preclude the

02:32:53.667 --> 02:32:57.600
goals of the proposed K27, which we've put before

02:32:57.667 --> 02:32:59.000
you this evening. However-

02:32:59.033 --> 02:33:01.400
Could you speak slightly... Could you speak up slightly?

02:33:01.500 --> 02:33:01.967
Yep, sorry.

02:33:02.066 --> 02:33:02.433
Thank you.

02:33:02.500 --> 02:33:05.867
However, acceptance of the plan in its current form adds to the

02:33:05.900 --> 02:33:09.834
complexity of achieving these goals by assigning further discussion

02:33:09.900 --> 02:33:13.467
of them to planning and zoning processes to follow

02:33:13.567 --> 02:33:13.900
later.

02:33:14.767 --> 02:33:18.734
These processes would have a better roadmap,

02:33:19.367 --> 02:33:22.734
in Milan's terms, better guidance, if the

02:33:22.767 --> 02:33:26.367
preservation goals for the district are clearly described in the

02:33:26.400 --> 02:33:30.033
comprehensive plan. They've already been advocated for and

02:33:30.066 --> 02:33:33.433
endorsed by literally thousands of South Portland citizens

02:33:33.867 --> 02:33:37.133
through comments before the CPC, through written

02:33:37.233 --> 02:33:39.934
petition, and through electronic comments on the

02:33:39.967 --> 02:33:43.900
plan.There is an opportunity to reconsider the land

02:33:44.000 --> 02:33:47.934
use policy for the shipyard area. I urge the council

02:33:48.000 --> 02:33:51.433
to vote option three in the planning director's letter and take time-

02:33:51.567 --> 02:33:51.634
Seconds

02:33:52.000 --> 02:33:55.533
... to consider and adopt the policy goals expressed

02:33:56.000 --> 02:33:59.600
in Save Our Shipyards CALUPA 27, so that the

02:33:59.700 --> 02:34:02.600
final accepted version of the comp plan

02:34:02.634 --> 02:34:06.567
expresses a clear vision of an accessible, open,

02:34:06.834 --> 02:34:10.300
eastern waterfront, and marine protection and heritage

02:34:10.400 --> 02:34:13.734
area for the people of South Portland and beyond.

02:34:17.333 --> 02:34:19.934
Thank you.

02:34:24.400 --> 02:34:24.900
Can you hear me?

02:34:26.433 --> 02:34:26.467
No.

02:34:26.500 --> 02:34:30.333
Hi, I'm Linda Skinner, 824 Sawyer Street in South Portland, where I've

02:34:30.400 --> 02:34:31.867
lived for 35 years now.

02:34:33.200 --> 02:34:36.767
We all know that South Portland is in desperate need of housing, especially

02:34:36.834 --> 02:34:40.433
affordable housing, and we need housing that serves a varied

02:34:40.467 --> 02:34:44.233
population and a range of incomes, from young families and single

02:34:44.333 --> 02:34:48.266
professionals to essential workers and students to retirees.

02:34:49.433 --> 02:34:53.133
South Portland is already the second most densely populated city in the

02:34:53.233 --> 02:34:57.166
state, and as more people are drawn to our town, that population just

02:34:57.200 --> 02:35:00.834
keeps growing, accelerating the need for residential development.

02:35:01.667 --> 02:35:04.367
The comprehensive plan clearly aims to meet that need.

02:35:05.300 --> 02:35:09.233
20 of the 26 CALUPAs in the plan are flagged for residential

02:35:09.233 --> 02:35:13.100
growth of varying degrees. That's a lot of new housing to put in an

02:35:13.200 --> 02:35:15.100
already heavily populated city.

02:35:16.333 --> 02:35:18.734
That additional growth will take several forms.

02:35:18.734 --> 02:35:22.734
There'll be ADUs on existing properties and smaller lots

02:35:22.734 --> 02:35:25.000
and multi-story buildings for new residential.

02:35:26.000 --> 02:35:29.600
The trade-off for more housing is much smaller yards per dwelling.

02:35:30.333 --> 02:35:34.300
People who live in densely populated neighborhoods need open

02:35:34.333 --> 02:35:37.834
air and green space for their mental, physical, and social wellbeing,

02:35:38.467 --> 02:35:42.400
a space like the proposed Shipyard Memorial Park on our eastern waterfront,

02:35:43.100 --> 02:35:47.100
and that could be easily accomplished by adding 27, page 27, back

02:35:47.133 --> 02:35:48.033
into the plan.

02:35:49.000 --> 02:35:52.800
Right now, we have Bug Light, a local treasure that sits on the edge

02:35:52.900 --> 02:35:56.800
of a tract of open space marked for three to six-story

02:35:56.900 --> 02:35:59.634
buildings, including residential.

02:35:59.734 --> 02:36:03.367
A lot of people think that's a bad idea for obvious environmental and safety

02:36:03.433 --> 02:36:07.333
reasons, but it's also the last undeveloped stretch of waterfront in

02:36:07.433 --> 02:36:11.166
southern Maine, and locals and tourists alike are drawn there daily by the

02:36:11.200 --> 02:36:15.133
beauty and peace. We have a once in a lifetime opportunity

02:36:15.133 --> 02:36:18.834
here to expand and preserve this lovely waterfront park for the public to

02:36:18.867 --> 02:36:21.567
enjoy until the sea ultimately claims it.

02:36:22.500 --> 02:36:26.433
In the meantime, it would be protected by nature-based infrastructure, the

02:36:26.500 --> 02:36:29.700
most cost-effective and durable response to rising seas.

02:36:30.567 --> 02:36:34.333
South Portland is known and respected as a green, eco-friendly city.

02:36:34.934 --> 02:36:38.867
We owe it to present and future residents to create housing in a way that upholds

02:36:38.900 --> 02:36:40.500
that reputation. Thank you.

02:36:41.333 --> 02:36:44.767
Thank you very much. And I forgot to say thank you to Mr. Kepler.

02:36:45.000 --> 02:36:46.100
Thank you, Mr. Kepler.

02:36:48.600 --> 02:36:50.834
And could you hang on for just one second, John?

02:36:51.233 --> 02:36:51.233
Yes.

02:36:51.300 --> 02:36:55.000
I'm unfamiliar with chairing hybrid meetings.

02:36:55.433 --> 02:36:58.800
Do we do all of the public comments in the room and

02:36:58.900 --> 02:37:01.300
then check on whether there's someone?

02:37:01.400 --> 02:37:02.767
I do. I don't have any hands up right now-

02:37:02.800 --> 02:37:02.800
Don't have... Okay

02:37:02.800 --> 02:37:05.400
... but that's a good reminder for folks online.

02:37:05.433 --> 02:37:09.333
If you do have comments you wish to make, please hit the raise your hand button.

02:37:09.900 --> 02:37:13.800
Okay, thank you. And then also, I think Milan, you might be in control

02:37:13.834 --> 02:37:17.133
of this screen. Oh, whoever's there is-

02:37:17.433 --> 02:37:17.700
That's up.

02:37:17.767 --> 02:37:21.133
Oh, that's you. There was a request to show what council's

02:37:21.266 --> 02:37:22.900
options are tonight.

02:37:23.700 --> 02:37:26.266
So that's Milan's slide. Do you want me to put back his Milan slide?

02:37:26.266 --> 02:37:26.266
It is Milan's slide.

02:37:26.300 --> 02:37:27.133
They have it on the screen. They have to...

02:37:28.200 --> 02:37:28.433
Okay.

02:37:28.600 --> 02:37:30.700
But will we still be able to show the timer, though?

02:37:30.734 --> 02:37:33.500
Nope, so this screen will remain on for the time for public comment.

02:37:33.567 --> 02:37:33.700
Okay.

02:37:33.734 --> 02:37:36.634
And then once we've concluded public comment, we can switch the screen back to

02:37:36.634 --> 02:37:38.000
Milan's to show the options.

02:37:38.767 --> 02:37:42.433
Okay. All right. Hear that, everybody? Can't show the options quite yet.

02:37:42.967 --> 02:37:44.867
All right, John Pawnee, I believe.

02:37:44.867 --> 02:37:46.066
Well, I don't have to say it.

02:37:46.133 --> 02:37:46.133
Thank you.

02:37:46.133 --> 02:37:46.734
You just did.

02:37:47.700 --> 02:37:50.433
Thank you, counselors. John Pawnee, Beach Street.

02:37:53.567 --> 02:37:57.433
I have to both speak to the microphone and see what I've written here.

02:37:58.133 --> 02:37:59.667
Sorry, we're full of tricks.

02:37:59.734 --> 02:38:00.667
Lean in to VR.

02:38:00.700 --> 02:38:03.800
John, I'm going to come down and read that for you if you want me going.

02:38:03.800 --> 02:38:04.000
If you would.

02:38:05.300 --> 02:38:08.700
The eastern waterfront is a derelict and

02:38:08.900 --> 02:38:11.000
currently useless property.

02:38:12.300 --> 02:38:16.000
It could, however, provide great value to the

02:38:16.033 --> 02:38:16.367
city.

02:38:17.433 --> 02:38:21.300
The difference between what it is worth to the city now

02:38:22.200 --> 02:38:25.734
and the value it could bring in tax revenue and public

02:38:25.800 --> 02:38:28.533
benefit, that difference is striking.

02:38:30.433 --> 02:38:34.033
Appropriate development there would help to solve a serious

02:38:34.367 --> 02:38:35.500
fiscal problem.

02:38:36.500 --> 02:38:40.233
The city is unable to fund basic needs at this

02:38:40.300 --> 02:38:43.867
time, and without a change in course, the

02:38:43.934 --> 02:38:46.500
situation is going to get worse.

02:38:48.333 --> 02:38:51.934
The eastern waterfront is privately owned.

02:38:52.867 --> 02:38:55.066
The city cannot just take it,

02:38:56.133 --> 02:38:59.800
including it is illegal by state and federal

02:38:59.900 --> 02:39:00.200
law

02:39:01.400 --> 02:39:05.266
for the city to make that property worthless by aggressive

02:39:05.500 --> 02:39:07.600
zoning. You cannot do that.

02:39:08.967 --> 02:39:12.800
The city or some nonprofit would have to buy the property at

02:39:12.900 --> 02:39:16.000
current market value. That's the law.

02:39:17.700 --> 02:39:20.400
I do not see that money materializing.

02:39:20.600 --> 02:39:24.400
I don't see where it comes from. The city needs to

02:39:24.433 --> 02:39:27.266
work with the people who own the property.

02:39:28.934 --> 02:39:32.400
We should negotiate with the owners who live locally,

02:39:32.734 --> 02:39:34.000
they live nearby,

02:39:35.533 --> 02:39:39.066
to provide a development that includes public benefits.

02:39:40.300 --> 02:39:42.567
Development could include small business,

02:39:43.600 --> 02:39:47.000
eating establishments, and housing, and would

02:39:47.100 --> 02:39:51.000
benefit the publicIt would be

02:39:51.100 --> 02:39:53.533
perfect for including amenities

02:39:54.500 --> 02:39:57.834
such as open access through the

02:39:57.900 --> 02:39:59.333
shoreline area,

02:40:01.100 --> 02:40:01.967
walking trails,

02:40:03.233 --> 02:40:07.066
bike paths, and connections between the Greenway,

02:40:07.800 --> 02:40:11.200
Bug Light, and the SMCC and Willard Beach

02:40:11.533 --> 02:40:14.734
areas. All we have to do is

02:40:15.667 --> 02:40:16.700
negotiate.

02:40:19.266 --> 02:40:21.533
There are challenges to developing there.

02:40:21.767 --> 02:40:21.767
30 seconds.

02:40:21.767 --> 02:40:23.000
We all know them,

02:40:24.467 --> 02:40:25.967
but they can be mitigated.

02:40:27.000 --> 02:40:30.333
They can be solved. We can solve those problems.

02:40:30.367 --> 02:40:33.867
The shoreland zoning is a beautiful example of

02:40:34.000 --> 02:40:37.400
solving a problem. That was brilliant work and it's an

02:40:37.467 --> 02:40:39.367
example of mitigation.

02:40:40.567 --> 02:40:44.433
The Comprehensive Plan Committee has spent years putting

02:40:44.467 --> 02:40:47.667
together an excellent plan that is thoughtful,

02:40:48.400 --> 02:40:50.100
careful, and responsible.

02:40:50.767 --> 02:40:50.934
Time.

02:40:50.934 --> 02:40:54.200
It provides guidance that points the way to reasonable

02:40:54.700 --> 02:40:57.834
opportunities. We should adopt it and move forward.

02:40:59.300 --> 02:41:00.533
Thank you very much.

02:41:06.467 --> 02:41:10.000
My name is Brian Nickerson. I live on Pine Street in Ferry Village, but I don't

02:41:10.066 --> 02:41:13.967
want to talk about Ferry Village. I'm talking about the

02:41:14.000 --> 02:41:16.066
Kalupa that's right under our feet right now.

02:41:16.700 --> 02:41:20.300
I've been working with Tim Honey, the coordinator of the Toss the Tanks

02:41:20.867 --> 02:41:24.667
initiative, and specifically the redevelopment of the Hill

02:41:24.700 --> 02:41:28.433
Street tank farm right next door, currently owned by

02:41:28.500 --> 02:41:30.300
Suncor Oil out of Canada.

02:41:31.667 --> 02:41:35.300
Tim, by the way, he's in New Zealand, and he wanted me to give you

02:41:35.467 --> 02:41:39.266
his regards. Both he and Deborah are there for

02:41:39.333 --> 02:41:42.300
about a month on both islands.

02:41:43.300 --> 02:41:47.066
The Toss the Tanks initiative wants to thank the Comprehensive Plan

02:41:47.200 --> 02:41:51.133
Citizens Committee for their hard work and the long hours they've had to put

02:41:51.200 --> 02:41:53.967
in in the past two years to create an excellent

02:41:54.133 --> 02:41:57.967
2040 comprehensive plan. We want to thank the committee

02:41:58.000 --> 02:42:01.967
for including specific language in the plan regarding the Hill Street

02:42:01.967 --> 02:42:05.600
tank farm that sets forth a vision and strategies for

02:42:05.634 --> 02:42:08.734
removing all 19 mostly empty tanks.

02:42:09.567 --> 02:42:13.200
By doing so, it'll set the stage for the property to be

02:42:13.200 --> 02:42:16.367
transformed into a mixed-use development,

02:42:16.700 --> 02:42:19.367
primarily focusing on housing needs of the city.

02:42:20.634 --> 02:42:24.567
This is the first comprehensive plan calling for the elimination of this large and

02:42:24.634 --> 02:42:28.266
underutilized tank farm. Redevelopment of the property should

02:42:28.266 --> 02:42:32.100
address two of the city's most important priorities, housing and

02:42:32.166 --> 02:42:33.800
expanding city tax base.

02:42:35.200 --> 02:42:38.767
This redevelopment could generate an estimated at least eight times

02:42:39.667 --> 02:42:43.567
or $4 million, the amount of annual property taxes now

02:42:43.600 --> 02:42:47.367
being paid for by Suncor Portland Pipeline

02:42:47.400 --> 02:42:48.066
Incorporated.

02:42:49.000 --> 02:42:50.066
We urge the council to

02:42:50.100 --> 02:42:54.266
unanimously

02:42:54.300 --> 02:42:58.000
support the 2040 comprehensive plan for the city, and we urge the

02:42:58.033 --> 02:43:02.000
council to support the Hill Street redevelopment plan, which calls for the

02:43:02.066 --> 02:43:05.266
elimination of the 19 oil tanks at the Hill Street facility,

02:43:06.400 --> 02:43:09.767
replacing them with a more compatible

02:43:09.867 --> 02:43:13.400
mixed-use redevelopment of housing, community

02:43:13.500 --> 02:43:17.133
services, offices, retail, and open space. Thank you.

02:43:18.100 --> 02:43:19.367
Thank you very much.

02:43:26.867 --> 02:43:29.066
Kay Michigan, Romano Road.

02:43:32.400 --> 02:43:35.567
Thank you, councilors, for this opportunity to make a statement.

02:43:37.066 --> 02:43:40.333
You have worked long and hard, as well as the committee,

02:43:41.200 --> 02:43:44.700
to determine how South Portland can navigate into the future.

02:43:46.166 --> 02:43:47.500
Thank you so much for that.

02:43:48.867 --> 02:43:52.300
Foreseeing the future is difficult and it weighs heavy in

02:43:52.300 --> 02:43:53.333
responsibility,

02:43:54.333 --> 02:43:56.467
and this document that we're looking at

02:43:57.266 --> 02:44:01.200
is really heavy lifting. I'm sure you all want

02:44:01.266 --> 02:44:02.867
it to be done.

02:44:04.467 --> 02:44:07.967
Among all the difficult and complicated issues of today,

02:44:08.900 --> 02:44:11.834
this for you could be one box ticked off,

02:44:12.634 --> 02:44:12.967
done.

02:44:15.133 --> 02:44:18.467
But it's a changing world and things are

02:44:18.567 --> 02:44:19.166
uncertain.

02:44:20.100 --> 02:44:23.100
I would ask you tonight to please take a deep breath

02:44:24.667 --> 02:44:25.500
and pause

02:44:26.634 --> 02:44:28.100
before this final vote.

02:44:29.166 --> 02:44:32.700
This is too important to make a decision you might

02:44:32.734 --> 02:44:33.266
regret.

02:44:35.000 --> 02:44:38.767
I've been attending some meetings of the Comp Plan Committee and hearing

02:44:38.834 --> 02:44:42.567
various presentations and discussions, and

02:44:42.567 --> 02:44:46.166
I have been convinced that there are some clauses

02:44:46.200 --> 02:44:46.800
containing

02:44:47.634 --> 02:44:51.166
that need to be changed that pertain to the Eastern Waterfront.

02:44:52.300 --> 02:44:56.166
I think residential development in this area is not

02:44:56.266 --> 02:44:56.700
wise.

02:44:58.166 --> 02:45:00.767
I know South Portland wants to strengthen its

02:45:00.800 --> 02:45:04.433
finances by getting more revenue from real

02:45:04.500 --> 02:45:06.367
estate taxes. That's good.

02:45:07.333 --> 02:45:10.367
South Portland needs more housing. Yes, we do.

02:45:11.467 --> 02:45:15.066
Residential building is allowed in many of the city's areas

02:45:15.634 --> 02:45:17.767
and could address both of those issues.

02:45:18.834 --> 02:45:22.634
But the narrow peninsula of Bug Light is not a

02:45:22.700 --> 02:45:26.400
place to allow this development, and we've heard all those

02:45:26.433 --> 02:45:28.000
reasons over and over.

02:45:29.467 --> 02:45:33.233
And now we have a suggestion to create a park in this area.

02:45:33.867 --> 02:45:37.467
This would protect the city from the impact of

02:45:37.500 --> 02:45:38.133
development,

02:45:39.166 --> 02:45:42.934
and in turn, probably generate additional

02:45:42.967 --> 02:45:43.400
revenue

02:45:44.266 --> 02:45:45.867
as a destination site

02:45:46.667 --> 02:45:48.767
for residents and those from away.

02:45:50.600 --> 02:45:54.500
I believe the average South Portland today is in the dark

02:45:54.567 --> 02:45:58.333
about all the things we're talking aboutBecause we

02:45:58.400 --> 02:46:00.700
lost our media print presence-

02:46:01.567 --> 02:46:01.567
Seconds

02:46:01.767 --> 02:46:05.700
... and community issues such as this comp plan are not known

02:46:05.734 --> 02:46:09.266
to most citizens in spite of what we've heard tonight.

02:46:11.634 --> 02:46:15.567
The hardworking families and young people in South Portland no longer

02:46:15.634 --> 02:46:19.634
have headlines facing them on the countertop or in the newsstand.

02:46:20.433 --> 02:46:24.367
And therefore, a lot of people have been left out of this process.

02:46:25.266 --> 02:46:27.900
They'll be shocked when they hear the results.

02:46:28.934 --> 02:46:32.800
And yet, these are the folks who have the most to lose

02:46:33.533 --> 02:46:36.734
and the most to gain from your decision. Thank you.

02:46:37.433 --> 02:46:38.634
Thank you very much.

02:46:42.033 --> 02:46:42.700
Come on up.

02:46:45.900 --> 02:46:48.333
Elaine Salekis, Strathmore Road.

02:46:48.400 --> 02:46:52.300
Thank you, council members, for this opportunity to speak and for your hard

02:46:52.400 --> 02:46:52.700
work.

02:46:53.934 --> 02:46:57.567
I'm deeply connected to Bug Light and my community, and I

02:46:57.634 --> 02:47:01.033
urge you to please pause and

02:47:01.100 --> 02:47:01.734
review.

02:47:03.166 --> 02:47:06.867
Not to go right into this comprehensive plan and

02:47:06.967 --> 02:47:08.533
start changing zonings,

02:47:09.967 --> 02:47:13.834
growth designations, and future development frameworks for the Eastern

02:47:13.934 --> 02:47:14.734
Waterfront.

02:47:16.033 --> 02:47:19.934
I oppose shrinking or curbing this remaining beautiful, accessible,

02:47:19.934 --> 02:47:23.667
and historic space. Caving in to the developer to

02:47:23.734 --> 02:47:27.133
change zoning would give them carte blanche for

02:47:27.200 --> 02:47:29.600
anything they want and set bad precedent.

02:47:29.900 --> 02:47:33.433
No matter all of the language that is used and all of the word

02:47:33.533 --> 02:47:37.467
salads that are going around for the comprehensive plan, there's a

02:47:37.600 --> 02:47:41.433
lot of language in there, even with unique growth and

02:47:41.567 --> 02:47:45.467
high growth, that can be parsed. We live

02:47:45.533 --> 02:47:48.700
in troubled times of division, war,

02:47:48.834 --> 02:47:52.634
anxiety, high costs, and even finding

02:47:52.667 --> 02:47:55.967
out and questioning what is real. We need

02:47:56.000 --> 02:47:59.634
community, and we need nature, and we're losing both

02:47:59.734 --> 02:48:00.200
quickly.

02:48:01.133 --> 02:48:04.333
South Portland has much to offer if we stay true and

02:48:04.433 --> 02:48:08.266
open to the treasures we have that are worth preserving,

02:48:08.367 --> 02:48:12.333
not exploiting. It's all well and good to see little round dots,

02:48:12.400 --> 02:48:15.634
and circles, and flat sections of land,

02:48:15.734 --> 02:48:19.367
supposedly illustrations, but we don't see the

02:48:19.433 --> 02:48:23.300
impact of that on our lives and what would

02:48:23.400 --> 02:48:24.367
actually happen.

02:48:25.533 --> 02:48:29.367
The treasure that no dollar can measure is Bug Light

02:48:29.533 --> 02:48:32.900
Park and surrounding land, providing a respite from the

02:48:33.100 --> 02:48:36.834
24/7 news cycle. A place to engage with nature

02:48:36.934 --> 02:48:40.367
unencumbered, and to reduce stress, and

02:48:40.800 --> 02:48:44.100
enjoy and engage with our neighbors in simple

02:48:44.200 --> 02:48:44.800
pleasures

02:48:46.233 --> 02:48:50.133
with walking, picnicking, kite flying, and exploring our history.

02:48:50.567 --> 02:48:54.033
To me, this proposal is an elitist community

02:48:54.133 --> 02:48:57.934
destroyer that will, if passed, irreparably change the identity of South

02:48:58.033 --> 02:49:01.500
Portland. We can try other options,

02:49:01.934 --> 02:49:04.834
such as the SOS proposal.

02:49:06.000 --> 02:49:09.700
I lived in Long Island, Maine, and the council couldn't

02:49:09.734 --> 02:49:13.667
wait. They rubbed their hands together once they separated from

02:49:13.700 --> 02:49:17.567
Portland and thought, "We're going to make a lot of money on selling

02:49:17.634 --> 02:49:18.100
these lots."

02:49:18.133 --> 02:49:18.734
30 seconds.

02:49:18.800 --> 02:49:22.700
And then they hit oil because of tanks that were there buried.

02:49:22.834 --> 02:49:26.000
And people that put money down on them from out of state,

02:49:27.000 --> 02:49:30.900
they demanded their money back. The bottom line is that think about

02:49:30.934 --> 02:49:32.900
the Eastern Prom in Portland, Maine,

02:49:33.734 --> 02:49:36.600
a treasured community oceanside green space.

02:49:37.400 --> 02:49:41.066
If they had approved a plan like we are going to try to

02:49:41.100 --> 02:49:43.734
approve now, it wouldn't even exist.

02:49:44.834 --> 02:49:45.300
Thank you.

02:49:46.266 --> 02:49:47.400
Thank you very much.

02:49:49.200 --> 02:49:52.834
Good evening, Mayor, council members.

02:49:53.033 --> 02:49:56.300
My name is Thomas O'Boyle. I serve as the advocacy director for the Portland

02:49:56.367 --> 02:49:59.433
Regional Chamber, representing 150 businesses in South Portland.

02:50:00.800 --> 02:50:01.433
Can't hear you.

02:50:02.533 --> 02:50:02.834
Thank you.

02:50:03.734 --> 02:50:07.033
Yeah, my name is Thomas O'Boyle. I serve as the advocacy director for the Portland

02:50:07.100 --> 02:50:10.233
Regional Chamber, representing 150 businesses across South Portland.

02:50:10.634 --> 02:50:14.300
We appreciate the significant work that has gone into the comprehensive plan by the

02:50:14.333 --> 02:50:16.033
committee, staff, and residents.

02:50:16.600 --> 02:50:19.767
It's also important to be clear about what this plan is and what it is not.

02:50:20.300 --> 02:50:23.166
It is not a site-specific development approval.

02:50:23.333 --> 02:50:23.533
It is

02:50:24.700 --> 02:50:26.834
a policy framework that sets direction

02:50:27.634 --> 02:50:30.934
while preserving the council's discretion as individual projects come

02:50:31.000 --> 02:50:34.367
forward. From the chamber's perspective, the most important outcome

02:50:34.934 --> 02:50:38.300
is that the plan supports housing growth, mixed-use development, and economic

02:50:38.333 --> 02:50:42.233
activity in appropriate areas along corridors in Sable Oaks,

02:50:42.433 --> 02:50:45.166
Mill Creek, and parts of the Eastern Waterfront.

02:50:45.233 --> 02:50:49.200
These are places where thoughtful growth can occur while still respecting

02:50:49.233 --> 02:50:53.066
surrounding neighborhoods. On the Eastern Waterfront specifically,

02:50:53.166 --> 02:50:55.600
the plan does not enable unchecked development.

02:50:56.100 --> 02:50:58.734
It establishes a conditional framework.

02:50:59.066 --> 02:51:02.967
Any residential development would require further environmental review, including

02:51:03.000 --> 02:51:06.734
emissions and health impacts. This ensures decisions are

02:51:06.767 --> 02:51:10.400
guided by science and regulatory expertise while allowing the city to

02:51:10.433 --> 02:51:13.834
plan for productive application of underutilized land.

02:51:15.066 --> 02:51:17.467
That growth is essential to the city's fiscal health.

02:51:18.066 --> 02:51:22.000
South Portland is facing rising costs, difficult choices around school staffing

02:51:22.033 --> 02:51:25.133
and facilities, and significant long-term capital needs.

02:51:26.166 --> 02:51:29.967
Without expanding the tax base through new housing and commercial development,

02:51:30.400 --> 02:51:33.934
those costs are not avoided, they are shifted onto existing residents.

02:51:34.767 --> 02:51:38.433
More broadly, the plan establishes South Portland's growth path for the next

02:51:38.500 --> 02:51:42.367
15 years. The choice before you will shape whether the city remains

02:51:42.400 --> 02:51:46.333
accessible and economically resilient for the next generation.

02:51:46.367 --> 02:51:48.166
This plan strikes a reasonable balance.

02:51:48.634 --> 02:51:51.433
It supports diverse housing, grows the tax base,

02:51:52.266 --> 02:51:55.533
and addresses environmental and infrastructure concerns responsibly.

02:51:56.100 --> 02:51:59.567
For those reasons, we encourage the council to adopt the comprehensive plan and

02:51:59.600 --> 02:52:01.533
move forward with implementation. Thank you.

02:52:02.367 --> 02:52:03.767
Thank you very much.

02:52:08.066 --> 02:52:11.233
Do I get to use the rest of that time, too? Oh, no. Too bad. Okay. Hi.

02:52:11.467 --> 02:52:13.000
Claire Holman from Ferry Village.

02:52:13.800 --> 02:52:16.066
And I just want to start out by saying,

02:52:17.233 --> 02:52:21.100
I strongly believe that South Portland needs to remain

02:52:21.200 --> 02:52:24.900
a small city. Small cities are the nicest places to

02:52:24.967 --> 02:52:28.500
live, and I have it from the authority of none other

02:52:28.533 --> 02:52:32.500
than Councilor West, that in fact, all those

02:52:32.533 --> 02:52:35.667
places with great density, they don't have lower property taxes.

02:52:35.667 --> 02:52:37.600
One of our biggest burdens is the property tax.

02:52:38.133 --> 02:52:39.266
And sorry,

02:52:40.133 --> 02:52:42.000
I thanked you so much for giving me that information.

02:52:42.467 --> 02:52:42.700
And,

02:52:45.767 --> 02:52:49.033
so what we're looking at is we are depending on property tax, a

02:52:49.066 --> 02:52:52.867
regressive way to get money, and that it's forcing

02:52:52.867 --> 02:52:56.433
development on us. It's forcing us to use open spaces as a piggy bank for the

02:52:56.467 --> 02:52:56.767
future,

02:52:57.834 --> 02:53:01.834
and I don't think we should be. I want to ask, please, will the city

02:53:02.166 --> 02:53:06.033
leaders, the city council, insist that we direct

02:53:07.333 --> 02:53:11.133
the people who can do it, whether it's the city planners, which are economists, to

02:53:11.500 --> 02:53:15.333
really push harder for some other ways for us to get money besides

02:53:15.367 --> 02:53:17.233
property tax. It's going to sink us.

02:53:17.433 --> 02:53:20.367
It's going to force us to have things that we don't want, and already we're a

02:53:20.400 --> 02:53:21.667
densely populated area.

02:53:22.734 --> 02:53:25.967
And I definitely support the bringing back K Looper 27 and

02:53:26.734 --> 02:53:27.834
this SOS plan.

02:53:28.800 --> 02:53:31.033
Because just take a moment. Imagine Fort Williams.

02:53:31.066 --> 02:53:32.934
Imagine if a few hotels over there,

02:53:34.233 --> 02:53:36.634
a few houses, a couple six stories in the background.

02:53:36.900 --> 02:53:40.600
It would never be anything like what it is, and we're talking an even smaller area.

02:53:41.066 --> 02:53:43.900
It sounds like it's going to be kind of okay because there's going to be green

02:53:43.967 --> 02:53:47.400
space. I wonder how much green is going to be left.

02:53:47.800 --> 02:53:51.400
At least there's something, and I know that so much work went into this, but I

02:53:51.433 --> 02:53:54.233
really question the wisdom. Also,

02:53:55.266 --> 02:53:59.166
will the city council please assure that if we're going to

02:53:59.233 --> 02:54:02.333
have corridors, because corridors means that buildings are going to be lining all

02:54:02.367 --> 02:54:04.867
those streets. Okay? That's like a city.

02:54:05.133 --> 02:54:07.600
Many cities have buildings lining all the main streets.

02:54:08.433 --> 02:54:11.634
We need trees. This is New England. Tree-lined streets.

02:54:11.900 --> 02:54:15.767
Not those little things that if you bump into it with your car, one plow hit,

02:54:15.800 --> 02:54:18.400
it's over. Okay? We need actual trees.

02:54:18.700 --> 02:54:21.867
Yes, there's some work, and they actually give us something that you can't get

02:54:21.934 --> 02:54:25.166
back. So I want to know, will the city council ensure that we're going to have

02:54:25.166 --> 02:54:27.634
actual trees along these so-called corridors?

02:54:28.300 --> 02:54:30.600
Just go over, I'll spend a lot of time in Red Bank these days.

02:54:30.867 --> 02:54:34.567
Those two new Avesta buildings, they have these little, one plow hit

02:54:34.867 --> 02:54:36.634
things in the front of them. They're not nice buildings.

02:54:36.667 --> 02:54:38.900
They're the typical, "Here, poor people live in this." Sorry.

02:54:38.967 --> 02:54:42.133
I'm sure they're probably perfectly adequate, 100% adequate, but they're not

02:54:42.166 --> 02:54:45.166
beautiful, okay? But if they had at least real trees in front of them, it wouldn't

02:54:45.233 --> 02:54:45.533
matter.

02:54:46.467 --> 02:54:50.300
So, thank you very much for that, and I just want to say

02:54:50.367 --> 02:54:50.634
that

02:54:51.834 --> 02:54:51.900
we

02:54:52.867 --> 02:54:56.667
just really need to drive forward in a way that doesn't force us always to sell

02:54:56.700 --> 02:54:58.767
ourselves out so we can get more property taxes.

02:54:58.967 --> 02:55:01.700
It's really a shame what we might end up doing to ourselves, because

02:55:02.567 --> 02:55:05.600
it doesn't end. There's no end to it. Just goes on forever.

02:55:06.467 --> 02:55:06.800
Thank you.

02:55:08.033 --> 02:55:09.266
Thank you very much.

02:55:13.967 --> 02:55:17.767
My name is Marianne Hill. I'm an economist and a resident of

02:55:17.800 --> 02:55:21.266
South Portland, and I've been very impressed by the discussion tonight.

02:55:21.867 --> 02:55:25.600
I think that both the city council and all the residents of the city would

02:55:25.667 --> 02:55:29.600
welcome a wonderful expanded park at Bug Light

02:55:29.634 --> 02:55:33.367
Park if there were private donations that funded

02:55:33.400 --> 02:55:37.166
it. So I've been looking at the financial feasibility

02:55:37.300 --> 02:55:38.600
of such a park.

02:55:39.467 --> 02:55:43.200
And I believe that it is possible and reasonable to

02:55:43.233 --> 02:55:47.066
expect that adequate funding could be found from private donors,

02:55:47.367 --> 02:55:50.800
especially if there were the moral support of the city

02:55:51.133 --> 02:55:52.900
behind it.

02:55:55.133 --> 02:55:58.634
An expanded park, in fact, of course, would bring vast

02:55:58.667 --> 02:56:02.567
economic benefit to the city, raises the city's ranking

02:56:02.634 --> 02:56:05.467
in livability, which is important in attracting

02:56:06.233 --> 02:56:10.133
businesses and high-skilled employees who

02:56:10.233 --> 02:56:13.533
value cultural attractions and green

02:56:13.967 --> 02:56:14.700
spaces.

02:56:16.333 --> 02:56:20.266
And of course, I agree with the last speaker, there is a great need for tax

02:56:20.300 --> 02:56:23.800
reform so that cities have more options for raising funds.

02:56:24.667 --> 02:56:28.567
But I want to point out an important alternative that the city of Portland is

02:56:28.600 --> 02:56:31.800
now piloting, a tax on nonprofits.

02:56:34.500 --> 02:56:38.166
We have 288 nonprofits here who

02:56:38.233 --> 02:56:38.567
have

02:56:39.700 --> 02:56:43.600
hundreds of millions of dollars in property with income of

02:56:43.667 --> 02:56:47.567
over half a billion per year. A small property tax

02:56:47.667 --> 02:56:51.400
on these nonprofits would make a huge difference to the city, bring in

02:56:51.467 --> 02:56:55.333
much more revenue than what you would get by development right around the

02:56:55.367 --> 02:56:56.567
Bug Light Park area.

02:56:57.600 --> 02:57:01.400
And let's look at what's some numbers regarding the current city

02:57:01.500 --> 02:57:05.300
budget. The city budget last year was 151

02:57:05.333 --> 02:57:06.667
million, more or less.

02:57:07.567 --> 02:57:11.300
Development near the park might bring in three or four million.

02:57:11.867 --> 02:57:15.734
However, along with that additional revenue, you'd have

02:57:15.834 --> 02:57:17.166
additional expenses.

02:57:19.967 --> 02:57:21.967
Once you take out those additional expenses,

02:57:23.767 --> 02:57:27.400
you would get about $2.5 million, which is

02:57:27.867 --> 02:57:30.133
less than 2% of the city's budget.

02:57:31.000 --> 02:57:34.767
Excuse me. And in addition, you have the problem of sea

02:57:34.867 --> 02:57:38.734
level rise remediation, which costs $100 million,

02:57:39.066 --> 02:57:43.000
actually a bit more than that, over 50 years, which is about 2 million per

02:57:43.100 --> 02:57:46.433
year. So with that in mind, you hardly make any-

02:57:47.667 --> 02:57:48.133
30 seconds

02:57:48.166 --> 02:57:49.133
... funds.

02:57:51.066 --> 02:57:54.900
And of course, the park itself could generate a lot of

02:57:55.166 --> 02:57:58.867
revenue. You could have an expanded museum that would

02:57:59.133 --> 02:58:00.266
bring visitors.

02:58:01.100 --> 02:58:01.400
You could

02:58:03.333 --> 02:58:07.266
host weddings and various fairs. Look at the 100,000 people that

02:58:07.300 --> 02:58:09.734
come to the Yarmouth Clam Festival.

02:58:09.767 --> 02:58:13.133
There are lots of options for fundraising with a wonderful park.

02:58:13.634 --> 02:58:16.800
And the legacy of our city would be expanded.

02:58:17.533 --> 02:58:17.734
Time.

02:58:17.767 --> 02:58:20.266
It would tell the history and it would

02:58:21.867 --> 02:58:22.066
Bring

02:58:23.133 --> 02:58:26.867
the city together as we celebrate the history of shipbuilding and our

02:58:26.934 --> 02:58:28.200
work during World War II.

02:58:29.100 --> 02:58:30.200
Thank you very much.

02:58:33.400 --> 02:58:33.567
Hi.

02:58:34.433 --> 02:58:36.533
My name is Liz Torocca. I live on Reynolds Street.

02:58:37.400 --> 02:58:41.133
And I have a question, and I'll ask for forgiveness if

02:58:41.600 --> 02:58:45.133
the answer to this question is somewhere in the plan that I haven't read.

02:58:45.834 --> 02:58:46.700
So sorry about that.

02:58:47.600 --> 02:58:49.200
My question is about affordable housing

02:58:50.200 --> 02:58:53.934
and how the plan, or if the plan,

02:58:54.533 --> 02:58:57.800
presents a citywide

02:58:58.600 --> 02:59:02.433
approach to affordable housing in

02:59:02.500 --> 02:59:03.400
terms of

02:59:05.333 --> 02:59:08.900
suggesting or prescribing percentages of housing

02:59:09.100 --> 02:59:12.133
that should be considered affordable, how

02:59:12.233 --> 02:59:14.066
affordability is

02:59:15.100 --> 02:59:18.500
calculated and whether that

02:59:19.200 --> 02:59:23.133
is a comprehensive plan domain or it's

02:59:23.166 --> 02:59:25.367
something that the city council

02:59:26.400 --> 02:59:30.233
would set policy around a goal for that in our

02:59:30.266 --> 02:59:31.200
city. Thank you.

02:59:32.700 --> 02:59:33.900
Thank you very much.

02:59:37.400 --> 02:59:39.533
Hello, everyone. My name is Angie Helton.

02:59:39.800 --> 02:59:43.467
I have lived on Bergen Street for more than 20 years.

02:59:44.200 --> 02:59:48.133
I have a PR consulting firm that I've had for a couple of decades, and I currently

02:59:48.200 --> 02:59:51.767
am the president of the South Portland-Cape Elizabeth Chamber of Commerce board of

02:59:51.834 --> 02:59:52.300
directors.

02:59:53.133 --> 02:59:56.800
First, thank you all, to the council for your service and to the planning board

02:59:56.867 --> 03:00:00.834
members and staff for the time, effort, and dedication you've brought to developing

03:00:01.367 --> 03:00:03.000
a comprehensive plan for our city.

03:00:03.900 --> 03:00:07.600
This has been an extensive process involving thoughtful discussion and

03:00:07.700 --> 03:00:11.200
careful consideration by a group of residents who want the best

03:00:11.400 --> 03:00:13.000
possible future for South Portland.

03:00:13.667 --> 03:00:17.333
For that reason, I'm here to support moving this plan forward and to encourage its

03:00:17.400 --> 03:00:18.333
adoption tonight.

03:00:19.266 --> 03:00:22.900
It's important to note that adopting the plan does not lock in every detail.

03:00:23.433 --> 03:00:27.367
Many components will still come before you through future ordinances.

03:00:27.400 --> 03:00:31.400
This plan provides guidance. It doesn't override existing rules or

03:00:31.433 --> 03:00:33.800
require you to adopt every recommendation.

03:00:34.567 --> 03:00:37.600
As we all know, South Portland needs to strengthen its tax base.

03:00:38.133 --> 03:00:41.634
The city's financial challenges are not on hold, and delaying the

03:00:41.700 --> 03:00:45.667
implementation of a plan that establishes a solid framework for housing

03:00:45.700 --> 03:00:49.133
and economic development only makes those challenges harder to address.

03:00:50.000 --> 03:00:50.667
For these reasons,

03:00:52.533 --> 03:00:55.900
I, as a longtime resident, a local business owner, and a chamber member,

03:00:56.333 --> 03:00:59.767
respectfully ask you to approve the plan as presented this evening.

03:01:00.400 --> 03:01:02.066
Thank you again for your time and your good work.

03:01:02.333 --> 03:01:04.500
I was going for shortest testimony. I think I won.

03:01:05.233 --> 03:01:07.133
You may have. Thank you so much.

03:01:07.200 --> 03:01:07.700
I favor it.

03:01:11.433 --> 03:01:15.266
You're about to start. Let me just check in on the online situation.

03:01:15.300 --> 03:01:16.033
What were you saying?

03:01:16.166 --> 03:01:19.100
We do have a hand raise online from Debbie Davis.

03:01:19.533 --> 03:01:20.433
Okay.

03:01:21.567 --> 03:01:21.667
Debbie-

03:01:21.667 --> 03:01:23.000
If you want her to go first, that's fine

03:01:23.133 --> 03:01:25.600
... would you-- Okay, let's let this person know that we are

03:01:26.500 --> 03:01:27.634
taking online

03:01:28.500 --> 03:01:29.033
comment.

03:01:29.800 --> 03:01:33.400
So Debbie Davis, if you could unmute yourself and go ahead.

03:01:33.433 --> 03:01:34.166
You have three minutes.

03:01:39.000 --> 03:01:39.567
Is she there?

03:01:41.333 --> 03:01:41.834
How's that?

03:01:42.133 --> 03:01:43.033
Yep. Good.

03:01:45.000 --> 03:01:45.567
Can you hear me?

03:01:45.900 --> 03:01:46.200
Yes.

03:01:47.333 --> 03:01:48.333
Okay. Thank you.

03:01:49.533 --> 03:01:53.333
Thanks for all the hard work, every city council.

03:01:54.667 --> 03:01:55.066
Hello?

03:01:56.734 --> 03:01:58.667
We can still hear you.

03:01:58.800 --> 03:02:00.133
Comprehensive plan.

03:02:01.667 --> 03:02:02.200
The whole

03:02:03.100 --> 03:02:05.667
community, thousands of hours, everybody.

03:02:06.634 --> 03:02:07.500
Thanks for that.

03:02:08.400 --> 03:02:12.266
I want to echo Claire's value of a small

03:02:12.433 --> 03:02:12.800
city

03:02:13.800 --> 03:02:15.066
as a bottom line.

03:02:15.900 --> 03:02:16.700
I also want to

03:02:16.834 --> 03:02:20.734
give kudos

03:02:20.767 --> 03:02:24.433
to Mariette Hill. She's a very experienced economist,

03:02:24.834 --> 03:02:28.634
and she's done a lot of assessment around the financial feasibility

03:02:29.033 --> 03:02:33.033
of SOS's plan. And I would also like to put a vote in for

03:02:33.200 --> 03:02:33.800
SOS's

03:02:35.333 --> 03:02:37.333
plan and supporting

03:02:38.467 --> 03:02:42.300
their plan for Memorial Park. It could be a legacy,

03:02:42.734 --> 03:02:43.934
a historical

03:02:45.600 --> 03:02:49.367
place, very powerful. If anyone's been to UNESCO sites

03:02:49.934 --> 03:02:53.400
around the world, these are really amazing

03:02:53.500 --> 03:02:54.000
places.

03:02:54.967 --> 03:02:58.400
This is an incredible gem. We have

03:02:58.600 --> 03:03:01.166
access to coastal green space,

03:03:02.133 --> 03:03:05.667
which benefits us all immensely in so many ways.

03:03:05.734 --> 03:03:09.600
It's priceless. It increases our mental and physical health and

03:03:09.634 --> 03:03:12.834
well-being of residents, which is right in the mission

03:03:13.000 --> 03:03:14.667
statement in the beginning.

03:03:16.533 --> 03:03:19.200
I've talked to a lot of young people around us,

03:03:20.500 --> 03:03:24.233
and they don't need to live right on the coast, they just need

03:03:24.400 --> 03:03:27.400
access. A big green space,

03:03:28.533 --> 03:03:29.133
just like

03:03:30.600 --> 03:03:32.634
various areas, Eastern Prom,

03:03:33.600 --> 03:03:37.367
Fort Williams. These places are amazing, and they do

03:03:37.433 --> 03:03:41.367
generate income. And it invigorates the whole town,

03:03:42.433 --> 03:03:44.233
including economically.

03:03:45.033 --> 03:03:47.000
And it does not make sense,

03:03:47.934 --> 03:03:49.767
financially or otherwise,

03:03:50.800 --> 03:03:54.000
to develop in areas with toxic

03:03:54.433 --> 03:03:54.834
soil,

03:03:56.266 --> 03:03:59.800
near tanks, tank hazards,

03:04:00.333 --> 03:04:04.200
at the end of a peninsula with traffic issues

03:04:04.233 --> 03:04:07.800
and congestion already, in areas

03:04:07.900 --> 03:04:11.467
prone to compound flooding and sea level rise,

03:04:11.533 --> 03:04:15.400
and-Problems with an aging infrastructure around sewer and

03:04:15.500 --> 03:04:19.367
groundwater. Come on, folks. Let's think of

03:04:19.433 --> 03:04:23.400
looking all over the city. I love the idea of

03:04:23.433 --> 03:04:26.467
having these small, little centers,

03:04:26.567 --> 03:04:30.367
downtowns. I think that the planning committee has had some

03:04:30.400 --> 03:04:33.000
really creative ideas around that.

03:04:33.300 --> 03:04:37.200
We just have to know where it makes sense to do all of these things.

03:04:37.800 --> 03:04:41.533
And I like Clea Holman's idea too, of expanding,

03:04:41.900 --> 03:04:45.734
instead of just property taxes, expanding other revenues through

03:04:45.767 --> 03:04:47.367
businesses, et cetera, like that.

03:04:48.166 --> 03:04:51.834
The business people speaking have a lot of passion. We have a lot of energy.

03:04:52.200 --> 03:04:52.500
Time.

03:04:52.533 --> 03:04:56.300
We need them in our town too. Thank you very much,

03:04:56.734 --> 03:04:57.133
all of you.

03:04:57.867 --> 03:04:59.233
Thank you very much.

03:05:00.233 --> 03:05:03.467
Okay, and thank you for your allowing that.

03:05:04.367 --> 03:05:04.533
Go.

03:05:05.433 --> 03:05:06.433
It's your turn.

03:05:06.533 --> 03:05:08.166
My turn. Hi, my name's Dave Burkey,

03:05:09.100 --> 03:05:13.100
225 High Street. I'm part of SOS. I'm here to

03:05:13.200 --> 03:05:16.867
ask the councilors to choose option three and to

03:05:16.934 --> 03:05:20.700
adopt CALEA LUPA 27. The first thing I'd like to do

03:05:20.834 --> 03:05:24.500
is to thank Misha Pride, Carter Scott, D**k

03:05:24.567 --> 03:05:27.834
Andrews, and Jessica Walker over the last week for

03:05:28.100 --> 03:05:30.166
meeting with me and us

03:05:31.200 --> 03:05:34.600
on holidays, when on vacation, out of country.

03:05:35.066 --> 03:05:38.533
I have been very impressed with the council and how

03:05:38.634 --> 03:05:42.467
responsive they are to me, a citizen, and so thank you for

03:05:42.567 --> 03:05:44.600
that. I'm very impressed.

03:05:45.700 --> 03:05:48.867
I would like you to imagine two scenarios.

03:05:49.533 --> 03:05:53.000
I want you to stand at Bug Light on the

03:05:53.066 --> 03:05:56.033
plateau in the year 2036.

03:05:56.867 --> 03:06:00.700
You are looking on a beautiful day like today out at Casco

03:06:00.734 --> 03:06:02.667
Bay, and you're looking at Diamond Island.

03:06:03.266 --> 03:06:06.767
You then pivot around to your left, and you look at Portland Harbor,

03:06:07.233 --> 03:06:11.033
and you look at the eastern prom. You look at the Maine

03:06:11.100 --> 03:06:15.066
State Pier, and then you pivot around, and you look west towards

03:06:15.133 --> 03:06:18.967
Fore River. Scenario one, you see

03:06:19.033 --> 03:06:19.533
condos,

03:06:20.367 --> 03:06:22.567
hotels, and residences

03:06:24.400 --> 03:06:27.133
lined up along Bug Light Park.

03:06:28.600 --> 03:06:29.867
How will that make you feel?

03:06:31.266 --> 03:06:34.634
These residences will not be low income.

03:06:35.133 --> 03:06:38.867
They have 720 degrees of water views from

03:06:38.934 --> 03:06:41.900
Portland to Casco Bay in the islands.

03:06:41.934 --> 03:06:45.400
These are going to be $2 to $4 million each.

03:06:45.467 --> 03:06:48.333
There's Radisson Hotels there. It's going to be 1,000 bucks a night.

03:06:49.000 --> 03:06:51.100
We're not looking at affordable housing here.

03:06:51.500 --> 03:06:54.900
We're looking at very high-end, wealthy housing.

03:06:55.967 --> 03:06:59.800
Scenario two, you turn around and you look at Fore River, and

03:06:59.800 --> 03:07:02.967
there's a park there. And in the middle of the park, there's a

03:07:03.000 --> 03:07:06.834
memorial to the efforts of folks at South Portland

03:07:06.867 --> 03:07:10.767
during World War II, victory at sea. Think about it.

03:07:12.200 --> 03:07:15.700
We should acknowledge that. It's not in the plan. It should be.

03:07:16.600 --> 03:07:19.233
The way to do that is with CALEA LUPA 27.

03:07:19.734 --> 03:07:23.567
The way to do that is to accept our history

03:07:24.033 --> 03:07:27.967
and listen to the folks as they come up to the mic, 10 to

03:07:28.066 --> 03:07:30.100
one, 20 to one, in favor of this.

03:07:34.400 --> 03:07:35.867
The last thing I'd like to say

03:07:37.400 --> 03:07:39.166
is that regardless of the vote tonight-

03:07:40.033 --> 03:07:40.033
30 seconds

03:07:40.033 --> 03:07:41.367
... regardless of the vote tonight,

03:07:43.066 --> 03:07:47.000
I have met with Dave Packard, and I'm actually optimistic

03:07:47.000 --> 03:07:50.734
that we can work with Dave and his sister, Jennifer, and come up

03:07:50.834 --> 03:07:54.767
with green space idea. But I would love to have the

03:07:54.934 --> 03:07:58.367
city of South Portland, the councilors, back that.

03:07:58.900 --> 03:07:59.066
Yeah.

03:08:00.900 --> 03:08:01.266
Thank you.

03:08:02.500 --> 03:08:03.667
Thank you very much.

03:08:11.033 --> 03:08:14.667
Good evening. My name is David Field. I'm at 64 Summit Street.

03:08:15.567 --> 03:08:19.200
I've basically thrown out everything that I had prepared to say,

03:08:19.867 --> 03:08:22.900
but what I do want to say is that I get it,

03:08:23.634 --> 03:08:27.400
is I get the fact that PK Realty owns this property,

03:08:28.033 --> 03:08:31.433
is that so many, we've got all these great plans.

03:08:31.500 --> 03:08:34.567
Let's build the shipyard. Let's do all of this.

03:08:35.433 --> 03:08:39.367
But I want to go back to the very beginning and what

03:08:39.400 --> 03:08:43.266
we are supposed to be. We're supposed to be a representative

03:08:43.400 --> 03:08:43.967
government.

03:08:44.767 --> 03:08:48.233
And, in the early days when PK first

03:08:48.333 --> 03:08:52.200
purchased this property, it was purchased as industrial.

03:08:52.767 --> 03:08:56.700
And you go back to the very beginning in their websites and the

03:08:56.800 --> 03:09:00.467
notes, it talks about how they were excited to

03:09:00.467 --> 03:09:04.266
have additional commercial space, and that was

03:09:04.300 --> 03:09:08.266
the business that they were in. And so, that

03:09:08.400 --> 03:09:12.033
was the direction that they said, and the properties

03:09:12.200 --> 03:09:13.567
fit into their business.

03:09:14.467 --> 03:09:18.433
Then you go forward a couple of years, and all of a

03:09:18.567 --> 03:09:21.634
sudden, we start having these huge development

03:09:22.266 --> 03:09:26.233
types of programs going on. And then you go take a look at

03:09:26.300 --> 03:09:29.934
some of the minutes of the meetings, and they talk about

03:09:29.967 --> 03:09:33.734
how, "Gee, we really like the plans that

03:09:33.800 --> 03:09:37.600
you're coming up with and the vision that you're putting

03:09:37.667 --> 03:09:41.166
forth for the city."And so then you keep going

03:09:41.233 --> 03:09:45.133
forward, and then all of a sudden you've got Yard

03:09:45.200 --> 03:09:49.100
South. And I think the people started saying, "Wait a

03:09:49.233 --> 03:09:53.066
second. Take a look at the hazards, the flooding, and

03:09:53.233 --> 03:09:56.533
all of that." But everything had been marching

03:09:56.734 --> 03:09:59.634
towards doing this big development.

03:09:59.934 --> 03:10:03.667
But the public, we talk about being representative, and I'd be

03:10:03.734 --> 03:10:07.166
curious too, as to how much of

03:10:08.000 --> 03:10:11.333
public input is saying, "We want these big buildings.

03:10:11.467 --> 03:10:15.233
We want this." Supposedly, I thought our

03:10:15.367 --> 03:10:19.333
government is supposed to, if we have got

03:10:19.400 --> 03:10:23.233
a strong feeling. Now, the good news is here is

03:10:23.333 --> 03:10:27.000
that if they had focused on, okay, let's take a

03:10:27.100 --> 03:10:30.834
look at what kind of flood-tolerant, low density,

03:10:31.333 --> 03:10:35.200
and those types of structures could be built out there

03:10:35.300 --> 03:10:38.967
within a modified, with that zoning that they

03:10:39.100 --> 03:10:42.900
bought it in. You could be really creative because

03:10:42.900 --> 03:10:44.200
there's so much need-

03:10:44.634 --> 03:10:48.600
30 seconds... for you take a look on a summer day, all the boats out there, there's so many.

03:10:48.700 --> 03:10:50.400
There could be some marine industry.

03:10:50.433 --> 03:10:54.266
But you build it low density, you don't have the impact

03:10:54.266 --> 03:10:57.433
that all of this development that it's had.

03:10:57.467 --> 03:11:01.100
The other issue I think is we're sort of ignoring is the flood issue,

03:11:01.700 --> 03:11:04.133
is what the base flood elevation is.

03:11:04.567 --> 03:11:08.200
You have down in Boston, all the areas flooded out there.

03:11:08.400 --> 03:11:11.734
So anyway, that's what I have to say. Thank you.

03:11:12.867 --> 03:11:14.000
Thank you very much.

03:11:16.900 --> 03:11:17.333
Okay.

03:11:20.634 --> 03:11:21.934
The line got longer behind me.

03:11:23.734 --> 03:11:25.667
Good evening. Reverend Allison Buttrick Patton.

03:11:25.934 --> 03:11:29.734
I am the senior pastor at First Congregational Church, United Church of

03:11:29.834 --> 03:11:30.400
Christ,

03:11:31.667 --> 03:11:34.600
the church on Meeting House Hill. And

03:11:35.533 --> 03:11:35.700
I

03:11:36.634 --> 03:11:39.900
want to add my voices to those who've expressed real

03:11:39.934 --> 03:11:43.867
appreciation for the energy, the time, the creativity that has

03:11:43.900 --> 03:11:47.834
been invested in this process over

03:11:47.900 --> 03:11:48.700
the last

03:11:49.900 --> 03:11:50.600
many months,

03:11:51.667 --> 03:11:55.567
and just share for a moment about a tiny part

03:11:55.567 --> 03:11:59.367
that we have played and therefore, a window we have into this

03:11:59.400 --> 03:12:02.734
process. About a year ago, First Congregational Church had the

03:12:02.800 --> 03:12:06.200
privilege of hosting a community forum in

03:12:06.233 --> 03:12:09.967
collaboration with the citizens' effort that's called Toss the Tanks, that's been

03:12:10.000 --> 03:12:11.967
lifted up once this evening already.

03:12:12.934 --> 03:12:16.900
At that forum, we gathered close to 100 South Portland participants

03:12:17.800 --> 03:12:21.700
and asked what might be possible if the Hill Street tank farm was

03:12:21.734 --> 03:12:25.700
removed and the 100 acres of property somehow

03:12:25.800 --> 03:12:26.634
redeveloped.

03:12:27.500 --> 03:12:31.000
SMRT Architects of Portland created a theoretical

03:12:31.100 --> 03:12:33.967
design for the space to help us imagine the

03:12:34.066 --> 03:12:35.100
possibilities.

03:12:36.000 --> 03:12:39.634
And at the round table discussions after that presentation, there was

03:12:39.800 --> 03:12:43.166
so much positive energy, and I think an

03:12:43.200 --> 03:12:46.800
appreciation to be invited into imagining

03:12:47.333 --> 03:12:50.467
all of the different ways that we can build community

03:12:50.467 --> 03:12:51.700
together.

03:12:53.066 --> 03:12:54.100
I want to acknowledge

03:12:55.734 --> 03:12:58.100
the values that you lifted up

03:12:59.367 --> 03:13:01.066
at the very beginning of this presentation.

03:13:01.667 --> 03:13:05.266
In particular, the commitment to climate resilience and to social

03:13:05.300 --> 03:13:09.266
vitality, because those are values that we embrace and talk about all the time

03:13:09.266 --> 03:13:12.867
at First Congregational Church. It's really exciting to see

03:13:12.967 --> 03:13:15.900
those elevated as part of this

03:13:15.900 --> 03:13:19.800
discussion. We heard interest in those

03:13:19.867 --> 03:13:22.634
values mentioned in the room that night.

03:13:22.667 --> 03:13:25.200
We also heard housing.

03:13:26.100 --> 03:13:29.867
It emerged as one of the top priorities as we talked together, which is no

03:13:29.967 --> 03:13:31.967
surprise. You all have heard that as well.

03:13:33.100 --> 03:13:36.967
If anyone's curious, the whole SMRT presentation is

03:13:37.066 --> 03:13:40.533
still online at tossthetanks.com,

03:13:41.100 --> 03:13:42.033
and you can take a look.

03:13:43.033 --> 03:13:46.934
What I just wanted to lift up is how exciting it is to see a

03:13:47.000 --> 03:13:50.700
comprehensive plan that includes the potential to transform

03:13:51.400 --> 03:13:55.100
the Hill Street tank farm into a mixed-use neighborhood with a focus

03:13:55.700 --> 03:13:56.734
on housing.

03:13:57.533 --> 03:13:57.567
30 seconds.

03:13:57.600 --> 03:13:58.000
And

03:13:59.500 --> 03:14:02.133
it's so clear that the work we have to do to implement is

03:14:02.166 --> 03:14:05.100
considerable, but the vision is worth pursuing,

03:14:05.934 --> 03:14:09.900
and we look forward to partnering in whatever way we can as we hold onto

03:14:09.934 --> 03:14:13.233
those values that you have lifted up from the outset.

03:14:13.300 --> 03:14:13.967
Thank you so much.

03:14:15.066 --> 03:14:16.233
Thank you very much.

03:14:18.133 --> 03:14:18.600
Okay.

03:14:19.934 --> 03:14:23.734
Good evening. Kathy Chapman, Beaufort Street, and thank you,

03:14:24.567 --> 03:14:28.533
counselors and Mayor, and of course, the CPC that did lots

03:14:28.600 --> 03:14:28.800
of

03:14:29.634 --> 03:14:32.734
years and hours of hard work on this

03:14:33.033 --> 03:14:33.634
proposal

03:14:35.233 --> 03:14:37.100
draft comprehensive plan.

03:14:39.767 --> 03:14:43.700
But I do think, and while I think that most of the plan I agree with,

03:14:44.233 --> 03:14:48.200
that's probably 90%, maybe 99%, I do think that it

03:14:48.233 --> 03:14:51.700
needs some revision in the shipyard zone.

03:14:53.300 --> 03:14:53.867
The public,

03:14:54.700 --> 03:14:58.634
as you know, as you've heard tonight, and as when we had Yard South,

03:15:00.500 --> 03:15:02.600
Barbara D. presented for us

03:15:03.433 --> 03:15:06.367
the petition with 2,500 signatures.

03:15:06.367 --> 03:15:08.734
And everyone tonight, I would say, would agree

03:15:09.734 --> 03:15:13.400
that that petition said no residential in the shipyard with the

03:15:13.433 --> 03:15:17.033
Chalupa, which was, I guess, 27. I guess it's now 22

03:15:17.700 --> 03:15:20.667
under the new updated version.

03:15:22.100 --> 03:15:25.700
The other two pieces to that were a low

03:15:25.767 --> 03:15:29.700
density, low growth, which was reflected in the survey that was done

03:15:29.700 --> 03:15:31.800
actually by the planning, although they

03:15:33.133 --> 03:15:33.533
seem to

03:15:34.567 --> 03:15:38.467
analyze it differently. But that 60% of the people said they wanted low growth

03:15:38.500 --> 03:15:41.634
in that area.... and being flood-tolerant

03:15:42.133 --> 03:15:42.634
uses

03:15:44.133 --> 03:15:47.634
because that area, as many speakers have said, and as we know through

03:15:47.767 --> 03:15:51.600
science and climate change in your very own South Portland flood

03:15:51.700 --> 03:15:55.000
maps, that there will be flooding in that area.

03:15:55.100 --> 03:15:57.734
The CPC chose to use 3'9",

03:15:58.834 --> 03:15:59.800
but you can

03:16:00.700 --> 03:16:04.600
give the statistics on that on how when I

03:16:04.634 --> 03:16:05.533
was at that meeting,

03:16:07.500 --> 03:16:09.600
given that 3'9"

03:16:10.734 --> 03:16:14.133
scenario, is considerable flooding, and I think it was

03:16:14.500 --> 03:16:18.300
more than once every two weeks. That's a lot

03:16:19.800 --> 03:16:20.000
of

03:16:21.734 --> 03:16:25.333
water. I'm thinking a foot, but I can be corrected by Milan, I'm sure, that can

03:16:25.367 --> 03:16:27.533
give the actual statistics on that flooding.

03:16:27.567 --> 03:16:30.033
But that was considered okay by the committee.

03:16:30.634 --> 03:16:33.934
I personally don't think that's okay for housing

03:16:34.600 --> 03:16:35.300
in that area.

03:16:36.600 --> 03:16:37.800
And I would prefer

03:16:38.834 --> 03:16:42.200
to see, as we had already seen before,

03:16:43.634 --> 03:16:46.934
the marine uses be predominant and

03:16:47.000 --> 03:16:49.533
recreational marine-related uses

03:16:50.500 --> 03:16:54.333
that could include restaurants, marinas, fishermen, lobstermen, piers,

03:16:55.300 --> 03:16:59.133
recreation, that they used in the past, and is part of our Maine

03:16:59.166 --> 03:17:03.100
tradition. I'm a native Mainer, and so I really feel strongly that we

03:17:03.133 --> 03:17:07.100
should continue that heritage that was in South Portland and in

03:17:07.533 --> 03:17:10.934
Maine as a whole. It's kind of our brand in Maine.

03:17:12.400 --> 03:17:14.133
I'm also concerned with bug light.

03:17:14.266 --> 03:17:18.033
So not having the residential is a key thing, not having high

03:17:18.100 --> 03:17:21.734
growth. We are the highest density city in the state,

03:17:22.300 --> 03:17:24.834
or second to Portland, depending on who you ask.

03:17:25.300 --> 03:17:25.500
Time.

03:17:25.900 --> 03:17:28.400
So we certainly have a lot of density. I'm not against housing.

03:17:28.433 --> 03:17:32.400
In fact, I'm pro-housing. That's why I'm against residential in this area, because

03:17:32.467 --> 03:17:34.133
we don't want housing that will be flooded-

03:17:34.467 --> 03:17:34.667
Your time has ended

03:17:34.667 --> 03:17:38.567
... a 50% chance in 50 years. So we can't do that,

03:17:38.700 --> 03:17:41.767
so please do the right thing for our future. Thank you.

03:17:43.233 --> 03:17:45.600
Next doctor.

03:17:49.400 --> 03:17:53.200
Priscilla Scary, a naturopathic doctors of South Portland.

03:17:55.567 --> 03:17:59.133
The human exposure model must be adopted by the tank farm owners.

03:17:59.834 --> 03:18:03.634
In your review of the 2040 comprehensive plan, it's in a current form.

03:18:03.934 --> 03:18:07.600
Please understand the problem of designating most of the shipyard district as

03:18:07.700 --> 03:18:11.333
unique high growth. This is the opposite of the

03:18:11.367 --> 03:18:14.900
expressed desire of South Portland residents, especially those living close

03:18:15.967 --> 03:18:18.900
to the tank hazards and the possibility of floods.

03:18:19.700 --> 03:18:23.066
As a South Portland naturopathic doctor, I've talked to you before about the need

03:18:23.166 --> 03:18:26.967
to acquire tank farms near residents to adhere to the VOC

03:18:27.033 --> 03:18:30.734
human exposure model, known as the HEM model, and

03:18:30.800 --> 03:18:33.300
establish appropriate buffers. Why?

03:18:34.033 --> 03:18:38.000
Well, you are about to make public policy that will affect people's exposure to

03:18:38.033 --> 03:18:39.533
cancer-causing compounds.

03:18:40.400 --> 03:18:40.834
In a letter

03:18:41.700 --> 03:18:45.400
I sent to Portland Press last year, I expressed a need to insist on proper buffers

03:18:45.400 --> 03:18:49.100
between industry and home, and mentioned why, given the work that the

03:18:49.133 --> 03:18:52.867
community had done over the previous decade to protect public health from the

03:18:52.934 --> 03:18:56.600
unknown 40 additional tons of health-harming volatile organic

03:18:56.667 --> 03:19:00.200
compounds, otherwise known as VOCs, such as benzene and

03:19:00.233 --> 03:19:02.800
neurotoxins like ethylbenzene and xylene.

03:19:04.367 --> 03:19:06.100
But back then, the citizens

03:19:08.100 --> 03:19:11.834
achieved a victory via the Clear Skies Ordinance, stopping tar sands from coming

03:19:11.867 --> 03:19:15.767
into South Portland. Then fence line monitoring was worked for after

03:19:15.900 --> 03:19:18.467
three EPA lawsuits against the tank farm allowing

03:19:19.333 --> 03:19:22.800
emissions violations. These weren't abstract concerns.

03:19:22.800 --> 03:19:26.533
They're about preventing respiratory, neurological, and immune system impacts on

03:19:26.567 --> 03:19:30.166
citizens. While no model is perfect, the HEM model was good

03:19:30.233 --> 03:19:32.433
enough to support passage of state law.

03:19:32.867 --> 03:19:36.133
In my opinion, it's clear that the HEM model is a very good

03:19:36.200 --> 03:19:38.700
approximation of what the neighborhood is likely

03:19:40.700 --> 03:19:43.600
increase in cancer and other disease rates would be.

03:19:44.200 --> 03:19:48.133
The science hasn't changed. Benzene still causes cancer, and especially leukemia in

03:19:48.200 --> 03:19:52.100
children. Ethylbenzene still damages the nervous system, and toluene still

03:19:52.367 --> 03:19:56.300
harms childhood development. What our actions changed back

03:19:56.333 --> 03:19:58.700
then was our willingness to ignore these facts.

03:19:59.133 --> 03:20:02.634
Demand that new residential zones not be allowed near tank farms.

03:20:02.934 --> 03:20:06.533
We must continue this. We must insist that tank farm

03:20:06.600 --> 03:20:10.500
owners recognize the harm that comes from the toxic fumes in these tanks, and

03:20:10.634 --> 03:20:14.567
yes, from other sources, too. But adding the HEM model and insisting

03:20:14.600 --> 03:20:18.533
that the fossil fuel industry adhere to this, must be done if we,

03:20:18.567 --> 03:20:22.433
as citizens, matter to this corporate world we are all a part of.

03:20:22.867 --> 03:20:23.200
Thank you.

03:20:24.600 --> 03:20:25.700
Thank you very much.

03:20:31.700 --> 03:20:34.033
Georgia Deveras, Victory Avenue.

03:20:35.033 --> 03:20:36.600
Good evening, Mayor and councilors.

03:20:38.667 --> 03:20:40.033
Two minutes.

03:20:40.133 --> 03:20:41.834
Good evening, Mayor and councilors.

03:20:42.433 --> 03:20:46.200
I've spent the last two-plus years alongside many of my neighbors,

03:20:46.333 --> 03:20:49.834
attending meetings and following the comprehensive plan process.

03:20:50.533 --> 03:20:54.433
I'm here tonight not to rehash the friction of the past, but to talk about

03:20:54.433 --> 03:20:57.734
the city we love and how we move forward together.

03:20:57.767 --> 03:21:01.700
For over 20 meetings in more than two years, many of us have shown up to comp plan

03:21:01.734 --> 03:21:05.300
meetings, sent detailed emails, conducted and shared

03:21:05.433 --> 03:21:08.600
in-depth research. We didn't do this to be difficult.

03:21:08.700 --> 03:21:12.567
We did it because we care. But I want to be honest with you,

03:21:12.634 --> 03:21:15.400
at times, it has been a heartbreaking process.

03:21:16.033 --> 03:21:19.266
There was a profound sense of a void where the public's voice should have

03:21:19.300 --> 03:21:23.233
been. When 80 residents show up to speak and are silenced, or when

03:21:23.433 --> 03:21:27.367
state statutes have to be read from the podium, it indicates a wound in

03:21:27.400 --> 03:21:30.133
our local democracy that we now have the chance to

03:21:30.200 --> 03:21:34.166
healOn June 12th, 2025, the council

03:21:34.233 --> 03:21:38.000
gave a clear directive to the planning department to reduce the growth level for

03:21:38.066 --> 03:21:41.934
the entire shipyard. However, the 2040 plan only

03:21:41.967 --> 03:21:45.233
applies that request to a small fraction of the area.

03:21:45.767 --> 03:21:49.233
Instead, the rest of the shipyard is designated as D,

03:21:49.700 --> 03:21:53.367
unique high growth, the highest growth level on page eight.

03:21:53.967 --> 03:21:57.667
We are asking for a plan that respects the physical limits of the land

03:21:57.967 --> 03:22:00.266
and protects the health and safety of the community.

03:22:01.367 --> 03:22:04.734
It has been difficult to watch the disparity in how voices are heard.

03:22:05.367 --> 03:22:07.567
As residents, we aren't looking for special treatment.

03:22:07.634 --> 03:22:10.100
We are simply looking for a seat at the table.

03:22:10.700 --> 03:22:14.266
Our research and our lived experience is an asset to this city,

03:22:14.700 --> 03:22:18.000
and yet our attempted contributions were openly mocked and

03:22:18.066 --> 03:22:20.066
disregarded by some committee members.

03:22:20.567 --> 03:22:24.467
Meeting after meeting, the committee and planning witnessed this,

03:22:24.767 --> 03:22:26.800
and nobody stood up for us.

03:22:27.667 --> 03:22:31.367
Despite the challenges of the last three years, something

03:22:31.433 --> 03:22:35.100
beautiful has reemerged. I want to point you toward CAELUPA

03:22:35.266 --> 03:22:39.066
27, the Eastern Waterfront Marine Protection and Heritage Area.

03:22:39.734 --> 03:22:43.266
This proposal isn't just a redrawn map, it is a labor of

03:22:43.400 --> 03:22:43.800
love.

03:22:44.600 --> 03:22:48.400
This vision for CAELUPA 27 is a product of collective

03:22:48.467 --> 03:22:52.033
imagination, refined and clarified through research and

03:22:52.100 --> 03:22:55.367
analysis, and a shared commitment to our community's future.

03:22:56.300 --> 03:23:00.066
It addresses the dangerous tank emissions, the traffic, the brownfields,

03:23:00.133 --> 03:23:03.934
flooding, and sea level rise, and aligns with the stated priorities of the

03:23:04.000 --> 03:23:07.900
plan. It is a profoundly meaningful, and I believe beautiful

03:23:08.000 --> 03:23:11.800
vision and solution for the shipyard area, and a legacy for this city

03:23:11.867 --> 03:23:12.367
council.

03:23:13.200 --> 03:23:17.000
Many of us feel a bit damaged by the toxicity of this plan's process,

03:23:17.133 --> 03:23:20.767
but we are still here because we love our city.

03:23:20.800 --> 03:23:24.667
Restore the public trust, adopt a plan that reflects the community it is

03:23:24.700 --> 03:23:28.600
meant to support and protect. Let's embrace the vision laid out in CAELUPA

03:23:28.767 --> 03:23:32.567
27. Please delay your vote and adopt the Eastern

03:23:32.600 --> 03:23:35.634
Waterfront Marine Protection and Heritage Area into this plan.

03:23:36.033 --> 03:23:36.233
Time.

03:23:36.300 --> 03:23:39.533
Thank you for listening, and thank you for your careful consideration.

03:23:41.667 --> 03:23:42.967
Thank you very much.

03:23:46.200 --> 03:23:50.066
Hi, my name is Prudence Bean, and I live on Henley

03:23:50.166 --> 03:23:54.000
Street. And I've lived here about 16 years.

03:23:55.200 --> 03:23:58.600
Obviously, I'm here because I do support the idea of

03:23:58.967 --> 03:24:01.767
leaving Bug Light as a park. It's very important.

03:24:02.266 --> 03:24:06.166
I think about Counselor Walker talking about the

03:24:06.200 --> 03:24:09.467
people who rent homes on the west side of the city.

03:24:09.500 --> 03:24:09.634
This

03:24:10.734 --> 03:24:14.367
area is an area to come to within South Portland to enjoy being

03:24:14.433 --> 03:24:15.066
outside.

03:24:16.834 --> 03:24:20.266
So, and I think this is a legacy that we want to leave for our children and

03:24:20.367 --> 03:24:24.066
grandchildren, this open area, one of the last few open areas

03:24:24.367 --> 03:24:27.400
truly on the coast of Maine that's accessible.

03:24:27.467 --> 03:24:30.800
And I want to just tell you about two instances.

03:24:31.000 --> 03:24:31.333
I used to

03:24:32.266 --> 03:24:36.066
be the treasurer of the Maine Lobster Festival, and we had

03:24:36.166 --> 03:24:40.100
one last piece of open oceanfront land in Rockland on

03:24:40.133 --> 03:24:44.000
Main Street. And we bought that land and deeded it to the

03:24:44.066 --> 03:24:48.033
city because we wanted to make sure that there was still open land for

03:24:48.166 --> 03:24:52.100
everybody to use. And then I also want to point out,

03:24:52.567 --> 03:24:55.867
Coastal Maine Botanical Gardens started as an

03:24:55.967 --> 03:24:59.800
effort of families who said they didn't want to see development.

03:25:00.133 --> 03:25:03.800
They wanted to put something in that was going to draw people to Boothbay,

03:25:04.400 --> 03:25:08.033
and they mortgaged their homes to buy the original land that the

03:25:08.066 --> 03:25:11.734
botanical gardens sit on. And that started a whole

03:25:11.767 --> 03:25:15.467
venture of finding the money to keep this organization going.

03:25:15.867 --> 03:25:19.700
It's possible for a community to come behind something like

03:25:19.767 --> 03:25:23.567
this and make it happen. I've seen it happen, and I think we can

03:25:23.600 --> 03:25:27.467
do this, and that would be a good purpose for this city. Thank you.

03:25:29.166 --> 03:25:30.233
Thank you very much.

03:25:34.333 --> 03:25:34.667
Hi.

03:25:35.500 --> 03:25:38.266
Mary Linneman, Simmons Road, South Portland.

03:25:38.800 --> 03:25:39.367
I want to

03:25:40.300 --> 03:25:44.066
add my voice to everyone who has commended both the

03:25:44.100 --> 03:25:46.867
Comprehensive Plan Committee and the city councilors.

03:25:47.533 --> 03:25:50.734
Your attention and your stamina has been tremendous.

03:25:52.800 --> 03:25:56.500
This week, tonight, you have the opportunity to

03:25:56.600 --> 03:26:00.300
slow down the implementation of the comprehensive

03:26:00.367 --> 03:26:03.867
plan that the CPC has put

03:26:03.900 --> 03:26:04.433
together.

03:26:05.300 --> 03:26:09.066
Please allow the citizens of our city to be

03:26:09.166 --> 03:26:13.033
truly represented in their desire to save the Eastern

03:26:13.166 --> 03:26:14.100
Waterfront

03:26:14.934 --> 03:26:18.266
from unwanted growth. We're asking you to

03:26:18.333 --> 03:26:22.000
designate this area as CAELUPA 27 or

03:26:22.500 --> 03:26:25.967
22 and 23, whatever it's called, with limited

03:26:26.066 --> 03:26:30.000
development, allowing for commercial fishing and

03:26:30.200 --> 03:26:31.033
aquaculture

03:26:31.900 --> 03:26:35.734
uses, and prohibiting housing, lodging, and

03:26:35.800 --> 03:26:36.600
hotels.

03:26:37.500 --> 03:26:41.000
The shoreline should be designated as a resource

03:26:41.000 --> 03:26:42.200
protection zone.

03:26:43.066 --> 03:26:46.800
There are currently unanswered questions relating to flood rules,

03:26:47.400 --> 03:26:51.000
cost analysis, impact fees, and environmental

03:26:51.033 --> 03:26:53.600
monitoring of tank farm emissions.

03:26:54.166 --> 03:26:57.834
These questions need to be answered before a framework is

03:26:57.867 --> 03:27:01.767
adopted that locks in a plan that does not meet the needs

03:27:01.800 --> 03:27:05.600
of our city in a changing and a more expensive

03:27:05.900 --> 03:27:06.700
environment.

03:27:07.900 --> 03:27:11.834
I would urge you again to take a look at the proposal to use this

03:27:11.867 --> 03:27:14.266
land to develop a heritage site,

03:27:15.066 --> 03:27:18.667
recognizing the achievement of hardworking ship builders

03:27:19.133 --> 03:27:23.000
and the sacrifice of homes that were lost to build

03:27:23.033 --> 03:27:25.066
the shipyard during World War II.

03:27:26.033 --> 03:27:30.000
South Portland is unique that we have this little space in our city.

03:27:31.100 --> 03:27:34.934
Bug Light and the Eastern Waterfront are our most beautiful

03:27:35.066 --> 03:27:38.767
parcels in South Portland. They're our Fort Williams.

03:27:39.233 --> 03:27:43.000
They're our Eastern Prom.This designation would

03:27:43.133 --> 03:27:47.100
also allow for nature-based infrastructure along

03:27:47.133 --> 03:27:51.100
the waterfront, which would be a cost-effective response to

03:27:51.133 --> 03:27:52.300
sea level rise.

03:27:53.634 --> 03:27:57.300
Please consider the requests of those of us in South Portland

03:27:57.767 --> 03:28:01.600
who have asked you to save our shipyard, to delay

03:28:01.634 --> 03:28:05.300
approving a plan that has unanswered questions,

03:28:06.100 --> 03:28:09.300
and who are looking to save open space for the future.

03:28:10.500 --> 03:28:14.200
I add my voice to those urging you to adopt option

03:28:14.333 --> 03:28:18.066
three to promote the policy goals of Save Our

03:28:18.166 --> 03:28:19.800
Shipyard. Thank you.

03:28:27.767 --> 03:28:28.900
Hello, councilors.

03:28:29.567 --> 03:28:30.000
Thank you.

03:28:32.266 --> 03:28:33.100
Robert Foster.

03:28:35.834 --> 03:28:39.567
I'm Mary's husband, and I live on Simmons Road as well.

03:28:41.233 --> 03:28:44.900
I've been a South Portland resident since 1993.

03:28:45.767 --> 03:28:49.300
Some folks who know me may know that before I moved

03:28:49.400 --> 03:28:51.967
to Maine, I lived in Florida,

03:28:53.600 --> 03:28:55.467
which may or may not seem relevant,

03:28:57.200 --> 03:29:00.734
but I think it is. My experience there was that I

03:29:00.767 --> 03:29:04.400
watched, to paraphrase Joni Mitchell's

03:29:04.400 --> 03:29:05.166
famous song,

03:29:06.400 --> 03:29:10.200
paradise get paved and put up a parking

03:29:10.233 --> 03:29:10.500
lot.

03:29:12.000 --> 03:29:13.433
And

03:29:14.300 --> 03:29:18.133
one of the things that I have loved about living

03:29:18.166 --> 03:29:21.967
in South Portland is, one of the many things I've prized about

03:29:22.066 --> 03:29:25.533
living here, is the value this community places,

03:29:25.967 --> 03:29:29.767
in my experience, on preserving its heritage and preserving

03:29:29.800 --> 03:29:32.900
its green spaces, preserving its natural beauty.

03:29:34.133 --> 03:29:37.800
And when I moved to Maine, I pledged that I would do everything I

03:29:37.900 --> 03:29:40.500
could to prevent Maine and the

03:29:41.367 --> 03:29:44.967
little part of it that I lived in from turning into South

03:29:45.000 --> 03:29:45.433
Florida.

03:29:46.567 --> 03:29:47.400
That may seem

03:29:48.567 --> 03:29:52.200
supercilious, but I'm very passionate about that.

03:29:52.266 --> 03:29:55.066
I've seen what happens when

03:29:56.300 --> 03:30:00.200
development is allowed to dictate decisions that get

03:30:00.266 --> 03:30:03.700
made in a public forum at the

03:30:03.734 --> 03:30:06.567
expense of environmental beauty,

03:30:07.567 --> 03:30:08.166
aesthetics,

03:30:09.133 --> 03:30:09.400
and

03:30:10.300 --> 03:30:14.000
just those things that are maybe

03:30:14.066 --> 03:30:17.200
abstractions, but they're incredibly important.

03:30:20.100 --> 03:30:23.233
Some comments have been made about the potential

03:30:23.300 --> 03:30:26.200
value of this parcel as a tax and

03:30:26.266 --> 03:30:30.000
revenue instrument. I certainly understand

03:30:30.066 --> 03:30:33.166
that. On the other hand, I would submit that

03:30:34.667 --> 03:30:38.367
value cannot be calculated simply in terms of dollars and

03:30:38.467 --> 03:30:38.900
cents,

03:30:40.000 --> 03:30:42.166
that these intangibles

03:30:43.367 --> 03:30:47.200
that I just mentioned are of extreme importance to

03:30:47.266 --> 03:30:49.467
the citizens and to the future citizens.

03:30:50.500 --> 03:30:54.266
I would urge the council to consider option

03:30:54.367 --> 03:30:57.467
three, to consider

03:30:57.533 --> 03:31:00.400
revisiting CALUPA 27,

03:31:01.300 --> 03:31:05.266
and to think about the people who will follow

03:31:05.300 --> 03:31:08.066
us living here who will either thank us

03:31:08.934 --> 03:31:12.834
or condemn us for not taking a

03:31:12.900 --> 03:31:16.233
courageous stance and preserving what

03:31:16.300 --> 03:31:20.100
is unique and beautiful about the community we live

03:31:20.166 --> 03:31:21.266
in. Thank you.

03:31:23.600 --> 03:31:24.700
Thank you very much.

03:31:27.467 --> 03:31:30.667
Good evening, PJ Cragun, and thanks all around.

03:31:30.800 --> 03:31:34.433
I reiterate all the thanks that have been said in this room tonight.

03:31:36.100 --> 03:31:37.166
Smith Street, and

03:31:38.100 --> 03:31:39.800
speaking on behalf of Protect South Portland.

03:31:40.467 --> 03:31:43.967
We have worked hard for an entire decade on

03:31:44.000 --> 03:31:47.900
petroleum harm reduction. We've done

03:31:47.934 --> 03:31:49.367
a ton of research.

03:31:51.367 --> 03:31:54.767
In all of our research, we find the same exact information:

03:31:55.266 --> 03:31:58.600
that tank fumes are dangerous to people's health

03:31:59.066 --> 03:32:01.066
and can cause cancer.

03:32:01.967 --> 03:32:05.667
This has just been something that we cannot avoid

03:32:06.133 --> 03:32:06.500
find...

03:32:08.300 --> 03:32:12.166
Sorry. Getting around to this health study idea is that somehow this health

03:32:12.266 --> 03:32:16.066
study is going to determine something different, that is going to say, "Well,

03:32:16.133 --> 03:32:20.000
maybe it is safe." There is nothing in the research

03:32:20.066 --> 03:32:22.867
to indicate that the fumes coming out of tanks are safe.

03:32:23.367 --> 03:32:26.900
It's just simply... So then you get down to exposure, then it's

03:32:27.066 --> 03:32:28.967
how much is safe? Well,

03:32:29.834 --> 03:32:33.800
during the Clear Skies Ordinance, there was a doctor that spoke on behalf of the

03:32:33.867 --> 03:32:36.634
City of South Portland, actually, who said that

03:32:37.600 --> 03:32:40.600
no amount of benzene is safe.

03:32:41.300 --> 03:32:44.400
So I think that's really where we need to begin this

03:32:44.467 --> 03:32:47.867
conversation, is how do we avoid

03:32:47.967 --> 03:32:51.500
exposing people unnecessarily to tank fumes?

03:32:51.567 --> 03:32:55.300
We spent $3 million to stop a tar sands terminal

03:32:55.400 --> 03:32:59.333
because that was going to bring 40 tons of volatile organic compounds

03:32:59.500 --> 03:33:03.000
into our neighborhoods. And now we're having very real discussions

03:33:03.066 --> 03:33:06.867
about putting a neighborhood next to 50

03:33:06.900 --> 03:33:10.834
tons of volatile organic compounds, and we're saying, "Well, if we

03:33:10.867 --> 03:33:14.634
do a health study, then maybe it will say that

03:33:14.834 --> 03:33:16.300
it's safe."

03:33:17.700 --> 03:33:21.367
So how this health study would likely play out is the Maine Center for

03:33:21.467 --> 03:33:25.133
Disease Control would probably be the seminal authority on whether or not it's

03:33:25.200 --> 03:33:28.634
safe. Well, the Maine Center for Disease Control also did not

03:33:28.667 --> 03:33:32.166
mention between 2013 and 2019 during the global

03:33:32.200 --> 03:33:35.400
lawsuit that we were being exposed to what they were

03:33:35.433 --> 03:33:38.567
investigating as dangerous amounts of, or

03:33:39.467 --> 03:33:41.066
overshooting their emissions permits.

03:33:41.767 --> 03:33:44.934
The Maine CDC did not alert the neighborhood after the Sprague

03:33:45.033 --> 03:33:48.867
lawsuit... that they had been polluted over their emissions

03:33:49.000 --> 03:33:52.967
permit. The Maine CDC did not let the people who lived around

03:33:53.066 --> 03:33:56.667
Bug Light and the Gulf Terminal when they exceeded their benzene levels.

03:33:57.033 --> 03:33:59.900
There's still people in the neighborhood who don't know that they were exposed to

03:34:00.066 --> 03:34:01.667
benzene at illegal levels.

03:34:02.634 --> 03:34:04.667
I only found out word of mouth, and-

03:34:04.667 --> 03:34:04.834
Next

03:34:04.867 --> 03:34:08.767
... this is kind of what we do. Okay, I'll stop there, but I think I've

03:34:08.834 --> 03:34:10.734
spoken to enough of you about that.

03:34:10.767 --> 03:34:13.767
And this issue of CALEPA 27, which I think is an excellent

03:34:13.834 --> 03:34:17.300
idea, it does make a difference in your comprehensive plan.

03:34:17.500 --> 03:34:20.700
How you have it now, it will be pretty well up zoned.

03:34:20.834 --> 03:34:24.767
So with the CALEPAs 2021 and '22, those

03:34:24.800 --> 03:34:27.900
are significant up zones which make their job

03:34:27.967 --> 03:34:31.800
significantly more difficult. So I do think that it's a

03:34:31.834 --> 03:34:35.734
big difference, CALEPA 27 and what it lays out and what the plan

03:34:35.800 --> 03:34:36.634
lays out. Thanks so much.

03:34:38.100 --> 03:34:39.233
Thank you very much.

03:34:41.934 --> 03:34:43.800
Hi, good evening. I'm Paul Cunningham.

03:34:44.400 --> 03:34:46.200
I live on Edward Street.

03:34:47.233 --> 03:34:51.166
And first, I do want to commend the Comprehensive

03:34:51.333 --> 03:34:52.667
Plan Committee for working

03:34:53.533 --> 03:34:56.533
so hard for so long. I can't imagine the

03:34:57.900 --> 03:34:59.433
amount of work that's gone into it.

03:35:02.033 --> 03:35:05.834
I attended a handful of the Comprehensive Plan

03:35:05.934 --> 03:35:09.667
Committee meetings, and I too am very concerned

03:35:09.734 --> 03:35:10.233
about

03:35:12.834 --> 03:35:15.900
the tanks, about the

03:35:16.667 --> 03:35:19.533
vulnerability to flooding with

03:35:20.433 --> 03:35:23.834
sea level rise, and I'm also very concerned about the

03:35:24.200 --> 03:35:24.800
traffic

03:35:26.400 --> 03:35:29.634
on Broadway and how it will affect the surrounding

03:35:30.266 --> 03:35:31.300
communities. I don't think

03:35:31.333 --> 03:35:35.266
from a traffic

03:35:35.300 --> 03:35:36.033
standpoint

03:35:37.700 --> 03:35:38.133
that

03:35:39.867 --> 03:35:41.133
our community can handle

03:35:42.333 --> 03:35:45.667
the type of development that may transpire in the

03:35:46.133 --> 03:35:47.033
Eastern Waterfront.

03:35:48.166 --> 03:35:48.367
So

03:35:50.367 --> 03:35:51.800
I wanted to talk a little bit about

03:35:52.800 --> 03:35:55.200
the CPC meetings themselves.

03:35:57.300 --> 03:36:00.433
As I said, I attended a handful of them, not a whole lot of them,

03:36:00.500 --> 03:36:01.734
but

03:36:06.333 --> 03:36:07.300
I came away from them

03:36:08.667 --> 03:36:09.900
sort of perplexed and

03:36:11.834 --> 03:36:14.367
I found that the reaction to public comment during the

03:36:15.266 --> 03:36:17.934
meetings was really

03:36:18.066 --> 03:36:19.266
strange.

03:36:20.900 --> 03:36:22.634
It wasn't adversarial, but

03:36:24.033 --> 03:36:27.100
I was left with a sense of sort of non-acknowledgement.

03:36:28.100 --> 03:36:30.133
Not by everyone on the

03:36:31.567 --> 03:36:33.367
planning committee, but

03:36:34.000 --> 03:36:37.634
there was a general

03:36:37.800 --> 03:36:40.500
reaction, though, as though people

03:36:41.600 --> 03:36:45.300
who had just spoken didn't exist, or what they had just said

03:36:46.233 --> 03:36:50.033
hadn't been spoken. People expressed their concerns

03:36:50.066 --> 03:36:50.500
about

03:36:52.433 --> 03:36:55.734
the tanks, and potential ocean

03:36:56.233 --> 03:36:59.567
rise, and the traffic, and then

03:37:01.066 --> 03:37:02.800
the discussion just went on to

03:37:03.934 --> 03:37:07.367
how are we going to develop the Eastern Waterfront

03:37:08.400 --> 03:37:12.367
as though no one had spoken. And I don't really feel like we were

03:37:12.400 --> 03:37:15.367
listened to. I guess that's my general consensus.

03:37:15.500 --> 03:37:15.900
Second.

03:37:16.000 --> 03:37:16.533
And

03:37:17.400 --> 03:37:21.300
hopefully, city council will listen to us. Thank you.

03:37:22.800 --> 03:37:24.000
Thank you very much.

03:37:26.800 --> 03:37:30.533
Come on. And if there's anyone else who'd like to make public comment.

03:37:32.533 --> 03:37:35.900
My name is John Howard. I live on

03:37:36.634 --> 03:37:39.967
Dimar Drive in South Portland.

03:37:42.000 --> 03:37:45.300
Not too long ago, I was talking to a

03:37:45.400 --> 03:37:48.800
commercial airline pilot. He had just

03:37:48.934 --> 03:37:52.634
landed the plane that I had been a passenger

03:37:52.700 --> 03:37:56.567
on. My seat was towards the rear of the plane,

03:37:57.200 --> 03:38:01.066
so by the time I got up to the exit, the cabin

03:38:01.200 --> 03:38:04.433
crew was getting ready to leave the plane.

03:38:05.166 --> 03:38:08.967
And so this pilot and I walked out together into

03:38:09.000 --> 03:38:13.000
the terminal. I asked him a couple of more or

03:38:13.033 --> 03:38:16.900
less technical questions about landings, which

03:38:16.934 --> 03:38:20.634
he was only too happy to respond to.

03:38:20.967 --> 03:38:24.100
He reminded me of an eight-year-old kid who

03:38:25.266 --> 03:38:28.000
became fascinated with aviation.

03:38:28.700 --> 03:38:32.600
But anyway, when we were done, it was not a long

03:38:32.734 --> 03:38:36.233
conversation. I asked him if he had a

03:38:36.300 --> 03:38:40.066
favorite airport to fly into, and

03:38:40.133 --> 03:38:44.000
he said, "Portland, Maine." He didn't hesitate

03:38:44.133 --> 03:38:47.433
for one moment. He said, "Portland, Maine."

03:38:48.100 --> 03:38:51.433
And as all of us know, the majority of the Portland

03:38:51.634 --> 03:38:54.533
Airport is located in South Portland.

03:38:55.600 --> 03:38:57.200
And he said that

03:38:58.533 --> 03:39:02.000
coming up on a clear day, a water

03:39:02.200 --> 03:39:06.200
approach, flying over the coastline from

03:39:06.300 --> 03:39:10.000
Saco and Scarborough into Cape Elizabeth in

03:39:10.200 --> 03:39:11.133
South Portland,

03:39:12.233 --> 03:39:15.934
every time he did it, he was amazed at the

03:39:16.100 --> 03:39:19.867
green open space, and the

03:39:20.033 --> 03:39:23.934
sense that gave him of peace and

03:39:24.233 --> 03:39:25.433
relaxation.

03:39:26.600 --> 03:39:30.200
I didn't say this to him, but I hoped that he was not too

03:39:30.266 --> 03:39:33.867
relaxed landing a commercial airliner.

03:39:34.634 --> 03:39:34.934
But

03:39:36.500 --> 03:39:38.934
as other speakers have said tonight,

03:39:39.834 --> 03:39:43.033
this is a gem. It is known

03:39:43.400 --> 03:39:47.367
wide and far. AndAs

03:39:47.433 --> 03:39:51.266
someone else said, once it's gone, it's

03:39:51.467 --> 03:39:52.734
never coming back.

03:39:53.533 --> 03:39:57.533
And this pilot's almost his closing words to me,

03:39:57.700 --> 03:40:01.400
parting words were, "I hope it never changes."

03:40:02.233 --> 03:40:05.734
So I hope all of you will keep that in the

03:40:05.767 --> 03:40:06.467
forefront-

03:40:07.066 --> 03:40:07.634
30 seconds

03:40:07.900 --> 03:40:07.900
...

03:40:09.467 --> 03:40:13.033
as you decide what to do here. Save our

03:40:13.133 --> 03:40:14.834
shipyard. Thank you.

03:40:17.533 --> 03:40:18.867
Thank you very much.

03:40:20.166 --> 03:40:23.333
I have to say, public comment doesn't usually make me tear up, but

03:40:26.266 --> 03:40:30.033
I fly fairly frequently, and that's how I feel coming

03:40:30.133 --> 03:40:30.667
home.

03:40:34.066 --> 03:40:37.400
All right. Do we have any other comment?

03:40:38.433 --> 03:40:38.433
I already speak.

03:40:38.433 --> 03:40:40.166
Pardon me, did you already speak?

03:40:41.467 --> 03:40:41.500
I did.

03:40:41.533 --> 03:40:45.367
Yes. Well, we appreciated your remarks, but you cannot speak

03:40:45.433 --> 03:40:48.200
again on the same topic. Thank you.

03:40:48.333 --> 03:40:49.667
Is there anyone online?

03:40:50.500 --> 03:40:53.834
Last chance. Anyone online to raise your hand for public comment?

03:40:56.467 --> 03:40:58.166
Do not see any hands raised.

03:40:58.233 --> 03:41:02.066
Okay. All right. Well then, on this item, the public

03:41:02.133 --> 03:41:06.133
hearing for our recommended comprehensive plan is

03:41:06.166 --> 03:41:07.567
closed.

03:41:11.133 --> 03:41:15.100
I know that we have some decisions to make and some discussion to have, but

03:41:15.133 --> 03:41:17.266
would anyone on council mind if we did-

03:41:17.467 --> 03:41:17.500
There's a few questions.

03:41:17.567 --> 03:41:21.400
Oh, well. How about this? Does

03:41:21.433 --> 03:41:24.700
anyone besides the mayor need a five-minute break?

03:41:27.200 --> 03:41:30.934
How about Scott answers the questions, and then I would really love to have a

03:41:30.967 --> 03:41:32.266
five-minute break. Five minutes.

03:41:33.100 --> 03:41:34.867
Even better, Milan answers the questions.

03:41:34.900 --> 03:41:34.900
Okay.

03:41:34.934 --> 03:41:35.567
He's been taking-

03:41:36.066 --> 03:41:36.100
Thanks

03:41:36.100 --> 03:41:38.467
... copious notes again. I think there are only a handful.

03:41:41.333 --> 03:41:41.834
I did not

03:41:42.767 --> 03:41:44.834
capture too many questions.

03:41:45.734 --> 03:41:49.166
There were two for the council. Will you insist on

03:41:49.834 --> 03:41:53.533
pushing for other ways to get money instead of property taxes?

03:41:53.567 --> 03:41:57.066
And secondly, from the same speaker, will council assure the

03:41:57.166 --> 03:41:59.767
corridors maintain trees?

03:42:01.300 --> 03:42:04.800
At least on the trees piece, that's a pretty straightforward

03:42:05.300 --> 03:42:08.700
zoning standard, and we can certainly include that at the council's discretion if

03:42:08.734 --> 03:42:11.367
we do look at zoning along the corridors.

03:42:12.166 --> 03:42:15.934
The next question that is more directed to planning staff was a

03:42:15.967 --> 03:42:19.533
question about affordable housing, how the plan provides

03:42:20.834 --> 03:42:23.767
for affordable housing citywide. Is there an approach?

03:42:23.800 --> 03:42:25.967
What's the calculation method? What's the definition?

03:42:27.066 --> 03:42:30.634
So the plan includes a... It's broken down into two

03:42:30.767 --> 03:42:32.900
parts. Part one is the one that has all the policies.

03:42:32.934 --> 03:42:36.767
Part two is the inventories and appendices, and that goes

03:42:36.834 --> 03:42:40.533
into length on matters of housing policy and our

03:42:41.133 --> 03:42:45.133
conditions in housing, and defers to the housing assessment

03:42:45.166 --> 03:42:47.800
that we did to define some of those conditions.

03:42:48.200 --> 03:42:50.734
And both of those sections really give

03:42:51.834 --> 03:42:54.533
some background on how we would define affordability.

03:42:55.533 --> 03:42:59.200
In this community, we would define affordability as below

03:42:59.934 --> 03:43:03.000
housing that serves households earning less

03:43:03.066 --> 03:43:06.867
than 80% of the area median income.

03:43:07.734 --> 03:43:11.433
For for-sale units, it can go up to 120% area median

03:43:11.467 --> 03:43:14.967
income. Those definitions are pretty

03:43:15.133 --> 03:43:18.634
standard across how the state looks at that, how the

03:43:19.467 --> 03:43:22.467
federal Department of Housing and Urban Development looks at

03:43:22.767 --> 03:43:26.567
defining affordable housing. We, in the plan, also

03:43:26.634 --> 03:43:30.000
recognize and use this term of workforce housing, and that reaches

03:43:30.033 --> 03:43:33.867
households that are between that 80% of area

03:43:33.934 --> 03:43:37.667
median and 120% of area median. And that's a

03:43:38.367 --> 03:43:41.967
tough group because there are no federal programs or state

03:43:42.000 --> 03:43:45.934
programs to help support housing that serves them, and they're also a

03:43:45.967 --> 03:43:49.867
group that isn't necessarily able to access all of the market

03:43:49.900 --> 03:43:53.033
rate housing, and so they're squeezed from both ends.

03:43:53.066 --> 03:43:57.000
They're not poor enough, but they're also not rich enough, and so

03:43:57.033 --> 03:44:00.300
really hard to find housing. We speak to that specifically in the plan.

03:44:00.934 --> 03:44:02.266
We have citywide policy.

03:44:03.166 --> 03:44:06.066
Specifically, I'd call your attention to Goal 3A,

03:44:07.033 --> 03:44:10.667
and then also 7H that speak to affordable housing.

03:44:10.734 --> 03:44:13.967
There's a variety of measures. We talk about anti-gentrification,

03:44:14.033 --> 03:44:17.834
anti-displacement, looking at inclusionary housing policies, allowing for

03:44:17.867 --> 03:44:21.433
infill housing, allowing for small-scale housing that

03:44:21.467 --> 03:44:25.367
can be squeezed into existing structures that typically come

03:44:25.467 --> 03:44:29.367
in as more affordable support for the ADU program, which

03:44:29.400 --> 03:44:31.767
has been statistically shown to be affordable housing.

03:44:33.400 --> 03:44:37.200
And then as well as other forms of deed restricted housing, so

03:44:37.333 --> 03:44:40.467
using affordable housing TIFs. All of that is specified in the plan.

03:44:40.533 --> 03:44:44.100
So we kind of approach it from any and all possible ways that we can

03:44:44.133 --> 03:44:47.934
participate in promoting, and we have a generalized policy of just do

03:44:47.934 --> 03:44:51.200
anything we can to support affordable housing development, and

03:44:51.266 --> 03:44:53.467
specifically restricted affordable housing.

03:44:54.967 --> 03:44:58.567
So happy to dive further if needed on that.And

03:44:58.967 --> 03:45:00.734
I think that's all the questions I had.

03:45:01.133 --> 03:45:01.166
Yeah.

03:45:02.266 --> 03:45:06.033
One thing that I would add on it too is, I'm not

03:45:06.100 --> 03:45:09.567
sure what the CDC did or didn't know back in

03:45:09.734 --> 03:45:13.500
2019 about those lawsuits. I'm not entirely sure they knew

03:45:13.533 --> 03:45:16.934
anything. So it's possible they did.

03:45:16.967 --> 03:45:20.867
It's possible they didn't. I would presume, and we can try

03:45:20.900 --> 03:45:24.500
to find this out by asking them, if they did know and felt there was a health risk

03:45:24.533 --> 03:45:28.166
or concern, that they would've alerted South Portland and the residents.

03:45:28.266 --> 03:45:32.166
But that's not what happened, and we found out like many people through

03:45:32.233 --> 03:45:35.667
a newspaper article unfortunately, at that

03:45:35.734 --> 03:45:39.700
time. And I also don't think it's necessarily a new study

03:45:39.734 --> 03:45:43.634
or report that would necessarily be used to help us understand the

03:45:43.634 --> 03:45:47.567
risks. It would be some part of the ongoing monitoring efforts that

03:45:47.634 --> 03:45:51.533
frankly are set to wrap up in one phase this

03:45:51.567 --> 03:45:55.166
June when DEP and CDC come in to give us a combined

03:45:55.233 --> 03:45:59.033
report. Those reports so far have said the risk right

03:45:59.133 --> 03:46:01.367
now is, as has been stated before,

03:46:02.367 --> 03:46:05.967
out of the entire population of South Portland over a 70-year period,

03:46:06.367 --> 03:46:08.867
if the exposure at that kind of worst area was

03:46:08.900 --> 03:46:12.433
constant, you would expect one

03:46:12.467 --> 03:46:14.867
additional cancer in the entire population.

03:46:15.300 --> 03:46:19.000
And so it's possible with new and updated information that that could

03:46:19.066 --> 03:46:22.967
change. That doesn't talk about short-term acute impacts, but that's what

03:46:23.000 --> 03:46:24.967
the information has told us so far.

03:46:25.433 --> 03:46:29.333
But again, we're going to get more information from CDC and DEP this

03:46:29.400 --> 03:46:33.000
June. And then there's the whole fence line monitoring, which are

03:46:33.033 --> 03:46:33.467
showing,

03:46:34.800 --> 03:46:38.567
as probably anticipated, elevated levels at the fence line, and so we need to

03:46:38.600 --> 03:46:41.767
figure out how is that dispersing into some of the neighborhoods.

03:46:42.233 --> 03:46:45.600
Some of the neighborhoods are almost essentially living on the fence line, and so

03:46:46.266 --> 03:46:49.967
how quickly does that disperse, or is it all just going into the neighborhood?

03:46:50.066 --> 03:46:53.934
So I think with what we've got going on now, we're going to have pretty good

03:46:54.000 --> 03:46:54.834
understanding.

03:46:55.634 --> 03:46:58.767
There can be more that's done, but I think we've already got a pretty good

03:46:58.834 --> 03:47:02.700
understanding of what's out there, and then that can be used as the

03:47:02.767 --> 03:47:03.266
basis

03:47:04.634 --> 03:47:08.400
when the council considers whether or not those areas are safe for residential

03:47:08.433 --> 03:47:08.967
development.

03:47:10.634 --> 03:47:12.967
Councilor West, were you asking a question, or...?

03:47:14.033 --> 03:47:17.900
I just thought it would be useful for staff to clarify one thing that we heard

03:47:17.967 --> 03:47:21.734
just in almost the last testimony, the assertion that the housing

03:47:21.767 --> 03:47:24.367
would flood every two weeks with the level of

03:47:25.934 --> 03:47:28.166
sea level rise considered in the plan.

03:47:32.934 --> 03:47:34.533
And I have maps if we need to look.

03:47:35.867 --> 03:47:39.767
We have done sea level rise projections at 3.9 and 8.8

03:47:39.834 --> 03:47:43.367
plus, and with and without storm conditions.

03:47:45.033 --> 03:47:48.834
I haven't seen data that specifically says every two

03:47:48.867 --> 03:47:52.700
weeks there will be flooding. The mapping we have shows

03:47:52.834 --> 03:47:56.467
average daily high tide condition, as well as a high tide,

03:47:57.000 --> 03:48:00.967
like a king tide annual condition, and then that 1% storm.

03:48:01.834 --> 03:48:04.734
We do also have depth information associated with that.

03:48:05.934 --> 03:48:09.300
But if you fast-forward in time long enough,

03:48:10.266 --> 03:48:13.967
with what we know about sea level rise, there will be a point where water

03:48:14.033 --> 03:48:18.033
becomes very frequent. However, the current projections

03:48:18.066 --> 03:48:22.033
that we're looking at are at the 2050 mark and then

03:48:22.433 --> 03:48:26.133
over to the 2120 mark. And I'm not

03:48:26.333 --> 03:48:30.300
sure that widespread throughout the shipyard we have any areas that are on a

03:48:30.700 --> 03:48:34.000
biweekly basis experiencing water. I'd have to take a closer look.

03:48:34.567 --> 03:48:34.834
Thank you.

03:48:36.266 --> 03:48:36.734
Thanks.

03:48:38.200 --> 03:48:40.333
Could we just please have a five-minute break?

03:48:40.467 --> 03:48:44.266
It's 10:25, and just come right back here at

03:48:44.333 --> 03:48:46.400
10:30 and decide what we're going to do next.

03:48:49.767 --> 03:48:53.500
Folks. All

03:48:53.634 --> 03:48:54.634
folks, if we could

03:48:56.333 --> 03:48:58.266
quiet down a little. Thank you.

03:49:00.634 --> 03:49:04.433
And our thanks to Officer Gerrish who figured out how to turn the lights

03:49:04.467 --> 03:49:05.533
on in the hallway.

03:49:07.734 --> 03:49:08.166
Yay.

03:49:09.367 --> 03:49:13.300
Okay. So it is 10:31, for anyone

03:49:13.367 --> 03:49:17.066
who wants to know. And we were going to begin

03:49:17.166 --> 03:49:21.100
council discussion by giving an opportunity

03:49:21.266 --> 03:49:24.934
to the two council members who served on the comprehensive planning

03:49:25.033 --> 03:49:28.900
committee a chance to make the

03:49:28.934 --> 03:49:32.200
first comments, and then we're , excuse me, I think we're going to

03:49:33.166 --> 03:49:35.233
talk some more and figure out what we're going to do here.

03:49:36.166 --> 03:49:37.967
Is there a motion for passage that we're going to have?

03:49:37.967 --> 03:49:39.266
Or are you part of the presentation?

03:49:40.300 --> 03:49:40.834
Pardon me?

03:49:41.033 --> 03:49:44.533
Are you part of the presentation, or is there a motion for

03:49:44.634 --> 03:49:48.233
passage that needs to be on the floor?

03:49:48.300 --> 03:49:51.934
I don't know. This is a little odd because we don't usually say to-

03:49:51.967 --> 03:49:53.800
Oh, it should've gone before public comment, but it's okay.

03:49:54.533 --> 03:49:58.300
Okay. We don't usually say that. And now the committee

03:49:58.367 --> 03:50:01.867
member councils will simply have some things to say. So you're right.

03:50:01.900 --> 03:50:02.867
It's just a process question.

03:50:02.967 --> 03:50:05.967
Generally, when we're speaking, we have a motion...

03:50:06.166 --> 03:50:10.000
I mean, we could probably do it either way if it really bothers you.

03:50:10.066 --> 03:50:12.600
The original plan was to give us a chance.

03:50:12.634 --> 03:50:12.967
Go ahead.

03:50:13.500 --> 03:50:15.000
Or just make the motion so it's done.

03:50:16.066 --> 03:50:16.233
Or you can make the motion.

03:50:16.266 --> 03:50:17.734
I'm not motioning anything.

03:50:17.967 --> 03:50:17.967
Okay.

03:50:18.066 --> 03:50:20.266
I'm not motioning anything at this moment.

03:50:20.300 --> 03:50:20.834
Holy cow.

03:50:21.266 --> 03:50:22.300
Councilor West?

03:50:23.567 --> 03:50:27.533
I make a motion to adopt as presented with a clear revision

03:50:27.600 --> 03:50:28.533
I'm going to suggest.

03:50:30.467 --> 03:50:31.166
And I'll second

03:50:33.467 --> 03:50:33.467
.

03:50:33.533 --> 03:50:33.900
Very-

03:50:33.934 --> 03:50:35.166
For purpose of discussion

03:50:35.166 --> 03:50:35.767
... your motion.

03:50:36.867 --> 03:50:38.634
All right. Well, now you can make your comments.

03:50:39.100 --> 03:50:40.734
No, because you're the mayor, you can go first.

03:50:41.300 --> 03:50:41.467
Oh, okay.

03:50:41.467 --> 03:50:42.433
Didn't you want to go first?

03:50:43.033 --> 03:50:45.333
No, I was going to be polite and let you go first.

03:50:45.367 --> 03:50:45.634
Okay.

03:50:45.834 --> 03:50:49.266
But whatever works. I can go first. I'm ready.

03:50:51.200 --> 03:50:52.600
Do you want me to go first now, Marie?

03:50:52.767 --> 03:50:53.133
Sure. Go first.

03:50:53.300 --> 03:50:57.233
Okay. So basically, all I really wanted to

03:50:57.266 --> 03:50:58.233
do was, the

03:50:59.066 --> 03:51:02.600
most important thing to me was to sayThat I

03:51:02.634 --> 03:51:05.900
really acknowledge and feel grateful to

03:51:06.000 --> 03:51:09.800
everyone who has come to committee meetings, who has come to

03:51:09.867 --> 03:51:13.133
this meeting, who has come to other council meetings, who has

03:51:13.734 --> 03:51:16.900
brought comment and has brought research, and

03:51:18.100 --> 03:51:21.800
others who have written to the council and written to the committee

03:51:21.900 --> 03:51:22.634
members. I

03:51:23.533 --> 03:51:25.834
want you to know that we read our email, that

03:51:26.834 --> 03:51:30.700
I heard you, I hear you now, I

03:51:30.800 --> 03:51:34.767
acknowledge all of that, and I'm grateful for it, and I'm grateful to live in a

03:51:34.800 --> 03:51:37.033
community where this happens.

03:51:39.200 --> 03:51:41.900
So that was my most important thing.

03:51:43.200 --> 03:51:47.166
Yes, I served on the committee, and in doing so, I didn't just sort of

03:51:47.233 --> 03:51:50.433
read or hear from others. I saw the thing unfold in real

03:51:50.500 --> 03:51:53.567
time, and I heard concerns and disagreements,

03:51:54.066 --> 03:51:57.800
deeply held perspectives from residents, from business owners,

03:51:57.934 --> 03:52:00.300
others who care about the community.

03:52:00.967 --> 03:52:04.200
And now here we are at a different stage.

03:52:04.333 --> 03:52:08.233
It's the council's responsibility to review the committee's

03:52:08.333 --> 03:52:12.333
plan and decide whether it's ready to be adopted or whether

03:52:12.400 --> 03:52:15.533
changes are needed before it is.

03:52:16.200 --> 03:52:16.533
And

03:52:17.800 --> 03:52:21.767
based on what I observed during my time on the committee, what I have

03:52:22.133 --> 03:52:26.066
heard here tonight, I do believe that there's still work

03:52:26.100 --> 03:52:29.667
for the council to do, and I don't support

03:52:29.800 --> 03:52:33.734
immediately moving forward with the plan as is.

03:52:34.166 --> 03:52:38.133
I think we've heard that there are consequential elements.

03:52:38.634 --> 03:52:42.033
Mostly those relate to the Eastern Waterfront.

03:52:43.033 --> 03:52:43.433
And

03:52:44.734 --> 03:52:48.467
I feel that in some cases at the committee

03:52:48.533 --> 03:52:52.166
level, decisions, as our chair alluded to,

03:52:52.300 --> 03:52:55.634
decisions were made by relatively narrow votes.

03:52:56.266 --> 03:52:56.500
And

03:52:57.300 --> 03:53:00.400
the conditions were such that although we have a

03:53:00.433 --> 03:53:02.000
16-seat committee,

03:53:02.900 --> 03:53:06.734
two of us didn't vote, and not all seats

03:53:06.834 --> 03:53:09.033
were actually filled at all times.

03:53:09.100 --> 03:53:13.033
So although quorum numbers were met, it still

03:53:13.233 --> 03:53:16.734
meant that on some significant decisions, a fairly few

03:53:16.800 --> 03:53:20.700
members voted. And it just makes

03:53:20.734 --> 03:53:24.700
me a bit uncomfortable because I think what we ended up with is a

03:53:24.767 --> 03:53:28.600
plan that reflects a divided set of views rather

03:53:28.667 --> 03:53:31.934
than something that was widely supported.

03:53:33.600 --> 03:53:37.500
I'll mention my experience of the original K Lupa

03:53:37.667 --> 03:53:38.500
27.

03:53:39.333 --> 03:53:43.133
It did come to us after staff had taken a great deal of

03:53:43.233 --> 03:53:47.200
time and trouble and brain power to meet with

03:53:47.300 --> 03:53:51.133
every member of the committee who wished to and had come up

03:53:51.200 --> 03:53:53.834
with this idea of a K Lupa

03:53:54.100 --> 03:53:58.000
27 that, as I recall, many of

03:53:58.033 --> 03:54:01.967
the committee members liked, and also it met

03:54:02.033 --> 03:54:05.800
with some good positive comments by community

03:54:05.834 --> 03:54:09.667
members who had previously not felt very good about

03:54:09.700 --> 03:54:12.367
where they thought the direction was going for the Eastern

03:54:13.767 --> 03:54:17.500
Shipyard area. So it was surprising to me.

03:54:17.600 --> 03:54:21.567
Actually, I got on a plane thinking that, "Oh, this K Lupa 27,

03:54:21.634 --> 03:54:25.500
it's saving us. Thank you, staff." And then I got off the plane and

03:54:25.533 --> 03:54:29.533
found out that it hadn't passed. And again, it was

03:54:29.600 --> 03:54:33.433
a fairly small number that legally

03:54:33.500 --> 03:54:36.500
could vote and be a majority, but it was a small number of

03:54:37.033 --> 03:54:40.834
16 seats. So that does bother me. Now we're

03:54:40.900 --> 03:54:44.033
here at our council level, and I do think that our

03:54:44.100 --> 03:54:47.934
council has shown in the past that we can take a closer

03:54:48.000 --> 03:54:51.567
look at things, and that we can be influenced by what

03:54:52.266 --> 03:54:55.900
members of the public, our community, our neighbors have to say.

03:54:56.700 --> 03:55:00.700
I thought of, well, there was gas-powered leaf blowers, and some of us

03:55:00.767 --> 03:55:04.166
thought that was a great idea to have a moratorium on them.

03:55:04.200 --> 03:55:07.867
And then we heard a lot from the community, and it was not a well-supported

03:55:07.967 --> 03:55:11.567
idea, and we pulled it back. And then I see what the school

03:55:11.667 --> 03:55:15.367
board's going through now. They're listening to parents who are

03:55:15.500 --> 03:55:18.266
asking them to slow down on some ideas.

03:55:18.367 --> 03:55:21.600
So I am not saying we need to start over.

03:55:21.700 --> 03:55:25.233
I'm not dismissing at all good work that's done.

03:55:25.233 --> 03:55:29.100
But I think there are some places where we could still discuss and

03:55:29.133 --> 03:55:32.867
make adjustments, and I do think that that is our

03:55:32.934 --> 03:55:34.900
council role for something of this

03:55:34.967 --> 03:55:37.734
magnitude.

03:55:40.567 --> 03:55:44.400
My view is that we should adopt tonight with a revision that I'm going to

03:55:44.667 --> 03:55:46.634
suggest. It was suggested by David Hebert.

03:55:47.634 --> 03:55:51.600
I want to start off by thanking everyone who's here tonight

03:55:51.634 --> 03:55:55.133
and thanking the members of the public who have participated throughout the long

03:55:55.200 --> 03:55:59.166
process. I've been serving on the committee since I was elected about three

03:55:59.200 --> 03:56:03.066
and a half years ago, and it makes me

03:56:03.133 --> 03:56:06.800
sad when I hear people stand up and say that they weren't listened to because we

03:56:06.934 --> 03:56:10.800
did. We did listen to you, and we incorporated

03:56:10.867 --> 03:56:13.767
many of your suggestions. We revised and

03:56:13.834 --> 03:56:17.567
considered. And the committee was made up of a

03:56:17.634 --> 03:56:20.600
cross-section of our community, as it should be.

03:56:21.500 --> 03:56:25.233
But to hear people stand up tonight and say that we took

03:56:25.266 --> 03:56:29.100
into account the property tax effect of

03:56:29.166 --> 03:56:31.734
development in the Eastern Waterfront is not accurate.

03:56:32.400 --> 03:56:36.233
It is true to say that there was a group, there were several members of the

03:56:36.300 --> 03:56:40.200
committee, who believed that we should have intense development in the Eastern

03:56:40.300 --> 03:56:43.634
Waterfront as a way of generating property taxes.

03:56:44.400 --> 03:56:48.233
But their view was the minority view. It did not prevail.

03:56:49.333 --> 03:56:53.300
The prevailing view for the Eastern Waterfront, and most of my comments are

03:56:53.300 --> 03:56:56.767
going to be about the Eastern Waterfront, because as you can tell, that's what

03:56:56.834 --> 03:57:00.634
people are talking about. The prevailing view is that

03:57:00.700 --> 03:57:03.734
some sort of balanced development is

03:57:03.767 --> 03:57:07.533
appropriate.We've heard talk tonight about the

03:57:08.700 --> 03:57:12.266
petition but the petition that was submitted

03:57:12.967 --> 03:57:16.800
said, "As a resident of South Portland, I added my name

03:57:16.867 --> 03:57:20.734
to this petition to express my opposition to any zoning

03:57:20.867 --> 03:57:24.767
change in the city's shipyard district that would allow

03:57:24.867 --> 03:57:28.600
intensive high-rise residential development

03:57:28.934 --> 03:57:31.367
like that proposed for Yard South."

03:57:32.667 --> 03:57:35.433
It did not say, "I oppose residential

03:57:35.533 --> 03:57:39.467
development." And I've spoken to many, many of you in the

03:57:39.533 --> 03:57:43.066
community who have told me that you recognize the need for

03:57:43.166 --> 03:57:47.033
residential development. Balanced, low-density

03:57:47.266 --> 03:57:50.934
residential development in the eastern waterfront could

03:57:51.066 --> 03:57:52.200
be appropriate.

03:57:54.100 --> 03:57:57.834
I want to say that I do think also as we go forward,

03:57:58.433 --> 03:58:01.300
regardless of what happens tonight, we need to have another

03:58:01.367 --> 03:58:05.066
workshop about implementation because three points that are very

03:58:05.100 --> 03:58:09.033
important to me are, I think the council has

03:58:09.066 --> 03:58:12.533
to have an annual review of our comprehensive plan.

03:58:12.634 --> 03:58:15.967
We need to have a public proceeding

03:58:16.867 --> 03:58:20.700
in front of all of you where we, who are elected to represent you,

03:58:21.200 --> 03:58:25.066
look at what the plan has done, what we've accomplished in the last year,

03:58:25.667 --> 03:58:29.166
and how we can proceed and set priorities for its

03:58:29.233 --> 03:58:30.066
implementation.

03:58:31.467 --> 03:58:35.200
I also think the city needs to take the initiative to engage with

03:58:35.200 --> 03:58:38.600
Portland Pipeline and Sunoco to determine their business

03:58:38.734 --> 03:58:42.634
plans and do what we can to end hydrocarbon storage

03:58:42.734 --> 03:58:46.567
on our eastern waterfront. We've heard some testimony tonight that

03:58:46.600 --> 03:58:50.400
nothing should happen because of the toxics, but I think the

03:58:50.467 --> 03:58:54.433
better approach is that the toxics should come to an end, and

03:58:54.467 --> 03:58:55.667
then something can happen.

03:58:57.834 --> 03:58:59.233
With regard to the park,

03:59:00.433 --> 03:59:01.333
I support a park.

03:59:02.266 --> 03:59:06.233
I actually think that a park would be better if you can focus

03:59:06.266 --> 03:59:10.266
on K Lupa 21 instead of K Lupa 22.

03:59:10.967 --> 03:59:14.767
K Lupa 21 extends along the Fore River.

03:59:14.834 --> 03:59:18.266
It is the waterfront area. If you look at the parcels...

03:59:18.734 --> 03:59:20.333
Well, I could go on for quite a long time.

03:59:20.367 --> 03:59:24.367
As you know, I get into the details, and I know it's really late,

03:59:24.433 --> 03:59:28.433
so I don't want to lose you. Let me just say that I think that I support

03:59:28.433 --> 03:59:28.934
a park,

03:59:29.800 --> 03:59:31.066
and I would suggest that

03:59:31.967 --> 03:59:35.867
if the group of people who want to have the park wanted to work

03:59:35.867 --> 03:59:39.533
with the city, that the city could form some kind of a committee,

03:59:39.900 --> 03:59:43.333
and work with the citizens to see if we can have a historic park.

03:59:43.967 --> 03:59:46.834
I've talked to a number of you about this.

03:59:46.834 --> 03:59:50.767
I also think it's important to ask our historic society to be a

03:59:50.834 --> 03:59:54.634
part of that. And I understand there's dynamics, but we know they have a lot of

03:59:54.667 --> 03:59:58.533
expertise and a lot of knowledge, and if we're going to have a historic park, our

03:59:58.600 --> 04:00:01.800
historic society should be part of what goes on.

04:00:02.367 --> 04:00:06.133
I thought the speaker who referenced the Maine Botanical Garden as a

04:00:06.133 --> 04:00:10.066
reference, that was a pretty useful idea to think of that creative and

04:00:10.133 --> 04:00:13.934
what that has done for that area. And I think a historical park could do the

04:00:13.967 --> 04:00:15.166
same for South Portland.

04:00:17.233 --> 04:00:20.834
With regard to the discussion on growth, the state statute

04:00:20.934 --> 04:00:23.834
says you have to decide if it's rural or growth.

04:00:23.900 --> 04:00:24.567
So we have

04:00:27.734 --> 04:00:31.433
kind of tweaked it a little bit to say limited growth and unique

04:00:31.467 --> 04:00:35.367
growth. But the statute says when you identify your land, you've got

04:00:35.367 --> 04:00:38.066
to say whether it's growth or not growth.

04:00:38.133 --> 04:00:41.934
So that's why our staff has done such a good job.

04:00:41.967 --> 04:00:45.667
And I do want to say, I hope when you listened to our staff presentation tonight,

04:00:45.767 --> 04:00:49.467
you realized how absolutely outstanding the quality of our

04:00:49.600 --> 04:00:53.333
staff has been. That was a clear and detailed,

04:00:53.867 --> 04:00:54.367
and I hope

04:00:55.667 --> 04:00:59.433
you could just have a lot of your questions answered by

04:00:59.500 --> 04:01:02.133
what a good presentation that was.

04:01:02.667 --> 04:01:06.433
In particular, how long it's going to take between now,

04:01:06.834 --> 04:01:10.767
which is trying to get our basic framework in place so that we

04:01:10.800 --> 04:01:12.967
can do the things that we need to do.

04:01:13.433 --> 04:01:17.367
We have a coastal resilience overlay zone that's close to being

04:01:17.400 --> 04:01:21.066
ready to go. We need our comp plan in place

04:01:21.266 --> 04:01:25.000
to bring that forward. We probably need to have some development

04:01:25.066 --> 04:01:29.000
impact fees. We need our comp plan in place so that we

04:01:29.066 --> 04:01:31.634
can start working on development impact fees.

04:01:32.634 --> 04:01:32.834
I

04:01:35.033 --> 04:01:38.800
guess I won't go into all the

04:01:38.934 --> 04:01:42.567
other questions, but I do want to say that I thought David...

04:01:42.634 --> 04:01:44.533
Oh, I've got to talk a little bit about the toxics.

04:01:45.500 --> 04:01:49.433
Yeah, we should be concerned because benzene is dangerous, and the latest

04:01:49.533 --> 04:01:53.467
fence line monitoring from the Sunoco site shows

04:01:53.500 --> 04:01:57.266
that 14 of those measurements exceeded our

04:01:57.367 --> 04:01:58.433
state level.

04:01:59.433 --> 04:02:02.367
One of them was seven times our state level.

04:02:02.433 --> 04:02:05.967
That is not acceptable, it's not safe, and I don't

04:02:05.967 --> 04:02:07.934
support people living close to it.

04:02:08.367 --> 04:02:11.567
But again, the answer is, let's get rid of the tanks.

04:02:12.767 --> 04:02:16.233
And so in our plan, we have a lot of

04:02:16.266 --> 04:02:20.133
protection. We have these necessities to make findings

04:02:20.166 --> 04:02:23.133
before anything could go forward with residential in that area.

04:02:23.667 --> 04:02:26.800
But when Dave Hubert stood up tonight, he suggested that we

04:02:26.867 --> 04:02:30.834
insert one sentence, and it would go in two places after the

04:02:30.867 --> 04:02:33.967
word occupancy to say the evaluation must

04:02:34.033 --> 04:02:36.667
incorporate the maximum potential

04:02:37.000 --> 04:02:40.266
to emit. And I would

04:02:40.333 --> 04:02:43.734
submit that we would add that to the plan

04:02:44.567 --> 04:02:47.233
and that that would be appropriate with one of

04:02:47.266 --> 04:02:51.066
the actions that we're entitled to

04:02:51.133 --> 04:02:55.100
take tonight, which would be the clear revision that I would suggest as

04:02:55.133 --> 04:02:58.967
part of our adoption. So that's where I'm coming from this evening,

04:02:59.033 --> 04:03:02.266
and I appreciate you giving me the time to speak to these points.

04:03:03.233 --> 04:03:05.433
Thank you very much, Councilor West.

04:03:05.600 --> 04:03:05.867
Just-

04:03:06.734 --> 04:03:10.367
A point of our process, and we want to move to the other

04:03:10.400 --> 04:03:11.500
councilors' comments.

04:03:12.367 --> 04:03:15.667
If the other councilors also have specific

04:03:15.734 --> 04:03:19.166
changes to suggest, this councilor might have a couple,

04:03:20.900 --> 04:03:22.900
how are we going to basically handle that one?

04:03:23.600 --> 04:03:27.467
Because often we are making amendments to one

04:03:27.634 --> 04:03:30.400
thing that is a short thing. These are

04:03:32.367 --> 04:03:33.734
not short things.

04:03:33.967 --> 04:03:35.166
Can I just offer a suggestion?

04:03:35.200 --> 04:03:35.700
Yes, please.

04:03:35.800 --> 04:03:39.300
I think that each councilor should offer their amendment via a motion, each

04:03:39.400 --> 04:03:42.500
separate amendment, and then have the whole council vote on each separate

04:03:42.567 --> 04:03:43.033
amendment.

04:03:43.300 --> 04:03:43.667
All right.

04:03:43.700 --> 04:03:46.467
Unless your amendments depend on each other, and then you can put them together.

04:03:46.567 --> 04:03:47.967
But if they're independent-

04:03:48.967 --> 04:03:49.166
All right

04:03:49.166 --> 04:03:51.100
... it's going to be a process, but it's the way we should do it.

04:03:51.300 --> 04:03:54.867
Councilor West, do you feel that you've offered that to your own

04:03:54.934 --> 04:03:58.100
satisfaction in amendment language?

04:03:58.667 --> 04:04:02.033
No. I'd make a motion,

04:04:02.467 --> 04:04:03.400
maybe someone will second,

04:04:04.333 --> 04:04:05.033
that in

04:04:06.667 --> 04:04:08.467
5C14,

04:04:09.467 --> 04:04:12.900
after the word "occupancy," we add, "The

04:04:12.967 --> 04:04:15.300
evaluation must incorporate

04:04:16.200 --> 04:04:17.600
maximum potential

04:04:19.200 --> 04:04:19.834
emissions."

04:04:21.000 --> 04:04:21.667
I second that.

04:04:22.634 --> 04:04:26.567
And then also in CALUPA 22-

04:04:28.700 --> 04:04:28.900
B.

04:04:30.266 --> 04:04:31.467
B. Is it B?

04:04:31.567 --> 04:04:32.033
Yeah. It's-

04:04:32.133 --> 04:04:35.800
Yeah, B, after the word "occupancy," insert the same

04:04:37.066 --> 04:04:37.533
phrase

04:04:38.967 --> 04:04:39.700
or sentence.

04:04:40.100 --> 04:04:41.000
I second that as well.

04:04:50.700 --> 04:04:51.066
Okay.

04:04:52.100 --> 04:04:55.900
So do you want to have a vote on... Anyone have comments

04:04:55.967 --> 04:04:56.867
about the amendment?

04:04:57.667 --> 04:05:01.533
I'll just say that I support it. I had 5C14

04:05:01.600 --> 04:05:04.967
as an area that I wanted to discuss as well, and I think that sentence that he

04:05:05.000 --> 04:05:06.133
offered really gets at-

04:05:06.467 --> 04:05:06.600
Yeah

04:05:06.900 --> 04:05:07.700
